(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1c.c995bd3acc.js"]={"16":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of: Hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis, Hypothyroidism, Hashimoto Thyroiditis, Lymphocytic (Subacute) Thyroiditis, Granulomatous (De Quervain) Thyroiditis, Graves Disease (Diffuse toxic hyperplasia).","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the immunologic mechanism responsible for the progressive thyroid failure seen in Hashimoto thyroiditis?","choices":{"A":"Loss of self-tolerance to thyroid autoantigens leads to CD8+ T cell killing of follicular cells and CD4+ Th1 cytokine release activating macrophages, with anti-TPO and antithyroglobulin antibodies also contributing","B":"Type I hypersensitivity mediated by IgE antibodies against thyroid peroxidase triggers mast cell degranulation and eosinophil activation within the follicular epithelium, causing progressive parenchymal destruction","C":"Molecular mimicry between viral surface antigens and the TSH receptor produces cross-reactive antibodies that block TSH binding and prevent follicular cell stimulation, causing hypothyroidism without lymphocytic infiltration","D":"Complement-mediated lysis of follicular cells initiated by anti-TSH receptor antibodies activating the classical complement pathway is the predominant mechanism, with T cells playing only a secondary modulatory role","E":"Neutrophil-dominated acute inflammatory response to retained thyroglobulin antigens released during viral follicular injury is the inciting event, with self-perpetuating granulomatous inflammation causing progressive tissue loss"},"explanation":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is caused by breakdown of self-tolerance to thyroid autoantigens. The primary effectors are CD8+ cytotoxic T cells that directly kill follicular cells via MHC-mediated recognition, and CD4+ Th1 cells that release IFN-gamma activating macrophages. Antithyroid peroxidase (formerly antimicrosomal) and antithyroglobulin antibodies are detectable in the vast majority of patients and contribute through antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. The immune response is self-perpetuating, distinguishing Hashimoto from viral thyroiditis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hashimoto = most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas. Autoimmune: CD8+ cytotoxic T cells + CD4+ Th1 cytokines. Anti-TPO antibodies are the most sensitive serologic marker. COMLEX: 10-20:1 female-to-male predominance; associated with other autoimmune conditions including type 1 diabetes, SLE, and myasthenia gravis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone excess and deficiency provide the hormonal framework for recognizing each thyroiditis type and Graves disease.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"E":"The immune response is self-pe"}},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of: Hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis, Hypothyroidism, Hashimoto Thyroiditis, Lymphocytic (Subacute) Thyroiditis, Granulomatous (De Quervain) Thyroiditis, Graves Disease (Diffuse toxic hyperplasia).","batch":1,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman presents with 3 weeks of painful anterior neck swelling, fever, and jaw pain following an upper respiratory infection. TSH is suppressed and free T4 is elevated. A thyroid biopsy is obtained. Which of the following best describes the expected radioactive iodine uptake scan finding in this condition?","choices":{"B":"RAI uptake is markedly increased throughout the gland because the diffuse granulomatous inflammation stimulates follicular cell hyperplasia and enhances sodium-iodide symporter expression in surrounding viable follicles","A":"RAI uptake is markedly decreased because the inflammatory process destroys follicular architecture and impairs iodide trapping, even though circulating hormone is high from release of stored hormone","C":"RAI uptake pattern is identical to that seen in Graves disease, because both conditions produce elevated free T4 and T3 with suppressed TSH through direct follicular cell stimulation","D":"RAI uptake is focally increased in the most inflamed lobes only, because activated macrophages upregulate NIS expression in adjacent follicular cells as part of the innate immune response","E":"RAI uptake is normal because the elevated circulating thyroid hormone reflects increased peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 rather than increased glandular secretion from functional follicular cells"},"explanation":"Subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis results from virus-initiated cytotoxic T lymphocyte damage to follicular cells. Disrupted follicles release preformed T4 and T3, causing transient thyrotoxicosis with suppressed TSH. However, the damaged follicular cells cannot trap new iodide, so RAI uptake is markedly decreased. This distinguishes de Quervain from Graves disease, which shows diffusely increased RAI uptake. The biopsy shows the characteristic multinucleated giant cells engulfing colloid. The condition is self-limited, typically resolving in 6 to 8 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: De Quervain = most common cause of thyroid PAIN. Key board trap: thyrotoxicosis with LOW RAI uptake (contrast with Graves = HIGH uptake). COMLEX: Post-viral; associated with coxsackievirus, mumps, SARS-CoV-2. Course: hyperthyroid -> hypothyroid -> euthyroid recovery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone excess and deficiency provide the hormonal framework for recognizing each thyroiditis type and Graves disease.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of: Hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis, Hypothyroidism, Hashimoto Thyroiditis, Lymphocytic (Subacute) Thyroiditis, Granulomatous (De Quervain) Thyroiditis, Graves Disease (Diffuse toxic hyperplasia).","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman presents with a 2-year history of progressive fatigue, cold intolerance, and weight gain. TSH is markedly elevated at 18 mIU/L and anti-TPO antibodies are strongly positive. A thyroid biopsy is obtained. Which of the following best describes the characteristic histologic findings in this condition?","choices":{"C":"Dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with well-developed germinal centers, atrophic thyroid follicles lined by oncocytes with abundant eosinophilic granular cytoplasm, and interstitial fibrosis that stays within the gland","A":"Neutrophilic microabscesses adjacent to collapsed follicles with multinucleated giant cells engulfing residual colloid pools and variable granuloma formation consistent with a virus-initiated self-limited process","B":"Diffuse hypertrophy and hyperplasia of follicular epithelial cells forming tall columnar papillae projecting into follicle lumens, with pale scalloped colloid and scattered lymphoid aggregates in the interstitium","D":"Monotonous sheets of polygonal to spindle-shaped neuroendocrine cells in nested trabeculae without follicle formation, with intercellular amyloid deposits derived from calcitonin-producing parafollicular cells","E":"Solitary well-circumscribed nodule with uniform follicles containing abundant colloid, surrounded by an intact thin fibrous capsule, with no evidence of lymphocytic infiltration or oncocytic change in the adjacent parenchyma"},"explanation":"Hashimoto thyroiditis shows a dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with well-formed germinal centers, which is the histologic hallmark distinguishing it from other thyroiditides. The follicles are atrophic and lined by metaplastic oncocytes (formerly Hurthle cells) with abundant eosinophilic granular cytoplasm. Interstitial fibrosis is present but does not extend beyond the gland capsule, unlike Riedel thyroiditis. De Quervain shows multinucleated giant cells; Graves shows tall columnar hyperplastic epithelium; medullary carcinoma shows amyloid deposits; and follicular adenoma is solitary with intact capsule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hashimoto histology = lymphocytes + germinal centers + oncocytes (Hurthle cells) + atrophic follicles. FNA shows lymphocytes + oncocytes as characteristic. COMLEX: Hashimoto is the most common cause of pediatric goiter in iodine-sufficient areas. Increased risk of primary thyroid B-cell lymphoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone excess and deficiency provide the hormonal framework for recognizing each thyroiditis type and Graves disease.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"De Quervain shows multinucleat","D":"De Quervain shows multinucleat","E":"De Quervain shows multinucleat"}},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of: Hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis, Hypothyroidism, Hashimoto Thyroiditis, Lymphocytic (Subacute) Thyroiditis, Granulomatous (De Quervain) Thyroiditis, Graves Disease (Diffuse toxic hyperplasia).","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman presents with 4 months of weight loss, palpitations, heat intolerance, and bilateral proptosis. TSH is undetectable and free T4 is markedly elevated. Radioactive iodine uptake scan shows diffusely increased uptake throughout the gland. Which of the following best describes the autoimmune mechanism responsible for her hyperthyroidism?","choices":{"E":"Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins bind the TSH receptor and mimic TSH action by activating adenylyl cyclase, increasing intracellular cAMP and stimulating follicular cell growth and constitutive hormone synthesis independent of pituitary regulation","A":"Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies bind and activate TPO directly, bypassing normal TSH regulation and stimulating T4 and T3 synthesis through an antibody-mediated enzyme activation mechanism","B":"Antithyroglobulin antibodies cross-react with the extracellular domain of the TSH receptor through molecular mimicry, creating allosteric receptor activation that drives hormone overproduction","C":"Complement-activating antibodies against follicular cell surface antigens promote membrane attack complex formation, causing uncontrolled intracellular calcium influx and constitutive hormone secretion","D":"Autoantibodies against type 1 deiodinase prevent peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion, driving compensatory follicular cell hypertrophy and autonomous TSH-independent hormone overproduction in feedback loops"},"explanation":"Graves disease is caused by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSIs) that bind the TSH receptor and act as TSH agonists, activating adenylyl cyclase and increasing cAMP. This causes follicular cell hypertrophy, hyperplasia, and constitutive hormone synthesis with loss of normal negative feedback. TSIs are present in approximately 90% of Graves patients and are almost never seen in other thyroid autoimmune diseases. Anti-TPO and antithyroglobulin antibodies are found in Graves but are not the primary drivers of hyperthyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Graves = TSI (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins) = TSH receptor agonist antibodies. TRIAD: hyperthyroidism + exophthalmos + pretibial myxedema. Female predominance 5 to 10:1. COMLEX: Diffusely increased RAI uptake distinguishes Graves from all forms of thyroiditis (which show decreased uptake).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone excess and deficiency provide the hormonal framework for recognizing each thyroiditis type and Graves disease.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Anti-TPO and antithyroglobulin","B":"Anti-TPO and antithyroglobulin"}},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of: Hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis, Hypothyroidism, Hashimoto Thyroiditis, Lymphocytic (Subacute) Thyroiditis, Granulomatous (De Quervain) Thyroiditis, Graves Disease (Diffuse toxic hyperplasia).","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman presents with a 6-month history of fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, and constipation. Her physician orders thyroid function tests. Which of the following laboratory patterns best distinguishes primary hypothyroidism from central (secondary or tertiary) hypothyroidism?","choices":{"B":"In primary hypothyroidism, TSH is elevated from loss of T4-mediated negative feedback on the pituitary, while in secondary or tertiary hypothyroidism TSH is low or inappropriately normal from intrinsic pituitary or hypothalamic failure","A":"In primary hypothyroidism, total T4 is paradoxically elevated because compensatory thyroid hypertrophy driven by high TSH produces increased total hormone synthesis, with the elevated values reflecting increased TBG binding","C":"In primary hypothyroidism, antithyroid peroxidase antibodies are always detectable because TSH excess stimulates autoreactive B cell production against thyroid antigens, while secondary hypothyroidism is invariably seronegative","D":"In primary hypothyroidism, free T3 is elevated because peripheral upregulation of type 2 deiodinase compensates for the low T4 by converting more T3, while secondary hypothyroidism shows simultaneous reductions in both T3 and T4","E":"In primary hypothyroidism, radioactive iodine uptake is uniformly increased due to TSH-driven follicular cell hypertrophy and enhanced NIS expression, whereas secondary hypothyroidism shows reduced uptake from absent pituitary stimulation"},"explanation":"TSH is the most sensitive screening test for primary thyroid dysfunction. In primary hypothyroidism, the thyroid gland fails to produce adequate T4/T3, loss of negative feedback causes pituitary TSH secretion to rise compensatorily. In secondary (pituitary) or tertiary (hypothalamic) hypothyroidism, the defect is upstream, so TSH is low or inappropriately normal despite low free T4. Both forms show low T4, but TSH is the key distinguishing test. Anti-TPO positivity indicates autoimmune etiology but does not distinguish primary from secondary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TSH = most sensitive test for primary hypothyroidism. Elevated TSH = primary; low or normal TSH with low free T4 = central (secondary/tertiary). Free T4 is low in both forms. COMLEX: Most common cause of primary hypothyroidism = Hashimoto; most common cause of central = pituitary adenoma/Sheehan syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone excess and deficiency provide the hormonal framework for recognizing each thyroiditis type and Graves disease.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Anti-TPO positivity indicates "}},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of: Hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis, Hypothyroidism, Hashimoto Thyroiditis, Lymphocytic (Subacute) Thyroiditis, Granulomatous (De Quervain) Thyroiditis, Graves Disease (Diffuse toxic hyperplasia).","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with Graves disease achieves euthyroid status after radioactive iodine therapy. However, 8 months later she returns reporting that her eye proptosis has worsened despite normal thyroid function tests. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of Graves ophthalmopathy?","choices":{"A":"CD4+ T cells in the retro-orbital space release cytokines that stimulate fibroblast proliferation and glycosaminoglycan synthesis, expanding connective tissue and extraocular muscle volume regardless of thyroid hormone levels","B":"Elevated circulating T3 and T4 directly stimulate orbital adipose tissue hypertrophy and lacrimal gland enlargement by binding nuclear thyroid hormone receptors in orbital fibroblasts, causing proptosis proportional to serum hormone levels","C":"TSH receptor antibodies cross-react with ciliary body surface antigens in the orbit, activating the renin-angiotensin system locally and promoting sodium and water retention in the pre-tarsal connective tissue space","D":"Antithyroglobulin antibodies from the systemic circulation cross-react with extraocular muscle myosin through molecular mimicry, causing inflammatory destruction of rectus muscle fibers and fibrotic contracture that displaces the globe","E":"Increased intraocular pressure resulting from ciliary body hyperplasia driven by thyroid hormone excess pushes the globe forward mechanically, and this process is therefore expected to reverse with successful restoration of euthyroid status"},"explanation":"Graves ophthalmopathy is driven by CD4+ T helper cell-mediated inflammation in the retro-orbital space, independent of thyroid hormone levels. Cytokines stimulate fibroblasts to proliferate and produce hydrophilic glycosaminoglycans including hyaluronic acid and chondroitin sulfate. This expands retro-orbital connective tissue, increases extraocular muscle volume due to edema, and adds adipocytes. The process can worsen after RAI therapy (possibly from antigen release) and may persist or progress despite achieving euthyroid state, explaining why proptosis can be irreversible.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Graves ophthalmopathy = T cell-mediated GAG deposition in retro-orbital tissue. Independent of thyroid hormone. May worsen after RAI. Only in Graves disease, not other causes of hyperthyroidism. COMLEX: Pretibial myxedema = same GAG mechanism in skin over shins; occurs in a minority of Graves patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone excess and deficiency provide the hormonal framework for recognizing each thyroiditis type and Graves disease.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"E":"The process can worsen after R"}},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of thyroid masses: Diffuse Nontoxic Hyperplasia, Nodular Hyperplasia (Multinodular Goiter), Follicular Adenoma, Follicular Carcinoma, Papillary Carcinoma, Anaplastic Carcinoma, Medullary Carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman is referred after an incidental 1.5 cm cold thyroid nodule is found on neck ultrasound. Fine-needle aspiration shows papillary clusters of cells with optically clear, overlapping nuclei containing grooves and intranuclear pseudoinclusions. Molecular testing reveals a BRAF V600E point mutation. Which of the following best describes the expected pathologic features and clinical behavior of this neoplasm?","choices":{"E":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma defined by Orphan Annie eye nuclei with nuclear grooves and pseudoinclusions and by psammoma bodies, spreading mainly via lymphatics to cervical nodes yet carrying a 10-year survival above 95%","A":"Follicular carcinoma with RAS mutations and PAX8::PPARG fusions, characterized by capsular and vascular invasion allowing hematogenous spread to bone and lung; distinguished from follicular adenoma only by histologic sampling of the resected tumor capsule","B":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma arising from parafollicular C cells that secrete calcitonin, with polygonal to spindle-shaped cells in nested trabeculae and intercellular amyloid deposits derived from calcitonin polypeptide precursors","C":"Follicular adenoma with oncocytic change showing an intact well-formed capsule and no evidence of vascular or capsular invasion, making the definitive diagnosis of benignity possible only after extensive histologic examination of the resected specimen","D":"Anaplastic carcinoma arising de novo from thyroid follicular cells, showing pleomorphic and spindled cells without follicular differentiation and acquiring TP53 and TERT promoter mutations for aggressive behavior"},"explanation":"The cytology shows the pathognomonic features of papillary thyroid carcinoma: Orphan Annie eye nuclei with finely dispersed chromatin and optically clear appearance, nuclear grooves creating coffee-bean shapes, and intranuclear cytoplasmic pseudoinclusions. BRAF V600E is present in up to 80% of conventional PTCs. Psammoma bodies are concentrically calcified structures virtually diagnostic of PTC when seen in FNA material. Lymphatic spread to cervical nodes occurs in up to 50% of cases, but the 10-year survival exceeds 95%. Follicular carcinoma spreads hematogenously and lacks these nuclear features; medullary carcinoma shows amyloid; adenoma has no invasion; anaplastic carcinoma is undifferentiated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTC hallmarks = Orphan Annie eye nuclei + psammoma bodies + nuclear grooves + pseudoinclusions. BRAF V600E in 80%. Lymphatic spread. Excellent prognosis. COMLEX: Most common thyroid cancer; associated with prior head/neck radiation. Hot nodule = almost never malignant; cold nodule = warrants FNA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — Pharmacologic management of thyroid disorders","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Follicular carcinoma spreads h","B":"Follicular carcinoma spreads h","C":"Follicular carcinoma spreads h","D":"Follicular carcinoma spreads h"}},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of thyroid masses: Diffuse Nontoxic Hyperplasia, Nodular Hyperplasia (Multinodular Goiter), Follicular Adenoma, Follicular Carcinoma, Papillary Carcinoma, Anaplastic Carcinoma, Medullary Carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman undergoes fine-needle aspiration of a 3 cm solitary cold thyroid nodule. The cytology report reads: well-differentiated follicular cells in microfollicular arrangement, indeterminate for malignancy. The nodule is surgically resected. Which histologic finding is necessary to confirm a diagnosis of follicular carcinoma rather than follicular adenoma?","choices":{"C":"Evidence of capsular or vascular invasion on extensive sampling of the tumor capsule, since no reliable cytologic difference separates follicular adenoma from minimally invasive follicular carcinoma on aspiration","A":"Presence of psammoma bodies within fibrovascular tumor cores, which are a specific marker of malignant follicular differentiation and indicate the nodule has undergone early lymphatic micrometastasis to adjacent cervical nodes","B":"High-grade nuclear atypia with prominent nucleoli, nuclear membrane irregularities, and intranuclear cytoplasmic pseudoinclusions, which together confirm malignant transformation of a pre-existing follicular adenoma","D":"Diffuse strong calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen immunoreactivity with amyloid deposits in the stroma, confirming C-cell origin and excluding follicular epithelial neoplasia from the differential diagnosis","E":"Complete loss of thyroglobulin and thyroid peroxidase expression by immunohistochemistry, representing dedifferentiation that marks transition from a benign adenoma to an overtly malignant follicular carcinoma"},"explanation":"Follicular carcinoma is distinguished from follicular adenoma solely by the demonstration of capsular invasion or vascular invasion. There is no reliable cytologic difference on FNA; both are composed of well-differentiated follicular cells. Extensive histologic sampling of the tumor capsule is required to identify capsular breach or tumor in venous vessels within or just beyond the capsule. This is why FNA of a follicular neoplasm is reported as indeterminate and surgical excision is required. Psammoma bodies are characteristic of PTC, not follicular carcinoma; calcitonin immunoreactivity indicates medullary carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Follicular carcinoma vs adenoma = capsular or vascular invasion on histology. FNA CANNOT make this distinction. Classic board trap. COMLEX: Follicular carcinoma = hematogenous spread to bone, lung, liver (NOT lymphatic). More common in iodine-deficient areas. Peak age 40 to 60 years.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — Pharmacologic management of thyroid disorders","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Psammoma bodies are characteri","D":"Psammoma bodies are characteri"}},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of thyroid masses: Diffuse Nontoxic Hyperplasia, Nodular Hyperplasia (Multinodular Goiter), Follicular Adenoma, Follicular Carcinoma, Papillary Carcinoma, Anaplastic Carcinoma, Medullary Carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man presents with a thyroid nodule and bilateral adrenal pheochromocytomas. Serum calcitonin is markedly elevated. Germline testing reveals a germline RET proto-oncogene mutation. Thyroid biopsy shows a nested neoplasm with round cells, stippled chromatin, and pink amyloid deposits in the stroma. Which of the following best characterizes this thyroid neoplasm?","choices":{"D":"Medullary carcinoma arising from parafollicular C cells, which secrete calcitonin that serves as a serum biomarker; familial cases occur in MEN2 through germline RET activation and are bilateral and multicentric with C-cell hyperplasia","A":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma arising in MEN2 syndrome through RET/PTC gene fusions that constitutively activate receptor tyrosine kinase signaling in follicular epithelial cells, producing the characteristic Orphan Annie eye nuclei and psammoma bodies","B":"Follicular carcinoma with extensive vascular invasion associated with PAX8::PPARG translocation, arising in the context of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 and spreading hematogenously to the adrenal gland as a distant metastasis","C":"Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma arising from dedifferentiation of a prior well-differentiated thyroid neoplasm in a young patient, with acquisition of TP53 and TERT promoter mutations producing the undifferentiated aggressive phenotype","E":"Follicular adenoma with toxic autonomous function driven by a TSHR gain-of-function somatic mutation, producing TSH-independent hormone synthesis, presenting as a warm nodule on radioiodine scan and causing the observed adrenal hyperplasia indirectly"},"explanation":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from parafollicular C cells and secretes calcitonin, which serves as both a functional hormone and a diagnostic/follow-up tumor marker. Histologically, polygonal to spindle-shaped cells form nests and trabeculae with characteristic amyloid deposits in the stroma derived from calcitonin polypeptides. In MEN2 syndrome, germline RET mutations drive constitutive receptor tyrosine kinase activation; familial cases present as bilateral and multicentric disease with surrounding C-cell hyperplasia. Approximately 70% of medullary carcinomas are sporadic, with somatic RET mutations. The combination of medullary carcinoma and pheochromocytoma is the hallmark of MEN2A or MEN2B.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medullary carcinoma = C cells + calcitonin + amyloid in stroma. Sporadic 70%, MEN2 30%. Germline RET mutations in familial cases. COMLEX: MEN2A = medullary carcinoma + pheochromocytoma + primary hyperparathyroidism. MEN2B = medullary carcinoma + pheochromocytoma + mucosal neuromas + marfanoid habitus. Prophylactic thyroidectomy in RET mutation carriers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — Pharmacologic management of thyroid disorders","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of thyroid masses: Diffuse Nontoxic Hyperplasia, Nodular Hyperplasia (Multinodular Goiter), Follicular Adenoma, Follicular Carcinoma, Papillary Carcinoma, Anaplastic Carcinoma, Medullary Carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man presents with a 4-week history of rapidly enlarging anterior neck mass, progressive hoarseness, dysphagia, and dyspnea. CT shows a large thyroid mass invading the trachea and right carotid sheath with bilateral pulmonary nodules. Biopsy reveals undifferentiated cells with pleomorphic and spindle-shaped morphology; molecular analysis identifies TP53 and TERT promoter mutations alongside a pre-existing BRAF V600E mutation. Which of the following best characterizes the expected clinical course and pathogenesis of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"High-grade papillary thyroid carcinoma with sarcomatoid differentiation that retains sufficient iodide trapping to respond to total thyroidectomy followed by adjuvant radioactive iodine ablation, with expected 10-year survival exceeding 70%","B":"Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma arising through dedifferentiation of a well-differentiated BRAF-mutated precursor, with acquisition of TP53 and TERT mutations driving the aggressive undifferentiated phenotype; it is one of the most lethal human cancers and most patients die within one year of diagnosis","C":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma in an elderly patient with late-presenting sporadic MEN2 syndrome, requiring calcitonin monitoring and staging for parathyroid involvement alongside total thyroidectomy and central neck dissection","D":"Widely invasive follicular thyroid carcinoma with PAX8::PPARG fusion producing hematogenous pulmonary and tracheal metastases, treated with TSH suppression after total thyroidectomy to slow progression through TSH-dependent growth pathways","E":"Primary thyroid lymphoma arising in the setting of Hashimoto thyroiditis, consisting of extranodal marginal zone B cells that respond well to rituximab-based chemoimmunotherapy with durable remissions in most treated patients"},"explanation":"Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma arises most commonly by dedifferentiation from a pre-existing papillary or follicular carcinoma. The acquisition of TP53 and TERT promoter mutations on top of driver mutations like BRAF V600E transforms a well-differentiated carcinoma into an undifferentiated, highly aggressive tumor. Microscopically, cells lose thyroid markers and show variable morphology including epithelioid, spindled, pleomorphic, and giant-cell patterns. Clinically, it presents as a rapidly enlarging neck mass with invasion of adjacent structures and early distant metastases. In most cases death occurs in less than 1 year; it accounts for only about 1% of thyroid cancers but is responsible for a disproportionate fraction of thyroid cancer deaths.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anaplastic carcinoma = undifferentiated + TP53 and TERT mutations + arise from PTC or follicular carcinoma. In most cases death occurs in less than 1 year (Robbins Ch 24). Rapid growth, dysphagia, dyspnea, hoarseness = compression/invasion of local structures. COMLEX: Constitutes approximately 1% of thyroid cancers but is the major cause of thyroid cancer mortality.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — Pharmacologic management of thyroid disorders","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of thyroid masses: Diffuse Nontoxic Hyperplasia, Nodular Hyperplasia (Multinodular Goiter), Follicular Adenoma, Follicular Carcinoma, Papillary Carcinoma, Anaplastic Carcinoma, Medullary Carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman reports a gradually enlarging anterior neck mass over 15 years, now causing occasional difficulty swallowing and positional shortness of breath. On examination, she has a large asymmetric thyroid mass that does not move easily during swallowing. CT reveals a substernal extension compressing the trachea and displacing the esophagus. TSH and thyroid hormone levels are normal. Which of the following best characterizes this lesion?","choices":{"B":"Multinodular goiter arising from recurrent cycles of follicular hyperplasia and colloid involution, producing asymmetric multilobulated enlargement that can exceed 2000 g; it causes the most extreme enlargements and is often mistaken for a neoplasm","A":"Diffuse nontoxic goiter caused by uniform iodine deficiency producing symmetric hypertrophy of all follicular cells without nodularity, presenting as a smooth homogeneous enlargement that typically resolves completely with dietary iodide supplementation","C":"Follicular adenoma that has undergone cystic change and hemorrhage producing rapid enlargement, presenting as a well-circumscribed solitary nodule with an intact capsule and no evidence of compression of surrounding structures","D":"Riedel thyroiditis characterized by extensive fibrosis replacing normal thyroid parenchyma and extending into surrounding neck structures, producing a stone-hard fixed mass indistinguishable from carcinoma on physical examination","E":"Graves disease with long-standing diffuse toxic hyperplasia producing progressive gland enlargement over 15 years, with normal TSH maintained by gradual downregulation of thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin production over time"},"explanation":"Multinodular goiter results from recurrent episodes of follicular hyperplasia and involution producing variably sized nodules throughout an asymmetrically enlarged gland. Glands can exceed 2000 g and extend substernal, compressing the trachea and esophagus. The dominant clinical features are mass effects including airway obstruction, dysphagia, and SVC syndrome. Most patients are euthyroid, though some develop toxic multinodular goiter (Plummer disease). It produces the most extreme thyroid enlargements and is most often mistaken for a neoplasm clinically. Unlike Graves disease, TSH is normal; unlike simple goiter, it is nodular and asymmetric.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Multinodular goiter = most extreme thyroid enlargement, most often mistaken for neoplasm. Symptoms from mass effects (airway, SVC). Most euthyroid; some become hyperthyroid (Plummer disease). Histology = follicular nodular disease (no capsule between nodules). COMLEX: Cold nodule within MNG warrants FNA to exclude carcinoma. RAI scan shows mixed hot and cold areas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — Pharmacologic management of thyroid disorders","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Unlike Graves disease, TSH is ","E":"Unlike Graves disease, TSH is "}},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of thyroid masses: Diffuse Nontoxic Hyperplasia, Nodular Hyperplasia (Multinodular Goiter), Follicular Adenoma, Follicular Carcinoma, Papillary Carcinoma, Anaplastic Carcinoma, Medullary Carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman undergoes total thyroidectomy for a 4 cm cold thyroid nodule. Pathology confirms follicular thyroid carcinoma with extensive vascular invasion. Five years later, she presents with back pain; MRI reveals a lytic lesion in the L3 vertebra. Serum thyroglobulin is markedly elevated. Which of the following best explains the metastatic route of follicular carcinoma and how it differs from papillary thyroid carcinoma?","choices":{"D":"Follicular carcinoma preferentially disseminates hematogenously to bone, lungs, liver, and brain through vascular invasion of veins at and beyond the tumor capsule, whereas papillary carcinoma metastasizes predominantly via lymphatics to cervical lymph nodes","A":"Follicular carcinoma spreads through afferent lymphatics to ipsilateral cervical lymph nodes as its primary route, mirroring the pattern of papillary carcinoma, with bone metastases occurring only as a late hematogenous event after lymphatic disease is established","B":"Follicular carcinoma secretes PTHrP that selectively stimulates osteoclastic bone resorption, directing tumor cells to skeletal sites while inhibiting pulmonary seeding through systemic hypercalcemia-mediated immune activation against pulmonary micrometastases","C":"Follicular carcinoma and papillary carcinoma share an identical metastatic pattern with predominant lymphatic spread to cervical nodes in early stages, and hematogenous bone metastases occur only when follicular carcinoma undergoes anaplastic transformation","E":"Follicular carcinoma spreads through the perineural lymphatics of the recurrent laryngeal nerve, reaching the posterior mediastinum before entering the systemic venous circulation, explaining the predilection for spinal metastases in the lumbar region"},"explanation":"Follicular carcinoma has little propensity for lymphatic invasion; instead, it invades venous vessels within and beyond the tumor capsule, giving rise to hematogenous metastases to bone, lungs, liver, and brain. Serum thyroglobulin serves as a tumor marker because any residual follicular thyroid tissue should be nearly absent after total thyroidectomy. In contrast, papillary thyroid carcinoma characteristically spreads via lymphatics to regional cervical nodes, even in small lesions. This fundamental difference in metastatic route is a high-yield board distinction: follicular = blood, papillary = lymph. Both carcinomas retain thyroglobulin expression and can take up radioactive iodine for both imaging and ablation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Follicular carcinoma = hematogenous spread (bone, lung, liver, brain). Papillary carcinoma = lymphatic spread (cervical nodes). Key mnemonics: F for Follicular = Far (hematogenous); P for Papillary = Proximal nodes (lymphatics). COMLEX: Thyroglobulin levels monitor recurrence after total thyroidectomy for both PTC and follicular carcinoma. Rising thyroglobulin = recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — Pharmacologic management of thyroid disorders","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"C":"This fundamental difference in"}}],"17":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three zones of the adrenal cortex, their primary hormone products, and the major regulators of each zone.","batch":1,"stem":"Which zone of the adrenal cortex is the ONLY one capable of synthesizing aldosterone, and what enzyme is uniquely responsible?","choices":{"C":"Zona reticularis; 17alpha-hydroxylase, which also governs androgen synthesis and requires ACTH stimulation for activity","A":"Zona glomerulosa; aldosterone synthase (P450 c11AS), which catalyzes the final two steps converting corticosterone to aldosterone","B":"Zona fasciculata; 11beta-hydroxylase, which converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol in the primary glucocorticoid pathway","D":"Zona fasciculata; cholesterol desmolase, which catalyzes the rate-limiting conversion of cholesterol to pregnenolone in all three zones","E":"Zona reticularis; aldosterone synthase upregulated in the inner cortex by angiotensin II when plasma potassium concentration is elevated"},"explanation":"The zona glomerulosa (about 15% of the cortex) is the only zone that contains aldosterone synthase (P450 c11AS), the enzyme that catalyzes the last two steps of aldosterone biosynthesis. The other zones lack this enzyme and therefore cannot produce aldosterone. Angiotensin II and elevated plasma K+ are the major regulators of the zona glomerulosa. The mnemonic GFR = salt/sugar/sex maps the zones: Glomerulosa (aldosterone/salt), Fasciculata (cortisol/sugar), Reticularis (androgens/sex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aldosterone synthase is uniquely expressed in zona glomerulosa — in 21-hydroxylase deficiency aldosterone is lost even though aldosterone synthase is intact, because 21-hydroxylation is needed upstream in the glomerulosa as well. COMLEX: GFR = salt, sugar, sex mnemonic; viscerosomatic levels: kidney T10–T11 (OMM course map) vs reflex T9–L1 with sympathetic T10–L1 (OMM atlas Ch 15); adrenal T8–T10 (OMM atlas Ch 15) vs sympathetic T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — Clinically relevant anatomy, histology, and physiology of the adrenal gland including zone-specific steroidogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three zones of the adrenal cortex, their primary hormone products, and the major regulators of each zone.","batch":1,"stem":"The zona fasciculata constitutes approximately 75% of the adrenal cortex and is responsible for cortisol production. Which hormone from the anterior pituitary is its primary regulator?","choices":{"A":"ACTH, released from the anterior pituitary in response to hypothalamic CRH, which drives cortisol synthesis and fasciculata hypertrophy","B":"FSH from the anterior pituitary acting through cAMP-dependent signaling pathways on zona fasciculata cells to stimulate cortisol production","C":"Angiotensin II acting through AT1 receptors and IP3/DAG signaling cascade in the zona fasciculata to stimulate glucocorticoid synthesis","D":"Renin secreted from the juxtaglomerular apparatus in response to decreased renal perfusion, diffusing to the adrenal cortex","E":"TSH from the anterior pituitary cross-reacting with adrenal ACTH receptors on the zona fasciculata via cAMP second messenger signaling"},"explanation":"The zona fasciculata (about 75% of the cortex per Robbins Ch 24; nearly 80% of cortical volume per Pawlina) is controlled primarily by ACTH, which binds MC2R receptors and stimulates cAMP-dependent cortisol synthesis. CRH from the hypothalamus drives ACTH release; cortisol provides negative feedback to both. Importantly, angiotensin II and potassium regulate only the zona glomerulosa — the fasciculata is largely unresponsive to these signals. This independence explains why Conn syndrome (aldosterone excess) does not elevate cortisol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACTH drives zona fasciculata and reticularis but NOT zona glomerulosa (aldosterone is independently regulated by RAAS/K+). This is classic Step 1 — boards love asking what happens to aldosterone vs cortisol when ACTH is elevated. COMLEX: Know that prolonged ACTH suppression by exogenous steroids causes zona fasciculata atrophy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — Clinically relevant anatomy, histology, and physiology of the adrenal gland including zone-specific steroidogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three zones of the adrenal cortex, their primary hormone products, and the major regulators of each zone.","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student reviews the adrenal cortex using the mnemonic \"GFR = salt, sugar, sex\" to remember zone-to-hormone assignments. Which of the following correctly pairs all three zones with their primary secretory products?","choices":{"B":"Zona Glomerulosa produces aldosterone (salt); Zona Fasciculata produces cortisol (sugar); Zona Reticularis produces DHEA and androgens (sex)","A":"Zona Glomerulosa produces cortisol (sugar); Zona Fasciculata produces aldosterone (salt); Zona Reticularis produces DHEA and androgens (sex)","C":"Zona Glomerulosa produces aldosterone (salt); Zona Fasciculata produces DHEA and androgens (sex); Zona Reticularis produces cortisol (sugar)","D":"Zona Glomerulosa produces DHEA and androgens (sex); Zona Fasciculata produces cortisol (sugar); Zona Reticularis produces aldosterone (salt)","E":"Zona Glomerulosa produces cortisol (sugar); Zona Fasciculata produces DHEA and androgens (sex); Zona Reticularis produces aldosterone (salt)"},"explanation":"GFR from outermost to innermost: Glomerulosa (aldosterone = salt), Fasciculata (cortisol = sugar), Reticularis (androgens/DHEA = sex). A useful extension is that the enzymes controlling each zone differ: zona glomerulosa uses aldosterone synthase and is regulated by angiotensin II/K+; zona fasciculata uses 11beta-hydroxylase and is regulated by ACTH. The zona reticularis secretes primarily DHEA and androstenedione, which are biologically weak androgens.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"The deeper you go, the sweeter it gets\" — outermost is salt (glomerulosa), middle is sugar (fasciculata), inner is sex (reticularis). Boards also test which zone is hyperplastic in CAH, Cushing disease, and which is independently regulated. COMLEX: RAAS regulates glomerulosa (adrenal); ACTH regulates fasciculata/reticularis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — Clinically relevant anatomy, histology, and physiology of the adrenal gland including zone-specific steroidogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain aldosterone's effects on sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the renal collecting duct and describe its cellular mechanism via mineralocorticoid receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"Aldosterone exerts its primary effect on the principal cells of the cortical collecting tubule. Which of the following most accurately describes its intracellular mechanism of action?","choices":{"D":"Aldosterone diffuses into the principal cell and binds a cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptor; the complex enters the nucleus to induce ENaC, Na/K-ATPase, and ROMK mRNA","A":"Aldosterone binds a G protein-coupled receptor on the cell surface and activates cAMP to rapidly phosphorylate and open pre-existing ENaC channels within seconds","B":"Aldosterone binds tyrosine kinase receptors on the luminal membrane of principal cells to phosphorylate ENaC and insert it into the luminal membrane within minutes","C":"Aldosterone binds directly to ENaC on the luminal membrane, constitutively activating sodium entry without requiring gene transcription or new protein synthesis","E":"Aldosterone binds to nuclear receptors in the collecting duct that cross-react with glucocorticoid response elements, inducing broad metabolic changes beyond sodium transport"},"explanation":"Aldosterone is a steroid hormone — it diffuses through the lipid bilayer and binds a cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptor (MR). The aldosterone-MR complex enters the nucleus and induces transcription of mRNAs encoding ENaC (luminal Na+ entry), Na/K-ATPase (basolateral Na+ pump), and ROMK (luminal K+ secretion). Because this requires gene transcription, the onset is delayed ~30-45 minutes, with maximum effect after several hours. This distinguishes it from peptide hormone signaling via cAMP or tyrosine kinase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aldosterone = steroid = nuclear receptor = delayed genomic action (new RNA in about 30 minutes, transport rising at about 45 minutes, Guyton Ch 78). Boards test the delayed time course vs. peptide hormones. ENaC is on the luminal side; Na/K-ATPase is on the basolateral side. Spironolactone competitively blocks the MR; amiloride blocks ENaC directly. COMLEX: Know that 11beta-HSD2 protects MR from cortisol in renal tubular cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome), Addison disease, and secondary aldosteronism: pathology and clinical management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"This distinguishes it from pep"}},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain aldosterone's effects on sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the renal collecting duct and describe its cellular mechanism via mineralocorticoid receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"Cortisol has high affinity for the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) and circulates at concentrations approximately 2000 times higher than aldosterone. Why does cortisol NOT normally activate MRs in renal tubular cells?","choices":{"B":"11beta-HSD2 in renal tubular cells converts cortisol to cortisone, which has low affinity for MRs, keeping cortisol from occupying the receptor","A":"Cortisol is fully bound to corticosteroid-binding globulin in the plasma and cannot dissociate to enter renal tubular cells through the cell membrane","C":"Cortisol lacks the C18 aldehyde group required for the stereomolecular binding specificity of the mineralocorticoid receptor in renal cells","D":"Cortisol is rapidly metabolized by hepatic first-pass metabolism before reaching the systemic circulation and the kidneys","E":"Renal collecting duct principal cells lack cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptors and respond only to aldosterone through luminal ENaC-linked mechanisms"},"explanation":"Despite cortisol having high MR affinity, renal tubular cells express 11beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (11beta-HSD2), which converts cortisol to cortisone. Cortisone binds MRs poorly, preventing cortisol from activating aldosterone-like effects. When 11beta-HSD2 is deficient (genetic defect) or inhibited (by glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice), cortisol accumulates in tubular cells and activates MRs — producing apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME): hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin/aldosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Licorice (glycyrrhetinic acid) inhibits 11beta-HSD2 → cortisol mimics aldosterone → hypertension + hypokalemia with LOW renin AND LOW aldosterone = apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME). This is a classic board trap — most causes of hypertension + hypokalemia have high aldosterone. COMLEX: Know the enzyme and where it acts (renal tubular cells, not liver).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome), Addison disease, and secondary aldosteronism: pathology and clinical management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain aldosterone's effects on sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the renal collecting duct and describe its cellular mechanism via mineralocorticoid receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"Excess aldosterone secretion characteristically causes both hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. Which of the following correctly explains the mechanism underlying BOTH findings?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone upregulates ROMK channels in principal cells, increasing K+ secretion into the tubular lumen causing hypokalemia, and stimulates H+ secretion via intercalated cells increasing bicarbonate reabsorption and raising plasma pH","B":"Aldosterone directly inhibits Na/K-ATPase in the basolateral membrane, reducing K+ reabsorption and simultaneously suppressing carbonic anhydrase to generate metabolic alkalosis","C":"Aldosterone increases ENaC insertion on the basolateral membrane instead of the luminal membrane, reversing the electrochemical gradient that normally drives potassium into the tubular lumen","D":"Aldosterone stimulates sodium reabsorption via thiazide-sensitive NCC transporters in the distal tubule, reducing the electrochemical gradient that drives K+ into the tubule lumen","E":"Aldosterone binds luminal ROMK channels directly and opens them within seconds independent of gene transcription, producing acute K+ wasting and alkalosis within minutes"},"explanation":"Aldosterone acts on two cell types in the cortical collecting duct. In principal cells, it upregulates ENaC (Na+ entry) and ROMK (K+ secretion), causing hypokalemia. In intercalated cells, aldosterone stimulates H+ secretion into the tubular lumen (via H+-ATPase) in exchange for K+ reabsorption — this H+ loss into the urine decreases plasma H+ concentration, raising pH and causing metabolic alkalosis. The classic triad of hyperaldosteronism: hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conn syndrome (primary hyperaldosteronism) = hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis + low renin. Boards frequently test why alkalosis occurs with aldosterone excess — it is due to intercalated cell H+ secretion, NOT just a simple dilution effect. COMLEX: Recognize hypokalemia-induced muscle weakness as a clinical feature of Conn syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome), Addison disease, and secondary aldosteronism: pathology and clinical management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the regulation of aldosterone secretion by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and by hyperkalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with chronic heart failure is started on a low-sodium diet by his cardiologist. Which of the following most directly describes the cascade leading to increased aldosterone secretion in this patient?","choices":{"C":"Reduced renal perfusion triggers renin release; renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, and lung ACE forms angiotensin II, which stimulates aldosterone secretion","A":"Decreased plasma sodium directly activates anterior pituitary ACTH release, which travels to the zona glomerulosa to stimulate aldosterone synthesis and secretion","B":"Decreased plasma sodium triggers atrial natriuretic peptide release from cardiac atria, which increases renin and amplifies angiotensin II production causing aldosterone secretion","D":"Decreased plasma sodium stimulates posterior pituitary ADH release, and ADH binds V1 receptors on the zona glomerulosa, directly triggering aldosterone synthesis","E":"Decreased plasma sodium activates the sympathetic adrenal medulla to release epinephrine, which stimulates aldosterone synthase upregulation via beta-adrenergic receptors"},"explanation":"The RAAS cascade: decreased renal perfusion (from heart failure, low sodium, or hypovolemia) → juxtaglomerular cells release renin → renin cleaves angiotensinogen (liver) to angiotensin I → ACE in pulmonary endothelium converts Ang I to Ang II → Ang II binds AT1 receptors on zona glomerulosa → aldosterone synthesis and secretion. Aldosterone then increases ENaC/Na-K-ATPase expression → Na+ retention → volume expansion → restored perfusion. ANP actually DECREASES aldosterone and opposes this cascade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the full RAAS cascade cold: renin (JGA) → angiotensinogen → Ang I → ACE (lung) → Ang II → aldosterone. ACE inhibitors block the lung step; ARBs block AT1 receptors. Both reduce aldosterone and can cause hyperkalemia. COMLEX: RAAS viscerosomatic reflex levels: kidney T10–T11 (OMM course map) vs T9–L1 (OMM atlas Ch 15), adrenal T8–T10 (OMM atlas Ch 15) vs sympathetic T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5; renal artery stenosis is a classic cause of secondary hyperaldosteronism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis: CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback regulation, and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"ANP actually DECREASES aldoste"}},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the regulation of aldosterone secretion by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and by hyperkalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the most potent DIRECT stimulus for aldosterone secretion from the zona glomerulosa, acting independently of the renin-angiotensin system?","choices":{"E":"Increased plasma potassium, which directly depolarizes zona glomerulosa cells and triggers calcium-dependent aldosterone synthesis without angiotensin II","A":"Decreased plasma sodium concentration acting directly on zona glomerulosa cells via osmoreceptors to stimulate aldosterone synthase gene transcription","B":"Increased ACTH from the anterior pituitary during periods of physiologic stress, which has the same potency on zona glomerulosa as it does on zona fasciculata","C":"Decreased atrial natriuretic peptide concentration during plasma volume contraction, which removes tonic inhibition of zona glomerulosa aldosterone secretion","D":"Increased cortisol secretion from zona fasciculata cross-activating mineralocorticoid receptors on zona glomerulosa cells to stimulate aldosterone production"},"explanation":"Both angiotensin II and elevated plasma K+ are the most potent stimulators of aldosterone secretion. Elevated K+ directly depolarizes zona glomerulosa cell membranes, opening voltage-gated calcium channels; the Ca2+ influx activates calcium-calmodulin pathways and aldosterone synthase. This is the body's direct mechanism for handling acute hyperkalemia: more K+ → more aldosterone → more ROMK → K+ excreted. ACTH plays only a permissive role. Decreased Na+ and ANP have minor effects on aldosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boards frequently test that K+ AND Ang II are the two primary aldosterone stimulators. A patient with hyperkalemia from renal failure who also has low renin (renal disease suppresses renin) still has elevated aldosterone via the direct K+ pathway. COMLEX: This direct K+-aldosterone axis is also important in understanding why ACE inhibitors can cause dangerous hyperkalemia (removes both Ang II and indirect K+ feedback).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis: CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback regulation, and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Decreased Na+ and ANP have min","B":"ACTH plays only a permissive r","C":"Decreased Na+ and ANP have min"}},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the regulation of aldosterone secretion by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and by hyperkalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma (Conn syndrome) presents with hypertension and hypokalemia. Plasma renin activity is suppressed and aldosterone is markedly elevated. Which of the following best explains why plasma renin is suppressed in this condition?","choices":{"D":"Excess aldosterone causes sodium retention and volume expansion, raising arterial pressure and renal perfusion, which suppresses juxtaglomerular renin release","A":"Excess aldosterone directly inhibits renin gene transcription in juxtaglomerular cells via a nuclear mineralocorticoid receptor mechanism independent of volume or pressure","B":"Elevated plasma potassium from Conn syndrome directly blocks juxtaglomerular macula densa cells from detecting tubular flow changes and releasing renin","C":"Excess aldosterone activates ACTH receptors on juxtaglomerular cells through cross-reactivity, inhibiting renin release by a pituitary-adrenal negative feedback pathway","E":"Excess aldosterone competitively binds angiotensin II receptors (AT1) on juxtaglomerular cells, blocking angiotensin II-mediated stimulation of renin secretion"},"explanation":"In Conn syndrome, autonomous aldosterone secretion from the adenoma causes Na+ retention, extracellular volume expansion, and hypertension. The elevated blood volume increases renal perfusion pressure, which suppresses juxtaglomerular renin release via baroreceptor feedback. The diagnostic hallmark is therefore high aldosterone with LOW renin (an elevated aldosterone-to-renin ratio). This contrasts with secondary hyperaldosteronism (e.g., renal artery stenosis), where both renin and aldosterone are elevated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn) = high aldosterone + LOW renin. Secondary hyperaldosteronism = high aldosterone + HIGH renin. An elevated plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio is the screening test for Conn (Robbins Ch 24). COMLEX: Conn syndrome is a named CP3 adrenal condition; hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis + low renin = Conn until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis: CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback regulation, and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three zones of the adrenal cortex, their primary hormone products, and the major regulators of each zone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman undergoes bilateral adrenalectomy for bilateral adrenocortical carcinoma. She is started on hydrocortisone post-operatively but her fludrocortisone prescription is inadvertently not filled. On day 3, she presents with hypotension, serum sodium of 121 mEq/L, and serum potassium of 6.8 mEq/L with peaked T waves on ECG. Which of the following most directly explains all three findings?","choices":{"E":"Without fludrocortisone, ENaC and Na/K-ATPase are not upregulated in the collecting duct, causing renal sodium wasting, volume depletion, hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia","A":"Hydrocortisone replacement is providing excess cortisol that cross-activates mineralocorticoid receptors, causing paradoxical sodium wasting from receptor desensitization in the renal tubules","B":"The bilateral adrenalectomy removed the adrenal medulla, eliminating epinephrine and norepinephrine, which causes distributive shock and secondary dilutional hyponatremia","C":"Insufficient hydrocortisone dosing has caused cortisol deficiency with compensatory ACTH-driven hypertrophy of residual adrenal tissue producing excess androgens","D":"Post-surgical inflammation has triggered cytokine-mediated suppression of the remaining renal ENaC channels, independent of aldosterone or mineralocorticoid levels"},"explanation":"After bilateral adrenalectomy, both glucocorticoid (cortisol) and mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) replacement are required. Hydrocortisone covers the glucocorticoid deficit. Without fludrocortisone (a synthetic mineralocorticoid), there is no aldosterone analog to drive ENaC and Na/K-ATPase expression in the principal cells of the collecting duct. The result is catastrophic renal Na+ wasting, hyponatremia, volume depletion, hypotension, and life-threatening hyperkalemia (because K+ is not excreted). This is acute primary adrenal insufficiency — sodium loss can cause death within days.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Always replace both glucocorticoid AND mineralocorticoid after bilateral adrenalectomy. Hydrocortisone alone is insufficient because it has weak mineralocorticoid activity at physiologic doses. Boards test: \"what is missing in this vignette?\" — fludrocortisone is the mineralocorticoid replacement. COMLEX: Addisonian crisis = hypotension + hyponatremia + hyperkalemia; treat with IV hydrocortisone AND normal saline.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — Clinically relevant anatomy, histology, and physiology of the adrenal gland","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three zones of the adrenal cortex, their primary hormone products, and the major regulators of each zone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old female infant presents in the ED with poor feeding, vomiting, dehydration, and a sodium of 118 mEq/L with a potassium of 7.2 mEq/L. Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone is markedly elevated. Genetic testing reveals homozygous loss-of-function mutations in CYP21A2. Which of the following correctly explains how this enzyme deficiency leads to the electrolyte abnormalities?","choices":{"A":"21-hydroxylase deficiency blocks both cortisol synthesis in the fasciculata and aldosterone synthesis in the glomerulosa, so aldosterone loss causes renal sodium wasting and hyperkalemia","B":"21-hydroxylase deficiency blocks only zona reticularis androgen synthesis; aldosterone from the zona glomerulosa is unaffected because aldosterone synthase uses a separate enzyme pathway","C":"21-hydroxylase deficiency causes excess DOC accumulation with mineralocorticoid activity, leading to sodium retention, hypertension, and hypokalemia rather than sodium wasting","D":"All three zones use 21-hydroxylase equally, so aldosterone production is maintained through compensatory upregulation of aldosterone synthase in response to markedly elevated ACTH","E":"21-hydroxylase deficiency blocks cortisol synthesis only in zona fasciculata while aldosterone is preserved; the electrolyte changes result from excess androgens causing renal tubular dysfunction"},"explanation":"CYP21A2 (21-hydroxylase) is required in both the zona fasciculata (for cortisol: progesterone → 11-deoxycortisol pathway) and the zona glomerulosa (for aldosterone: progesterone → 11-deoxycorticosterone → aldosterone pathway). In the classic salt-wasting form, both cortisol and aldosterone are severely deficient (simple virilizing disease without salt wasting occurs in about one-third of patients, Robbins Ch 24). Without aldosterone, ENaC and Na/K-ATPase are not expressed, causing Na+ wasting and hyperkalemia. Elevated ACTH (from loss of cortisol feedback) drives excess 17-OH-progesterone into the androgen pathway. 11-hydroxylase deficiency is the one that causes DOC accumulation and hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 21-OH deficiency = most common CAH = salt-wasting (no cortisol, no aldosterone) + virilization (excess androgens) + low BP + high renin. 11-OH deficiency = hypertension (DOC accumulates and has mineralocorticoid activity) + virilization + no aldosterone. Mnemonic: \"If the enzyme starts with 1, it causes hypertension (11-OH); if it ends with 1, it causes virilization.\" COMLEX: CAH is autosomal recessive; hyperpigmentation from elevated ACTH/MSH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — Clinically relevant anatomy, histology, and physiology of the adrenal gland","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the three zones of the adrenal cortex, their primary hormone products, and the major regulators of each zone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis has been on high-dose prednisone for 9 months. His rheumatologist abruptly discontinues the medication. Two days later he presents with hypotension, hypoglycemia, nausea, and hyponatremia. Which of the following best explains why abrupt steroid withdrawal caused this presentation?","choices":{"D":"Chronic exogenous glucocorticoid suppressed pituitary ACTH via negative feedback, causing adrenal cortical atrophy; withdrawal leaves the zona fasciculata unable to produce cortisol, causing hypoglycemia, hypotension, and impaired stress response","A":"Prednisone directly destroyed adrenal cortical cells by apoptosis over 9 months; the resulting adrenal atrophy causes permanent inability to synthesize any steroid hormones","B":"Prednisone withdrawal causes a rebound inflammatory response that destroys adrenocortical cells via cytokine-mediated autoimmune mechanisms within 48 hours","C":"Abrupt prednisone cessation triggers massive cortisol release from any remaining cortical cells, causing rapid receptor desensitization and paradoxical adrenal insufficiency","E":"Chronic prednisone use permanently inhibits cholesterol desmolase, the rate-limiting enzyme for steroidogenesis, preventing recovery even after exogenous steroid cessation"},"explanation":"Exogenous glucocorticoids suppress CRH (hypothalamus) and ACTH (pituitary) via negative feedback. With prolonged suppression, the zona fasciculata atrophies from lack of ACTH trophic support. After abrupt cessation, the atrophied gland cannot produce sufficient cortisol. Cortisol deficiency causes: hypoglycemia (loss of gluconeogenic effect), hypotension (loss of vascular alpha-1 receptor upregulation and catecholamine permissive effect), and hyponatremia (inappropriate ADH secretion without cortisol inhibition). The rule: corticosteroids given for more than 2 weeks can suppress the adrenal (Katzung Ch 39), so taper; never stop abruptly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Any patient on steroids for more than 2 weeks may have adrenal suppression and needs a taper (Katzung Ch 39). \"Steroid-induced adrenal insufficiency\" = inability to mount a cortisol stress response after HPA axis suppression. Boards test: what labs help distinguish primary vs secondary adrenal insufficiency? Primary (Addison) = high ACTH + low cortisol + hyperpigmentation + mineralocorticoid loss. Secondary (pituitary/steroid suppression) = low ACTH + low cortisol, NO hyperpigmentation, aldosterone often preserved. COMLEX: Taper steroids gradually to allow HPA axis recovery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — Clinically relevant anatomy, histology, and physiology of the adrenal gland","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain aldosterone's effects on sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the renal collecting duct and describe its cellular mechanism via mineralocorticoid receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) is started on spironolactone while awaiting surgical resection of her adrenal adenoma. Her blood pressure decreases and serum potassium normalizes within 2 weeks. Spironolactone achieves its therapeutic effect through which of the following mechanisms?","choices":{"E":"Spironolactone competitively antagonizes the cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptor in principal cells, preventing aldosterone-driven transcription of ENaC, Na/K-ATPase, and ROMK","A":"Spironolactone blocks ACE activity in the pulmonary endothelium, preventing formation of angiotensin II and thereby reducing the signal that drives zona glomerulosa aldosterone synthesis","B":"Spironolactone blocks ENaC channels on the luminal membrane of principal cells directly, preventing sodium entry without interfering with mineralocorticoid receptor binding","C":"Spironolactone inhibits aldosterone synthase (P450 c11AS) in the zona glomerulosa, blocking the final two biosynthetic steps that convert corticosterone to aldosterone","D":"Spironolactone inhibits 11beta-HSD2 in tubular cells, preventing cortisol-to-cortisone conversion and indirectly competing with aldosterone for mineralocorticoid receptor occupancy"},"explanation":"Spironolactone is a competitive mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) antagonist. It enters principal cells and displaces aldosterone from the cytoplasmic MR, preventing the receptor-hormone complex from entering the nucleus and transcribing ENaC, Na/K-ATPase, and ROMK. Without these new transport proteins, sodium is not reabsorbed, potassium is not secreted, and blood pressure falls. Amiloride, in contrast, blocks ENaC directly downstream of the receptor. Spironolactone's anti-androgenic side effects (gynecomastia, impotence) occur because it also weakly antagonizes androgen receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spironolactone = MR antagonist (works even if no aldosterone is present, making it useful in heart failure too). Eplerenone is a more selective MR antagonist without the anti-androgenic side effects. Amiloride blocks ENaC — useful when MR antagonist side effects are problematic. COMLEX: Spironolactone causes gynecomastia (anti-androgenic effect) — boards love testing this adverse effect in a male patient with heart failure on spironolactone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome): aldosterone-to-renin ratio, adrenal vein sampling, and adrenalectomy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Amiloride, in contrast, blocks"}},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain aldosterone's effects on sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the renal collecting duct and describe its cellular mechanism via mineralocorticoid receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man presents with hypertension (168/102 mmHg) and weakness. He has been consuming large amounts of licorice candy daily for 6 months. Labs: Na+ 148, K+ 2.8, bicarbonate 30 mEq/L. Plasma aldosterone is LOW and plasma renin is SUPPRESSED. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of his hypertension?","choices":{"C":"Glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice inhibits 11beta-HSD2, so cortisol activates renal mineralocorticoid receptors, mimicking primary aldosteronism with low aldosterone","A":"Licorice contains phytoestrogens that bind mineralocorticoid receptors directly and constitutively activate ENaC insertion without requiring cortisol or aldosterone","B":"Glycyrrhetinic acid stimulates renin release from juxtaglomerular cells, causing secondary hyperaldosteronism with markedly elevated plasma aldosterone and renin","D":"Licorice contains synthetic aldosterone analogues that bind and activate ENaC on the luminal membrane directly, bypassing the need for mineralocorticoid receptor gene transcription","E":"Glycyrrhetinic acid increases cortisol-binding globulin synthesis, releasing excess free cortisol into tubular cells where it activates alpha-1 receptors causing vasoconstriction"},"explanation":"Glycyrrhetinic acid (active compound in licorice) inhibits 11beta-HSD2. Normally, 11beta-HSD2 converts cortisol to cortisone in renal tubular cells, preventing cortisol from occupying MRs. When inhibited, cortisol accumulates and activates MRs just like aldosterone would, causing sodium retention, hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis. The key diagnostic clue: LOW aldosterone with LOW renin. Renin is suppressed because volume expansion from cortisol-mediated Na+ retention provides negative feedback. This is apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Licorice = 11beta-HSD2 inhibitor = apparent mineralocorticoid excess = low renin + low aldosterone + hypertension + hypokalemia. This is the OPPOSITE of Conn syndrome (which has high aldosterone) — boards use this to test understanding of the enzyme, not just the hormone. COMLEX: AME from licorice is a clinical presentation question; recognizing low aldosterone + low renin + hypertension = AME (not Conn, not secondary hyperaldosteronism).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome): aldosterone-to-renin ratio, adrenal vein sampling, and adrenalectomy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain aldosterone's effects on sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the renal collecting duct and describe its cellular mechanism via mineralocorticoid receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman undergoes successful laparoscopic right adrenalectomy for a unilateral aldosterone-secreting adenoma. Six weeks post-operatively, serum potassium has normalized to 4.0 mEq/L, but her blood pressure remains elevated at 154/94 mmHg despite the resolution of hyperaldosteronism. Which of the following best explains why hypertension persists?","choices":{"B":"Prolonged aldosterone-mediated hypertension caused structural vascular changes, including arterial remodeling and stiffness, that do not immediately reverse after adenoma removal","A":"Residual aldosterone secretion from the contralateral adrenal gland continues to activate ENaC and ROMK, maintaining persistent sodium retention and sustained blood pressure elevation","C":"Surgical removal of the adenoma triggers compensatory renin hypersecretion from the juxtaglomerular apparatus, which maintains angiotensin II-mediated hypertension independent of aldosterone","D":"Post-surgical hydrocortisone stress dosing has cross-activated mineralocorticoid receptors throughout the renal collecting duct, maintaining sodium retention and volume expansion","E":"Potassium normalization has eliminated hypokalemia-induced vasodilation, paradoxically increasing systemic vascular resistance and raising blood pressure after successful surgery"},"explanation":"Even after successful adrenalectomy for Conn syndrome, hypertension often persists. Prolonged aldosterone excess causes structural cardiovascular remodeling: arterial wall fibrosis, increased collagen deposition, reduced arterial compliance, and hypertrophy of vascular smooth muscle. These structural changes do not reverse immediately — patients often require continued antihypertensive therapy for months to years post-operatively. Normalizing potassium happens quickly (days to weeks), but reversing vascular remodeling takes much longer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key concept — removing the hormonal stimulus does not immediately reverse structural end-organ damage. This applies to Conn (vascular remodeling), acromegaly (cardiomegaly), and Cushing (osteoporosis). Boards may present a vignette where a patient is treated but the finding persists. COMLEX: Post-adrenalectomy hypertension is a clinical management question; younger patients with shorter duration of disease have better cure rates after surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome): aldosterone-to-renin ratio, adrenal vein sampling, and adrenalectomy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the regulation of aldosterone secretion by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and by hyperkalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man receives 3 L of IV normal saline over a few hours. His central venous pressure rises, and plasma volume expands. Plasma renin falls, and a hormone released in response to the volume expansion also acts on the adrenal cortex to reduce aldosterone secretion. Which signal best fits this description?","choices":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone from the posterior pituitary, which binds V2 receptors on glomerulosa cells and decreases aldosterone synthase","B":"Atrial natriuretic peptide from stretched atrial cells, which decreases aldosterone secretion by the zona glomerulosa","C":"Rising plasma potassium from the saline load, which hyperpolarizes glomerulosa cells and decreases aldosterone secretion","D":"Increased ACTH from the anterior pituitary, which shifts glomerulosa precursors toward cortisol and away from aldosterone","E":"Higher plasma sodium from the saline, which suppresses aldosterone as its main controlling factor"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 78) lists the regulators of aldosterone secretion: increased extracellular potassium and increased angiotensin II greatly increase it, increased sodium slightly decreases it, increased atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP), secreted by the heart when cells of the atria are stretched, decreases it, and ACTH is necessary for normal secretion but has little effect on its rate. An increase in ANP secondary to plasma volume expansion and atrial stretch may induce natriuresis, in part by inhibiting aldosterone secretion. ADH acts on collecting duct V2 receptors, not on glomerulosa cells. Saline does not raise potassium, and a rise in potassium increases aldosterone rather than decreasing it. ACTH plays a permissive role and does not switch the glomerulosa to cortisol. Sodium concentration has only a minor effect on aldosterone, so it is not the main controller.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aldosterone regulators (Guyton Ch 78): ↑K+ and ↑angiotensin II strongly increase; ↑Na+ slightly decreases; ↑ANP decreases; ACTH is permissive. COMLEX: Volume expansion lowers aldosterone by two routes: less renin and angiotensin II, and more ANP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in collecting duct principal cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"ADH acts on collecting duct V2","D":"ACTH plays a permissive role a"}},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the regulation of aldosterone secretion by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and by hyperkalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes is started on lisinopril. Four weeks later his serum potassium has risen from 4.0 to 5.6 mEq/L. He takes no other medications that affect potassium. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of his hyperkalemia?","choices":{"C":"Lisinopril blocks ACE, preventing angiotensin II formation; less Ang II lowers zona glomerulosa aldosterone secretion, reducing ENaC and ROMK expression and renal potassium excretion","A":"Lisinopril directly blocks ENaC channels in the renal collecting duct independent of the aldosterone pathway, preventing sodium entry and allowing potassium to accumulate in tubular cells","B":"Lisinopril-induced bradykinin accumulation activates aldosterone receptors on zona glomerulosa cells, paradoxically increasing aldosterone secretion and driving excess ROMK-mediated K+ reabsorption","D":"Lisinopril directly inhibits Na/K-ATPase on the basolateral membrane of principal cells by reducing angiotensin II-mediated pump phosphorylation, impairing K+ entry into the cell","E":"Lisinopril reduces Ang II, which directly stimulates ROMK channels on the tubular lumen independent of aldosterone, and reduced ROMK activation causes K+ to accumulate in the cell"},"explanation":"ACE inhibitors (lisinopril) block the conversion of Ang I to Ang II. With less Ang II, there is less stimulation of AT1 receptors on the zona glomerulosa, resulting in reduced aldosterone secretion. Without aldosterone, the principal cells of the collecting duct do not upregulate ENaC or ROMK — potassium is not secreted into the tubular lumen, and serum K+ rises. Hyperkalemia is a well-known class effect of ACE inhibitors and ARBs. Patients at highest risk: chronic kidney disease, diabetes (type 4 RTA), or concurrent use of potassium-sparing diuretics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor side effects: hyperkalemia (most common electrolyte issue), dry cough (bradykinin), angioedema (bradykinin). ARBs also cause hyperkalemia but not cough. The hyperkalemia mechanism is always via reduced aldosterone, not direct renal effects. COMLEX: Avoid ACE inhibitors + spironolactone together (both reduce aldosterone effect) unless carefully monitored — dangerous hyperkalemia risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis: CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback regulation, and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the regulation of aldosterone secretion by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and by hyperkalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman presents with hypertension (BP 162/98), hypokalemia, and hirsutism; clitoromegaly was noted in infancy. Labs: cortisol low, aldosterone low, ACTH markedly elevated, plasma renin suppressed, and 11-deoxycorticosterone (DOC) markedly elevated. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous CYP11B1 mutation. Which of the following best explains why she has hypertension despite having low aldosterone?","choices":{"B":"Elevated ACTH drives excess cortisol precursor synthesis in the zona fasciculata, and these precursors directly activate glucocorticoid receptors on vascular smooth muscle causing vasoconstriction","A":"Low cortisol from the enzymatic block removes permissive upregulation of alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on arterioles, triggering compensatory renin and angiotensin II hypertension","C":"11-deoxycorticosterone accumulates proximal to the block and activates MRs as a potent mineralocorticoid, causing sodium retention, volume expansion, and suppression of renin and aldosterone","D":"11beta-hydroxylase deficiency blocks aldosterone synthesis in zona glomerulosa, triggering compensatory renin elevation and secondary mineralocorticoid-mediated hypertension via angiotensin II","E":"Low aldosterone triggers compensatory ACTH hypersecretion, and excess ACTH directly binds V1 receptors on systemic arterioles causing vasoconstriction and sustained blood pressure elevation"},"explanation":"CYP11B1 (11beta-hydroxylase) deficiency blocks the conversion of 11-deoxycorticosterone (DOC) to corticosterone and 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol. DOC accumulates and has significant mineralocorticoid activity — it binds MRs in the collecting duct and acts like aldosterone, causing Na+ retention, volume expansion, hypertension, and hypokalemia. The volume expansion suppresses renin (explaining low renin + low aldosterone). This is the classic board distinction: 21-OH deficiency causes salt-WASTING (both cortisol and aldosterone low), while 11-OH deficiency causes hypertension (DOC accumulates with mineralocorticoid activity).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 11-OH deficiency = hypertension + virilization (in females) + low cortisol + elevated DOC + suppressed renin/aldosterone. The HTN is from DOC, not aldosterone. First Aid mnemonic: \"If the enzyme starts with 1 (11-OH), it causes hypertension.\" Contrast with 21-OH: no HTN, salt-wasting, virilization. COMLEX: All CAH types are autosomal recessive with bilateral adrenal hyperplasia from elevated ACTH; hyperpigmentation occurs because MSH is co-secreted with ACTH from POMC.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis: CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback regulation, and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"}],"18":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain cortisol’s effects on carbohydrate metabolism (gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance), protein metabolism (catabolism), and fat metabolism (mobilization and redistribution).","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes cortisol's primary mechanism for increasing blood glucose concentration?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates hepatic gluconeogenesis by inducing gluconeogenic enzymes and mobilizing muscle amino acids, while it reduces GLUT4 translocation to produce insulin resistance","B":"Directly inhibits insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells by hyperpolarizing the beta cell membrane, thereby preventing glucose uptake into adipose and muscle tissue","C":"Stimulates glycogen synthesis in the liver to expand glycogen stores, which are subsequently mobilized by glucagon-induced glycogenolysis when blood glucose begins to fall","D":"Increases intestinal glucose absorption by upregulating sodium-glucose cotransporter type 1 expression in duodenal and jejunal enterocytes, elevating postprandial glucose","E":"Activates GLUT4 translocation to the plasma membrane in skeletal muscle cells to enhance glucose uptake during stress, generating ATP to fuel the fight-or-flight response"},"explanation":"Cortisol stimulates hepatic gluconeogenesis 6- to 10-fold by inducing gluconeogenic enzymes (PEPCK, glucose-6-phosphatase) and mobilizing amino acids from muscle catabolism as substrates. Simultaneously, cortisol inhibits GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue, impairing insulin-stimulated glucose uptake and causing insulin resistance. Insulin secretion is not directly blocked by cortisol; rather, the resulting hyperglycemia may secondarily increase insulin output. Cortisol does not increase GLUT4 translocation — it decreases it. SGLT1 upregulation and glycogen storage are not cortisol's primary glucose-raising mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA: Cortisol is A BIG FIB — ↑Appetite, ↑Blood pressure, ↑Insulin resistance, ↑Gluconeogenesis, ↓Fibroblast activity, ↓Inflammatory/Immune responses, ↓Bone formation. Chronic exogenous steroids cause steroid-induced diabetes through these mechanisms. COMLEX: Cushing syndrome (excess cortisol) classically presents with central obesity, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and proximal muscle weakness — connect to OMT avoidance in patients on chronic steroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome: excess cortisol disrupts glucose, protein, and fat homeostasis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"SGLT1 upregulation and glycoge","D":"SGLT1 upregulation and glycoge","E":"Cortisol does not increase GLU"}},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain cortisol’s effects on carbohydrate metabolism (gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance), protein metabolism (catabolism), and fat metabolism (mobilization and redistribution).","batch":1,"stem":"Cortisol's net effect on protein metabolism in skeletal muscle contributes to which of the following laboratory and clinical findings?","choices":{"E":"Elevated plasma amino acid concentrations and decreased muscle mass, as cortisol reduces protein synthesis and increases catabolism, mobilizing amino acids as gluconeogenic precursors for the liver","A":"Decreased plasma amino acid concentrations and increased muscle hypertrophy, as cortisol activates mTOR signaling to promote anabolic protein synthesis during physiologic stress responses","B":"Elevated plasma amino acid concentrations and increased muscle mass, as cortisol selectively inhibits ubiquitin-proteasome degradation while stimulating satellite cell differentiation in muscle fibers","C":"Normal plasma amino acid concentrations and decreased muscle mass, as cortisol selectively catabolizes contractile proteins while routing the released amino acids to cardiac muscle for hypertrophic remodeling","D":"Decreased plasma amino acid concentrations and decreased muscle mass, as cortisol sequesters circulating amino acids in hepatic storage vacuoles, depleting both plasma pools and muscle structural proteins"},"explanation":"Cortisol decreases protein synthesis and increases catabolism in skeletal muscle (and most extrahepatic tissues), releasing amino acids into the bloodstream. These amino acids are taken up by the liver and used as gluconeogenic substrates. The result is elevated plasma amino acids combined with progressive loss of muscle mass — the proximal myopathy seen in Cushing syndrome. Cortisol does not stimulate mTOR or satellite cell activation; those are anabolic pathways. Hepatic proteins (albumin, clotting factors) are actually increased by cortisol, unlike muscle proteins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proximal muscle weakness is a hallmark of Cushing syndrome — driven by cortisol-induced protein catabolism in skeletal muscle. Distinguished from hypothyroid myopathy (normal cortisol, elevated CK) and polymyositis (elevated CK, inflammatory infiltrates). COMLEX: Patients on chronic steroids are at high risk for osteoporosis and myopathy; OMT should be modified to avoid high-velocity low-amplitude techniques at fragile vertebral segments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome: excess cortisol disrupts glucose, protein, and fat homeostasis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain cortisol’s effects on carbohydrate metabolism (gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance), protein metabolism (catabolism), and fat metabolism (mobilization and redistribution).","batch":1,"stem":"Excess cortisol causes central obesity with buffalo hump and moon facies despite also promoting lipolysis in peripheral fat depots. Which of the following best explains this apparent paradox?","choices":{"B":"Cortisol-driven peripheral fat mobilization is offset by cortisol-stimulated appetite and caloric intake, with central, truncal, and facial adipose depots preferentially re-accumulating fat due to their higher glucocorticoid receptor density and distinct metabolic responses","A":"Cortisol selectively inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase in central adipose tissue while activating it in peripheral depots, routing free fatty acids toward visceral fat via VLDL repackaging in the liver","C":"Cortisol reduces fatty acid beta-oxidation in peripheral tissues, causing re-esterification of mobilized fatty acids in the liver and their export as VLDL preferentially to central adipose depots","D":"Cortisol-induced hyperinsulinemia promotes de novo lipogenesis exclusively in visceral adipose tissue because portal insulin concentrations are highest in the omentum and mesenteric fat depots","E":"Peripheral fat undergoes glucocorticoid-receptor-mediated apoptosis, while central adipocytes are protected from apoptosis by higher concentrations of BCL-2 anti-apoptotic proteins in visceral fat"},"explanation":"The fat redistribution of Cushing syndrome results from two concurrent cortisol effects: peripheral fat mobilization (reduced alpha-glycerophosphate availability shifts cells from glucose to fat oxidation, releasing FAs) combined with cortisol-stimulated appetite and excess caloric intake. Central and facial adipose depots have higher glucocorticoid receptor density and distinct responses that favor fat re-accumulation. This produces the characteristic central obesity, buffalo hump, and moon facies alongside thin, wasted extremities. The other choices attribute redistribution to mechanisms that are incorrect or oversimplified.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cushing syndrome body habitus — central obesity + buffalo hump + moon facies + purple striae + thin extremities — is a classic board vignette trigger. Purple striae (vs. white striae of simple obesity) reflect cortisol-induced protein catabolism in skin collagen. COMLEX: Viscerosomatic reflexes from the adrenal region project to the T8–T10 paraspinal muscles (OMM atlas Ch 15; sympathetic T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5); Chapman reflex for the adrenals (course chart): anterior, about 2.5 in above the kidney point, lateral and superior to the umbilicus at 11 and 1 o'clock; posterior, the T11–T12 intertransverse spaces (An Osteopathic Approach to Diagnosis and Treatment Ch 22: between the T11 spinous and T12 transverse processes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome: excess cortisol disrupts glucose, protein, and fat homeostasis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe cortisol’s anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms.","batch":1,"stem":"Cortisol limits the extent of tissue destruction at sites of acute inflammation by stabilizing which cellular structure, thereby preventing the release of enzymes that would otherwise perpetuate tissue damage?","choices":{"D":"Lysosomal membranes, preventing the release of hydrolytic and proteolytic enzymes stored in lysosomes that would otherwise be released into inflamed tissue, amplifying local destruction","A":"Mitochondrial outer membranes, preventing cytochrome c release into the cytoplasm and the subsequent activation of intrinsic caspase-9-mediated apoptosis pathways in inflamed cells","B":"Nuclear envelopes of resident macrophages, preventing transcription factor translocation into the nucleus and the subsequent upregulation of inflammatory cytokine gene expression","C":"Plasma membranes of circulating neutrophils, blocking membrane fusion events required for NADPH oxidase assembly and subsequent generation of reactive oxygen species during phagocytosis","E":"Endoplasmic reticulum membranes in dendritic cells, preventing unfolded protein response activation and IL-6 and IL-8 secretion triggered by misfolded antigen-presenting molecules"},"explanation":"One of cortisol's earliest described anti-inflammatory mechanisms is stabilization of lysosomal membranes, preventing the leakage of hydrolytic enzymes (proteases, lipases, phosphatases) that are stored inside lysosomes. At sites of inflammation these enzymes would normally be released and amplify tissue injury. Cortisol does not primarily act on mitochondrial or nuclear membranes for its anti-inflammatory effects, though it does reduce cytokine gene transcription via glucocorticoid response elements. The other choices describe mechanisms that are either unrelated to cortisol or represent a different class of anti-inflammatory action.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lysosomal membrane stabilization is one of four classic anti-inflammatory mechanisms of cortisol: (1) lysosomal stabilization, (2) decreased capillary permeability, (3) decreased WBC migration and phagocytosis, (4) suppression of prostaglandins and leukotrienes. COMLEX: Glucocorticoids are first-line for autoimmune and inflammatory conditions; OMT is complementary and can reduce corticosteroid dose needs in some inflammatory musculoskeletal conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 5 — Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of synthetic glucocorticoids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Cortisol does not primarily ac"}},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe cortisol’s anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms.","batch":1,"stem":"Glucocorticoids are a cornerstone of immunosuppressive therapy for solid organ transplantation. Which of the following mechanisms best explains their ability to prevent T-cell-mediated allograft rejection?","choices":{"B":"Glucocorticoids stimulate proliferation of FOXP3-expressing regulatory T cells, which actively suppress effector T cells through IL-10 and TGF-beta secretion at the site of the transplanted organ","A":"Glucocorticoids suppress lymphocyte proliferation and cause atrophy of lymphoid tissue, reducing the number and activity of T cells that would mount an immune response to donor MHC antigens","C":"Glucocorticoids block B-cell immunoglobulin class switching, preventing production of anti-donor IgG antibodies while preserving mucosal IgA responses that do not contribute to allograft rejection","D":"Glucocorticoids inhibit NK-cell cytotoxicity by downregulating NKp46 and NKG2D activating receptors, preventing direct killing of donor cells expressing stress ligands on MHC-negative endothelium","E":"Glucocorticoids upregulate PD-L1 expression on donor endothelial cells, inducing programmed T-cell exhaustion and long-term tolerance to alloantigens through the PD-1 checkpoint pathway"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids suppress the immune system primarily by decreasing lymphocyte proliferation and causing lymphoid tissue atrophy, particularly affecting T cells. This reduces the effector T-cell numbers available to recognize and attack donor MHC antigens in the transplanted organ. Cortisol also decreases IL-2 production, further impairing T-cell clonal expansion. While regulatory T cells and PD-L1 are active areas of transplant research, they are not the primary mechanism of glucocorticoid immunosuppression. The B-cell class switching and NK-cell pathways describe other immunological processes not central to glucocorticoid action.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid immunosuppression targets T cells primarily — decreased lymphocyte proliferation, decreased IL-2, atrophy of lymphoid tissue (thymus, spleen, lymph nodes). The clinical consequence is increased susceptibility to opportunistic infections (PCP, CMV, fungal) in transplant patients. COMLEX: Chronic steroid immunosuppression is a significant complication of long-term therapy; patients may present with atypical infections. Monitor for adrenal suppression if tapering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 5 — Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of synthetic glucocorticoids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"While regulatory T cells and P","C":"The B-cell class switching and","D":"The B-cell class switching and","E":"While regulatory T cells and P"}},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe cortisol’s anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following anti-inflammatory effects of cortisol most directly contributes to the reduction of edema at sites of acute inflammation?","choices":{"A":"Decreased capillary permeability, limiting the leakage of plasma proteins and fluid into the interstitial space at the site of inflammation and reducing tissue swelling","B":"Upregulation of aquaporin-1 channels in lymphatic endothelial cells, enhancing lymphatic drainage of excess interstitial fluid accumulating at sites of acute inflammatory reactions","C":"Activation of the kallikrein-kinin system to generate bradykinin antagonists that competitively bind bradykinin receptors, counteracting bradykinin-induced vasodilation and fluid extravasation","D":"Stimulation of sodium reabsorption in inflamed capillary endothelium through mineralocorticoid receptor activation, retaining fluid within the vascular compartment at inflammatory sites","E":"Direct inhibition of mast cell IgE receptor signaling, preventing histamine-induced arteriolar dilation and the subsequent increase in capillary hydrostatic pressure driving fluid filtration"},"explanation":"Cortisol decreases capillary permeability at sites of inflammation, which reduces the leakage of plasma proteins (including albumin) and fluid into the interstitial space, thereby limiting edema formation. This is one of cortisol's core anti-inflammatory mechanisms alongside lysosomal stabilization and suppression of arachidonic acid metabolites. Lymphatic upregulation and bradykinin antagonism are not mechanisms attributed to glucocorticoids. While mineralocorticoid effects on sodium occur with aldosterone, anti-inflammatory edema reduction by cortisol is primarily a permeability effect. Mast cell stabilization is more characteristic of cromolyn, not glucocorticoids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cortisol's anti-inflammatory effects span four categories: membrane stabilization (lysosomes), vascular effects (decreased permeability, decreased WBC margination), arachidonic acid pathway inhibition (blocks phospholipase A2 via lipocortin/annexin-1, reducing prostaglandins and leukotrienes), and immune suppression (T cells, lymphoid atrophy). COMLEX: In anaphylaxis, corticosteroids are given after epinephrine to prevent the biphasic late-phase reaction by blocking prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 5 — Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of synthetic glucocorticoids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Lymphatic upregulation and bra","C":"Lymphatic upregulation and bra","D":"While mineralocorticoid effect","E":"Mast cell stabilization is mor"}},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis including CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback, and circadian rhythm.","batch":1,"stem":"Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) is synthesized in which hypothalamic structure before traveling through the hypophysial portal circulation to stimulate ACTH release from the anterior pituitary?","choices":{"C":"Paraventricular nucleus, where parvocellular CRH neurons send axons to the median eminence and release CRH into portal blood for delivery to anterior pituitary corticotrophs","A":"Arcuate nucleus, where CRH co-localizes with dopamine and neuropeptide Y to coordinate neuroendocrine stress signaling with feeding behavior and energy homeostasis regulation","B":"Supraoptic nucleus, where CRH neurons are anatomically adjacent to ADH-producing neurons and share portal blood supply with the posterior pituitary neurohypophysial system","D":"Lateral hypothalamic area, where CRH neurons co-express orexin and coordinate HPA axis activation with the arousal and wakefulness circuits during physiologic stress responses","E":"Posterior pituitary, where CRH is stored in Herring bodies alongside ADH and oxytocin before being released directly into the systemic posterior pituitary venous drainage"},"explanation":"CRH is a 41-amino acid peptide synthesized by parvocellular neurons of the paraventricular nucleus (PVN) of the hypothalamus. These neurons project axons to the median eminence, where CRH is released into the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal blood. CRH travels through portal vessels to the anterior pituitary, where it binds CRH receptors on corticotroph cells and stimulates ACTH secretion. The arcuate nucleus primarily releases dopamine (inhibits prolactin) and GHRH. The supraoptic nucleus produces ADH and oxytocin for the posterior pituitary. CRH is not stored in the posterior pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PVN is the key hypothalamic nucleus for the HPA axis — CRH here, not in the arcuate or supraoptic nuclei. Remember: PVN also produces oxytocin and ADH (magnocellular neurons) but the parvocellular neurons make CRH and TRH. COMLEX: The hypothalamic PVN integrates limbic, brainstem, and cortical stress inputs; chronic psychosocial stress causes sustained HPA activation, connecting to osteopathic principles of somatic-visceral integration and allostatic load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis control of cortisol and aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The arcuate nucleus primarily ","E":"CRH is not stored in the poste"}},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis including CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback, and circadian rhythm.","batch":1,"stem":"ACTH stimulates cortisol biosynthesis in zona fasciculata cells by activating which intracellular signaling cascade?","choices":{"B":"Adenylyl cyclase leading to cAMP elevation and protein kinase A activation, which activates cholesterol desmolase (CYP11A1), the rate-limiting enzyme that converts cholesterol to pregnenolone","A":"Phospholipase C leading to IP3 and DAG generation, which activate protein kinase C to phosphorylate StAR protein and accelerate mitochondrial cholesterol transport for corticosteroid synthesis","C":"Receptor tyrosine kinase autophosphorylation leading to RAS and MAPK cascade activation, which upregulates transcription of CYP11B1 encoding the 11-beta-hydroxylase enzyme in zona fasciculata cells","D":"JAK2 and STAT5 signaling leading to STAT5 nuclear translocation, which directly transcribes genes encoding StAR and CYP11A1 for de novo induction of steroidogenic enzyme synthesis","E":"Gq protein activation leading to PLC-beta and DAG generation, which activates PKC to translocate the StAR protein to the inner mitochondrial membrane to initiate cortisol synthesis"},"explanation":"ACTH binds the MC2R (melanocortin-2 receptor), a Gs-coupled GPCR on zona fasciculata cells. This activates adenylyl cyclase, elevates intracellular cAMP, and activates protein kinase A. PKA phosphorylates cholesterol desmolase (CYP11A1), the rate-limiting enzyme that cleaves the cholesterol side chain to yield pregnenolone in the mitochondrial inner membrane. Long-term ACTH stimulation also induces StAR protein and causes adrenal hyperplasia. ACTH does not primarily signal through Gq/PLC, JAK-STAT, or receptor tyrosine kinase pathways. IP3/DAG (Gq) is the pathway for angiotensin II in aldosterone synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gs-cAMP-PKA is the ACTH signaling pathway. Compare: angiotensin II uses Gq-IP3-PKC for aldosterone synthesis. Cholesterol desmolase (CYP11A1) is the rate-limiting step in ALL steroid synthesis — shared by cortisol, aldosterone, and sex steroids. Ketoconazole inhibits CYP enzymes in the adrenal. COMLEX: ACTH-dependent Cushing (pituitary adenoma or ectopic) shows bilateral adrenal hyperplasia from chronic ACTH stimulation; ACTH-independent (adrenal adenoma) does not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis control of cortisol and aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"ACTH does not primarily signal","C":"ACTH does not primarily signal","D":"ACTH does not primarily signal","E":"ACTH does not primarily signal"}},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis including CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback, and circadian rhythm.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the normal circadian pattern of cortisol secretion in a healthy adult sleeping on a standard day-night schedule?","choices":{"A":"Cortisol peaks near 20 mcg/dL around 8 AM and declines through the day to a nadir near 5 mcg/dL around midnight, driven by a suprachiasmatic nucleus rhythm that entrains the CRH-ACTH axis","B":"Cortisol peaks at midnight when sleep onset and melatonin release occur, progressively rising overnight to prepare the body for waking, with the lowest levels observed in the early morning","C":"Cortisol is secreted in equal-amplitude pulses throughout the 24-hour period with no dominant peak or trough, as the HPA axis is regulated solely by physiologic stressors and not by time of day","D":"Cortisol peaks at noon after morning food intake, driven by postprandial glucose elevation that signals the hypothalamus via glucose sensors in the portal vein to release a CRH surge","E":"Cortisol peaks during REM sleep to facilitate hippocampal memory consolidation through glucocorticoid receptor activation, with minimum levels occurring in the late afternoon before bedtime"},"explanation":"Cortisol follows a well-defined circadian rhythm: it peaks in the early morning (~8 AM, approximately 20 mcg/dL) and declines progressively to a nadir around midnight (~5 mcg/dL). This rhythm is driven by the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) acting on the paraventricular nucleus to regulate CRH and ACTH pulsatility. The morning surge prepares the body for daily activity by mobilizing glucose, protein, and fat. This rhythm is disrupted in Cushing syndrome (elevated midnight cortisol is a sensitive diagnostic criterion) and by shift work, jet lag, and chronic stress. The other choices describe patterns that are incorrect or reversed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midnight cortisol is the most sensitive test for Cushing syndrome — loss of normal nadir is an early sign. Comparison: late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour urinary free cortisol, and 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test are the three first-line screening tools. COMLEX: Disruption of circadian cortisol rhythm by shift work and chronic stress contributes to metabolic syndrome, insulin resistance, and increased cardiovascular risk — connects to osteopathic preventive medicine and lifestyle counseling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis control of cortisol and aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain cortisol’s effects on carbohydrate metabolism (gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance), protein metabolism (catabolism), and fat metabolism (mobilization and redistribution).","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis presents for a 3-month follow-up. She was started on prednisone 20 mg daily 4 months ago to control a severe flare. Today, her fasting blood glucose is 168 mg/dL, up from 98 mg/dL at her last visit. She has no personal or family history of diabetes mellitus. Physical examination reveals moon facies, central obesity, and multiple easily bruised areas on her forearms. Methotrexate is her only other medication. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of her new hyperglycemia?","choices":{"C":"Prednisone activates hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes (PEPCK and glucose-6-phosphatase) to increase glucose output while reducing GLUT4 translocation in muscle and fat, producing insulin resistance","A":"Prednisone directly stimulates pancreatic alpha cells to secrete excess glucagon, driving hepatic glycogenolysis and preventing the normal postprandial suppression of endogenous glucose production","B":"Prednisone inhibits insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells by activating ATP-sensitive potassium channels that hyperpolarize the beta cell membrane and prevent calcium-triggered exocytosis","D":"Prednisone increases intestinal glucose absorption by upregulating SGLT1 cotransporter expression in duodenal enterocytes, elevating postprandial blood glucose to supraphysiologic levels","E":"Prednisone activates adipose triglyceride lipase to release glycerol as a gluconeogenic substrate, causing hyperglycemia through substrate-driven hepatic glucose production independent of insulin signaling"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids cause steroid-induced diabetes mellitus through two complementary mechanisms: (1) increased hepatic gluconeogenesis via induction of gluconeogenic enzymes (PEPCK, glucose-6-phosphatase) combined with amino acid mobilization from muscle catabolism as substrates; and (2) peripheral insulin resistance through impaired GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue. Insulin secretion is not directly blocked — in fact, insulin levels often rise compensatorily but cannot overcome the resistance. Glucagon stimulation, SGLT1 upregulation, and glycerol-driven gluconeogenesis are not the primary mechanisms of glucocorticoid-induced hyperglycemia. Her moon facies and easy bruising confirm systemic glucocorticoid excess.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroid-induced hyperglycemia is the most common complication of chronic glucocorticoid therapy. Glucose levels are highest in the afternoon (matching the postprandial prednisone peak) — a characteristic pattern that helps distinguish from type 2 diabetes. COMLEX: Patients on chronic steroids require blood glucose monitoring; NPH insulin is often used because its peak matches the afternoon steroid glucose surge. Also, taper steroids slowly to avoid iatrogenic adrenal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome: pathogenesis, clinical presentation, and management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Glucagon stimulation, SGLT1 up","B":"Insulin secretion is not direc","D":"Glucagon stimulation, SGLT1 up","E":"Glucagon stimulation, SGLT1 up"}},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain cortisol’s effects on carbohydrate metabolism (gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance), protein metabolism (catabolism), and fat metabolism (mobilization and redistribution).","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with Cushing disease is evaluated for progressive proximal muscle weakness developing over 8 months. He reports difficulty rising from a seated position and climbing stairs. Laboratory results include fasting glucose 148 mg/dL, potassium 3.2 mEq/L, and 24-hour urinary free cortisol markedly elevated. MRI of the thighs reveals diffuse bilateral reduction in muscle cross-sectional area. Serum creatine kinase is within normal limits. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of his muscle wasting?","choices":{"A":"Excess cortisol stimulates myostatin secretion from visceral adipocytes, which inhibits satellite cell activation and myoblast differentiation, preventing muscle fiber regeneration after normal turnover","B":"Excess cortisol blocks downstream signaling of the insulin and IGF-1 receptors in myocytes, preventing activation of the PI3K-AKT-mTOR anabolic pathway and halting normal protein accretion","C":"Excess cortisol decreases protein synthesis and increases protein catabolism in skeletal muscle via glucocorticoid receptor-mediated gene expression, mobilizing amino acids for gluconeogenesis","D":"Excess cortisol upregulates NF-kB-driven inflammatory cytokine production including TNF-alpha and IL-6, which directly induce ubiquitin-proteasome degradation of myofibrillar proteins in muscle","E":"Excess cortisol redistributes regional blood flow away from skeletal muscle toward splanchnic and central organs, causing chronic low-grade ischemic atrophy selectively in the proximal muscle groups"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoid-induced myopathy results from direct activation of glucocorticoid receptors in skeletal muscle, which decreases transcription of structural muscle proteins (decreased protein synthesis) while upregulating proteolytic pathways (increased catabolism). The liberated amino acids are directed to the liver for gluconeogenesis. The result is type IIb (fast-twitch glycolytic) fiber atrophy and proximal muscle weakness — characteristically with a normal CK (unlike inflammatory myopathies). This distinguishes Cushing myopathy from polymyositis or inclusion body myositis. Blocking IGF-1/mTOR, myostatin, or blood flow redistribution are not the primary mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cushing proximal myopathy has a normal CK — a high-yield differentiator from polymyositis (elevated CK, inflammatory infiltrates) and hypothyroid myopathy (elevated CK, pseudohypertrophy). The classic board scenario: proximal weakness + moon facies + striae + hyperglycemia + low CK = Cushing syndrome. COMLEX: Differentiate steroid myopathy (proximal, normal CK, no inflammation) from autoimmune myositis before prescribing OMT involving high-load muscle techniques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome: pathogenesis, clinical presentation, and management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Blocking IGF-1/mTOR, myostatin","B":"Blocking IGF-1/mTOR, myostatin","E":"Blocking IGF-1/mTOR, myostatin"}},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain cortisol’s effects on carbohydrate metabolism (gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance), protein metabolism (catabolism), and fat metabolism (mobilization and redistribution).","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with Cushing syndrome from an adrenal adenoma has proximal weakness, thin skin, and loss of muscle mass, and her plasma amino acid levels are elevated. Metabolic studies show that while muscle protein stores fall, hepatic protein content and several liver-made plasma proteins increase. Which mechanism best explains this difference between muscle and liver?","choices":{"D":"Cortisol increases amino acid transport into hepatocytes and the liver enzymes for protein synthesis, while it reduces amino acid uptake into muscle","A":"Cortisol blocks hepatic gluconeogenesis, so amino acids that would be turned into glucose are diverted into liver and plasma protein synthesis","B":"Cortisol suppresses lysosomal protein breakdown in hepatocytes, while muscle loses protein mainly from disuse caused by the proximal weakness","C":"Cortisol stimulates insulin-dependent amino acid uptake in the liver, because the high insulin of insulin resistance acts on the liver but not muscle","E":"Cortisol excess raises hepatic IGF-1 output, which drives protein synthesis in hepatocytes while muscle becomes resistant to IGF-1 signaling"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 78) states that cortisol reduces protein stores in essentially all cells except those of the liver, through decreased protein synthesis and increased catabolism, especially in muscle and lymphoid tissue. Concurrently, liver proteins increase, and the plasma proteins produced by the liver also increase; this may result from an effect of cortisol to enhance amino acid transport into liver cells, but not most other cells, and to enhance the liver enzymes required for protein synthesis. Cortisol depresses amino acid transport into muscle, so plasma amino acids rise and are used by the liver for deamination, protein synthesis, and gluconeogenesis. Cortisol increases gluconeogenesis 6- to 10-fold rather than blocking it. Disuse is not the mechanism of protein loss described. The liver effect is not attributed to insulin or IGF-1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cortisol is catabolic in muscle and lymphoid tissue but anabolic in liver: ↑ hepatic and plasma proteins, ↑ plasma amino acids, ↑ gluconeogenesis (Guyton Ch 78). COMLEX: With great cortisol excess, muscles can become so weak that the patient cannot rise from a squat (Guyton Ch 78).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome: pathogenesis, clinical presentation, and management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Disuse is not the mechanism of","C":"The liver effect is not attrib","E":"The liver effect is not attrib"}},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe cortisol’s anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after being stung by a bee. She develops diffuse urticaria, facial angioedema, wheezing, and hypotension within 10 minutes. Epinephrine 0.3 mg IM, IV normal saline, and diphenhydramine are administered. Methylprednisolone 125 mg IV is also given. The attending explains that corticosteroids are included to prevent a delayed complication. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which corticosteroids provide benefit in this setting?","choices":{"E":"Corticosteroids inhibit phospholipase A2 via annexin-1 induction, blocking arachidonic acid release and suppressing the prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis that drives late-phase inflammation","A":"Corticosteroids stabilize mast cell plasma membranes to immediately block IgE receptor crosslinking, halting ongoing histamine and tryptase release within minutes of intravenous administration","B":"Corticosteroids competitively inhibit histamine at H1 receptors on bronchial smooth muscle and vascular endothelium, providing antihistamine effects complementary to and stronger than diphenhydramine","C":"Corticosteroids activate alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on arteriolar smooth muscle, producing rapid vasoconstriction that counteracts histamine-mediated vasodilation and restores systemic vascular resistance","D":"Corticosteroids upregulate beta-2 adrenergic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle within minutes, sensitizing the airway to epinephrine and providing immediate bronchodilation superior to inhaled albuterol"},"explanation":"Corticosteroids in anaphylaxis target the late-phase response, not the immediate reaction. They induce annexin-1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2, blocking arachidonic acid mobilization from membrane phospholipids. Without arachidonic acid as substrate, synthesis of prostaglandins (via COX) and leukotrienes (via 5-LOX) is suppressed. This prevents the biphasic anaphylaxis reaction (late recurrence) by attenuating late-phase inflammation. Corticosteroids do not act immediately on mast cells, do not block H1 receptors, and do not act as direct vasoconstrictors. Receptor upregulation takes hours, not minutes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The order of priority in anaphylaxis treatment: (1) epinephrine (immediate, life-saving), (2) antihistamines (H1 and H2 block), (3) corticosteroids (delayed-onset, prevent biphasic reaction). Corticosteroids act through gene transcription over hours — their benefit in anaphylaxis is entirely for the late phase. COMLEX: Epinephrine auto-injector education is a critical patient safety measure in anaphylaxis; OMT has no role in acute anaphylaxis management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Corticosteroids do not act imm","B":"Corticosteroids do not act imm","C":"Corticosteroids do not act imm","D":"Receptor upregulation takes ho"}},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe cortisol’s anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman receiving chronic prednisone therapy for polymyalgia rheumatica has routine labs drawn. CBC differential shows: neutrophils 87%, lymphocytes 6%, monocytes 5%, eosinophils 0.5%, basophils 1.5%. Her pre-steroid CBC showed lymphocytes 27% and eosinophils 4%. She has no fever or lymphadenopathy. Which of the following best explains her current lymphocyte and eosinophil counts?","choices":{"C":"Glucocorticoids suppress lymphocyte proliferation, cause lymphoid atrophy, and shift lymphocytes and eosinophils out of the blood into tissues; lymphopenia and eosinopenia are characteristic of glucocorticoid excess","A":"Glucocorticoids stimulate granulocyte-colony stimulating factor production, driving the bone marrow to preferentially produce neutrophils, causing a dilutional reduction in lymphocyte and eosinophil percentages without absolute cell loss","B":"Glucocorticoids induce apoptosis selectively in myeloid progenitors of the lymphoid and eosinophil lineages in the bone marrow, while sparing the neutrophil progenitor compartment from apoptosis","D":"Glucocorticoids induce IgG autoantibodies against lymphocytes and eosinophils, so the antibody-coated cells are destroyed in the spleen and their total body numbers fall over several weeks","E":"Glucocorticoids enhance NK-cell-mediated cytotoxicity against circulating lymphocytes and eosinophils via perforin upregulation, selectively eliminating these cells from peripheral blood over several weeks"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids produce lymphopenia and eosinopenia through distinct mechanisms: lymphocytes undergo apoptosis and lymphoid tissue (thymus, spleen, lymph nodes) atrophies, reducing lymphocyte production and survival. Eosinophils are redistributed from peripheral blood to the bone marrow. Neutrophilia also occurs because glucocorticoids decrease adhesion molecule expression on endothelium, demarginating neutrophils from vessel walls into circulation. The clinical pearl is that lymphopenia and eosinopenia on a CBC differential should raise suspicion for glucocorticoid excess. Katzung adds that the fall in circulating lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils after a dose is mainly from their movement out of the blood into lymphoid tissue, so atrophy (Guyton) and redistribution (Katzung) both contribute. Antibody-mediated splenic destruction, NK-cell cytotoxicity, and selective bone marrow apoptosis are not the mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid effects on WBCs: neutrophils up; eosinophils, monocytes and lymphocytes down. The elevated neutrophil count from steroids is a demargination effect (not new production) — WBC rises within hours of a steroid dose. COMLEX: When evaluating a CBC in a hospitalized patient on steroids, a neutrophilia should not be automatically attributed to infection; check for recent steroid administration first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Antibody-mediated splenic dest","D":"Antibody-mediated splenic dest"}},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe cortisol’s anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man presents with hypertension (160/100 mmHg) and muscle cramps. Labs show serum potassium 2.9 mEq/L, serum sodium 148 mEq/L, and metabolic alkalosis. Plasma aldosterone is low-normal and plasma renin activity is suppressed. Serum cortisol is mildly elevated. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in HSD11B2, which encodes 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2. Which of the following best explains his hypertension and hypokalemia?","choices":{"B":"Without 11-beta-HSD2 to inactivate cortisol to cortisone in the kidney, cortisol occupies collecting duct mineralocorticoid receptors, which cannot tell it from aldosterone, causing sodium retention, potassium wasting, and hypertension","A":"Loss of 11-beta-HSD2 prevents conversion of cortisone to active cortisol in the adrenal zona fasciculata, causing compensatory ACTH-driven adrenal hyperplasia with excess synthesis of mineralocorticoid precursors including deoxycorticosterone","C":"Loss of 11-beta-HSD2 impairs cortisol catabolism in the liver and adipose tissue, elevating circulating cortisol to concentrations high enough to saturate all hepatic binding proteins and trigger secondary hypertension","D":"Loss of 11-beta-HSD2 prevents inactivation of aldosterone within the renal collecting duct, prolonging aldosterone-mineralocorticoid receptor binding well beyond its normal half-life and amplifying sodium reabsorption","E":"Loss of 11-beta-HSD2 blocks glucocorticoid-mediated suppression of renin gene expression in juxtaglomerular cells, driving RAAS overactivation that produces secondary hyperaldosteronism and potassium wasting"},"explanation":"11-beta-HSD2 normally protects mineralocorticoid receptors (MRs) in the kidney by converting active cortisol to inactive cortisone locally. Cortisol binds MRs with the same affinity as aldosterone — the only reason aldosterone normally dominates at the MR is the protective enzymatic inactivation of cortisol by 11-beta-HSD2. Without this enzyme, cortisol activates MRs in the collecting duct, causing sodium retention, potassium excretion, and hypertension despite normal or low aldosterone levels — this is called apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME). This is also the mechanism by which licorice (glycyrrhizin inhibits 11-beta-HSD2) causes hypertension. Note: 11-beta-HSD1 (not HSD2) activates cortisol from cortisone in liver and adipose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME) = HSD11B2 loss-of-function = low renin, low aldosterone, hypertension, hypokalemia. Compare to: Conn syndrome (primary aldosteronism: low renin, HIGH aldosterone), Cushing disease (high cortisol, normal or high aldosterone). Licorice ingestion causes identical physiology — a classic board trap. COMLEX: 11-beta-HSD1 (activates) vs 11-beta-HSD2 (inactivates) is a common source of confusion; remember the kidney has HSD2 to protect from cortisol, while liver/adipose have HSD1 for local cortisol activation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Note: 11-beta-HSD1 (not HSD2) ","C":"Note: 11-beta-HSD1 (not HSD2) "}},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis including CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback, and circadian rhythm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man sustains multiple rib fractures and a splenic laceration in a motor vehicle collision. He is conscious and in significant pain. Within 15 minutes of the trauma, his plasma cortisol rises from a baseline of 12 mcg/dL to over 50 mcg/dL. Despite this marked cortisol elevation that would normally suppress the HPA axis, cortisol continues to rise throughout his emergency surgery. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which physiologic stress overrides normal negative feedback?","choices":{"C":"Severe pain stimulates adrenal medullary epinephrine secretion, and epinephrine cross-reacts with glucocorticoid receptors in the zona fasciculata to amplify CYP11A1 gene transcription and cortisol synthesis","A":"Hemorrhage-induced hypovolemia directly stimulates cortisol secretion from zona fasciculata cells through adrenal arterial baroreceptors, bypassing the HPA axis and negative feedback regulation entirely","B":"Trauma, pain, and hemorrhage activate limbic and brainstem pathways that excite the paraventricular nucleus, generating a CRH surge that overrides cortisol negative feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary","D":"Trauma accelerates hepatic cortisol catabolism via 11-beta-HSD1 upregulation in response to sympathetic stimulation, rapidly lowering effective cortisol concentrations and releasing the HPA axis from feedback inhibition","E":"Prostaglandins released at the site of tissue trauma directly stimulate anterior pituitary corticotroph cells to secrete ACTH through a hypothalamus-independent mechanism that bypasses CRH regulation"},"explanation":"Physiologic stress overrides cortisol's normal negative feedback through neural pathways. Trauma, pain, hemorrhage, and surgery activate the amygdala and brainstem (locus coeruleus, nucleus tractus solitarius), which send excitatory noradrenergic and serotonergic inputs to CRH neurons in the paraventricular nucleus. This neural override is stronger than the inhibitory cortisol feedback signal at the hypothalamus and pituitary, driving a 10-20 fold cortisol surge within minutes. This stress response is adaptive — the resulting cortisol surge mobilizes glucose and amino acids, supports cardiovascular function, and suppresses immune activation during acute injury. The adrenal gland does not have autonomous baroreceptors, and epinephrine does not cross-react with glucocorticoid receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stress overrides negative feedback via limbic/brainstem inputs to the PVN — this is why patients undergoing surgery need adequate stress-dose steroids if they are on chronic exogenous glucocorticoids (their HPA axis is suppressed and cannot mount the normal stress response). Stress dosing is needed: during surgery or trauma the body may need 10 or more times the normal quantity of glucocorticoid (Guyton Ch 78). COMLEX: The HPA axis is a key connection between somatic stressors (tissue injury, pain) and visceral cortisol secretion — a direct example of the somatic-visceral integration principle in osteopathic medicine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis and control of cortisol and aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The adrenal gland does not hav"}},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis including CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback, and circadian rhythm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman presents with 30-pound weight gain, central obesity, purple abdominal striae, and new-onset hypertension over 18 months. Her 24-hour urinary free cortisol is markedly elevated. Plasma ACTH is 72 pg/mL (reference: 10-60 pg/mL). Low-dose dexamethasone suppression test (1 mg overnight) fails to suppress morning cortisol. High-dose dexamethasone suppression test (single 8-mg dose at 11 PM) suppresses her plasma cortisol by 62%. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"D":"Cushing disease from an ACTH-secreting pituitary corticotroph adenoma, which retains partial sensitivity to glucocorticoid negative feedback and is suppressed by high-dose but not low-dose dexamethasone","A":"Ectopic ACTH syndrome from a pulmonary carcinoid tumor, which would show elevated ACTH but characteristically does not suppress with high-dose dexamethasone because ectopic tumors lack functional glucocorticoid receptors","B":"Primary adrenal cortical adenoma autonomously secreting cortisol, in which ACTH would be suppressed below normal due to intact negative feedback from the elevated cortisol at both the hypothalamus and pituitary","C":"Pseudo-Cushing syndrome from chronic major depressive disorder, which produces mild hypercortisolism due to CRH hypersecretion that characteristically suppresses with low-dose but not high-dose dexamethasone","E":"Bilateral macronodular adrenal hyperplasia driven by aberrant receptor expression, which shows elevated ACTH from inadequate pituitary suppression and suppresses with both low-dose and high-dose dexamethasone"},"explanation":"The high-dose dexamethasone suppression test distinguishes pituitary-dependent (Cushing disease) from ectopic ACTH and adrenal-dependent Cushing syndrome. A pituitary ACTH adenoma retains a partially intact set-point for glucocorticoid negative feedback — it is resistant to physiologic cortisol concentrations (hence the disease) but can be suppressed by supraphysiologic dexamethasone (high-dose). Ectopic ACTH sources (lung carcinoid, small cell lung cancer) typically lack glucocorticoid receptors and do not suppress. Primary adrenal adenomas are ACTH-independent and would show suppressed (not elevated) ACTH. Pseudo-Cushing suppresses with low-dose dexamethasone. The elevated ACTH in this patient rules out primary adrenal pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dexamethasone suppression test algorithm: (1) Elevated cortisol + elevated ACTH = ACTH-dependent; (2) High-dose suppresses = pituitary (Cushing disease, most common cause overall); (3) High-dose does NOT suppress = ectopic ACTH. Primary adrenal = elevated cortisol + LOW ACTH. Cushing disease is responsible for the majority of endogenous Cushing syndrome (FA). COMLEX: MRI of the pituitary (sella turcica) is the next step after biochemical confirmation of Cushing disease; inferior petrosal sinus sampling confirms pituitary source if MRI is negative.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis and control of cortisol and aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Primary adrenal adenomas are A","C":"Pseudo-Cushing suppresses with"}},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis including CRH, ACTH, cortisol secretion, negative feedback, and circadian rhythm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) is evaluated for progressive darkening of his skin over 9 months. He reports hyperpigmentation of his buccal mucosa, knuckles, surgical scars, and skin creases. Vitals are notable for blood pressure 94/60 mmHg. Labs show sodium 128 mEq/L, potassium 5.8 mEq/L, plasma cortisol undetectable, and plasma ACTH markedly elevated at 950 pg/mL. Which of the following best explains his hyperpigmentation?","choices":{"E":"Loss of cortisol negative feedback causes excess POMC synthesis in anterior pituitary corticotrophs; POMC is cleaved into ACTH and other fragments including MSH-related peptides, and the elevated ACTH shares its N-terminal amino acid sequence with alpha-MSH, directly stimulating melanocortin-1 receptors on melanocytes to increase melanin production","A":"Elevated ACTH directly stimulates adrenal cortical cells to produce large quantities of corticosteroid precursors such as 17-hydroxyprogesterone, which act as agonists at skin melanocortin receptors to drive pigmentation","B":"Loss of cortisol removes its direct inhibitory effect on DOPA oxidase in epidermal melanocytes, permitting unregulated tyrosine-to-melanin conversion independent of ACTH or MSH receptor activation","C":"Chronic hyponatremia and hyperkalemia alter the cellular osmotic environment of melanocytes, activating mechanosensitive ion channels that trigger constitutive melanin synthesis in the basal epidermal layer","D":"Aldosterone deficiency causes severe volume depletion that activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and elevated angiotensin II cross-reacts with melanocortin-2 receptors on skin melanocytes to drive pigmentation"},"explanation":"In primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), the absence of cortisol removes negative feedback at the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, driving markedly elevated POMC gene expression in corticotroph cells. POMC is cleaved by prohormone convertase 1 into ACTH and beta-lipotropin. ACTH shares part of its sequence with alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH) and can directly bind melanocortin-1 receptors (MC1R) on melanocytes, stimulating tyrosinase and increasing melanin production. Additionally, beta-lipotropin is further cleaved to produce gamma-MSH and beta-endorphin in other tissues. This explains why hyperpigmentation is a feature of primary (not secondary) adrenal insufficiency — secondary disease suppresses ACTH. The other choices describe mechanisms that are not supported by the physiology of cortisol or POMC processing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperpigmentation in Addison disease = primary adrenal insufficiency with high ACTH (shares sequence with MSH). Secondary adrenal insufficiency (pituitary failure) has LOW ACTH and NO hyperpigmentation — a key distinguishing feature. Classic Addison presentation: fatigue, weight loss, postural hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hyperpigmentation of buccal mucosa and creases. COMLEX: Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (bilateral adrenal hemorrhage in meningococcemia) = acute adrenal crisis without prior hyperpigmentation; viscerosomatic reflex levels are T8–T10 for the adrenal (OMM atlas Ch 15; sympathetic T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5) and T10–T11 for the kidney (OMM course map; T9–L1 per the OMM atlas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis and control of cortisol and aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"}],"19":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the adrenal medulla as a modified sympathetic ganglion and identify epinephrine as its primary secretory product.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the developmental origin and neural classification of adrenal medullary chromaffin cells?","choices":{"A":"Modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons derived from neural crest, innervated directly by preganglionic cholinergic splanchnic fibers, and releasing catecholamines into the circulation without a prior synapse in a peripheral ganglion","B":"Modified preganglionic parasympathetic neurons derived from the ventral neural tube, innervated by postganglionic adrenergic fibers, and capable of releasing acetylcholine into the perimedullary sinusoids during states of heightened parasympathetic activity","C":"Mesoderm-derived steroidogenic cells structurally analogous to zona reticularis cells, regulated by adrenocorticotropic hormone from the anterior pituitary, and capable of synthesizing aldosterone in response to states of intravascular volume depletion","D":"Neural crest-derived postganglionic parasympathetic neurons innervated by preganglionic fibers from the vagus nerve, capable of releasing both epinephrine and acetylcholine depending on the magnitude of the sympathetic activation signal","E":"Ectoderm-derived neuroendocrine cells that receive direct innervation from hypothalamic neurons via the dorsal vagal complex, capable of modulating glucocorticoid and catecholamine output in coordinated response to circadian pacemaker signals"},"explanation":"Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla are derived from neural crest cells and are functionally equivalent to modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons. Uniquely, preganglionic cholinergic fibers traveling through the splanchnic nerves innervate them directly without first synapsing in a peripheral ganglion, allowing mass catecholamine release into the bloodstream. This anatomical arrangement means the adrenal medulla can amplify sympathetic responses to all tissues, including those without direct sympathetic innervation. Aldosterone synthesis occurs in the zona glomerulosa under angiotensin II and hyperkalemia control, not in medullary chromaffin cells. Parasympathetic innervation does not drive medullary secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The thoracic splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic fibers (T10–L1 for the suprarenal gland per Moore Ch 5) that bypass peripheral ganglia and synapse directly on chromaffin cells, making the adrenal medulla a \"displaced ganglion.\" COMLEX: Viscerosomatic reflex levels for the adrenal are T8–T10 (OMM atlas Ch 15, which lists its sympathetic supply as T7–T11). Chapman reflex for the adrenals (course chart): anterior, about 2.5 in above the kidney point, lateral and superior to the umbilicus at 11 and 1 o'clock; posterior, the T11–T12 intertransverse spaces (An Osteopathic Approach to Diagnosis and Treatment Ch 22: between the T11 spinous and T12 transverse processes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zones and medulla as the functionally distinct neuroendocrine core","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Parasympathetic innervation do","C":"Aldosterone synthesis occurs i","D":"Parasympathetic innervation do"}},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the adrenal medulla as a modified sympathetic ganglion and identify epinephrine as its primary secretory product.","batch":1,"stem":"Under resting physiologic conditions, which of the following best characterizes the ratio and enzymatic basis of catecholamine secretion by the adrenal medulla?","choices":{"D":"Epinephrine and norepinephrine are secreted in approximately equal proportions because both catecholamines are produced by separate but equally abundant chromaffin cell populations that respond to identical preganglionic stimuli at the same activation threshold","A":"Epinephrine constitutes approximately 80% of adrenal medullary output because most chromaffin cells express phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase, an enzyme induced by high local cortisol concentrations delivered in venous blood from the adrenal cortex","B":"Norepinephrine constitutes approximately 80% of adrenal medullary secretion because the majority of chromaffin cells lack phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase and therefore cannot convert norepinephrine to epinephrine via N-methylation","C":"Dopamine constitutes the predominant catecholamine secreted by the adrenal medulla because most chromaffin cells express only dopamine beta-hydroxylase and lack the enzymatic machinery to complete sequential synthesis to norepinephrine and then epinephrine","E":"Norepinephrine and dopamine are secreted in roughly equal proportions because splanchnic nerve stimulation activates two distinct chromaffin cell subtypes that each produce one of these catecholamines as their principal secretory product"},"explanation":"The adrenal medulla secretes roughly 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine because most chromaffin cells express PNMT, the enzyme that methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine. PNMT expression is maintained by high intra-adrenal cortisol concentrations delivered from the adrenal cortex through the centripetal venous drainage. This cortisol dependence explains why adrenal cortical atrophy can reduce epinephrine synthesis. Postganglionic sympathetic nerves release predominantly norepinephrine, so the adrenal medulla is the primary source of circulating epinephrine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PNMT is the key enzymatic distinction between adrenal medullary chromaffin cells and peripheral sympathetic neurons. Cortisol induces PNMT, which is why exogenous corticosteroid withdrawal can impair epinephrine synthesis. COMLEX: The cortisol-PNMT axis connects adrenal cortex physiology directly to medullary function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zones and medulla as the functionally distinct neuroendocrine core","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the adrenal medulla as a modified sympathetic ganglion and identify epinephrine as its primary secretory product.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why circulating adrenal medullary catecholamines produce physiologic effects lasting 2 to 4 minutes, while catecholamines released at a sympathetic synapse dissipate in less than one second?","choices":{"E":"Catecholamines enter the systemic circulation and distribute throughout the body including tissues without direct sympathetic innervation, and are cleared only through hepatic and renal metabolism, producing effects much longer than synaptic catecholamines that are immediately inactivated by neuronal reuptake and local enzymatic degradation","A":"Adrenal medullary chromaffin cells release catecholamines in a pulsatile fashion with multiple successive bursts, sustaining plasma concentrations for several minutes after the initial sympathetic nerve signal activates secretory vesicle exocytosis","B":"Catecholamines released by the adrenal medulla bind to high-affinity serum carrier proteins that protect them from enzymatic degradation by monoamine oxidase and catechol-O-methyltransferase in peripheral vascular endothelium and liver","C":"The adrenal medulla secretes a chemically distinct isoform of epinephrine with a modified catechol ring that cannot be degraded by the standard catecholamine-metabolizing enzymes present at high concentrations in the hepatic and renal cortex","D":"Catecholamines secreted by the adrenal medulla are stored in receptor-coated endosomes within target organ cells, from which they are released slowly over several minutes by receptor-mediated exocytosis independent of plasma concentration"},"explanation":"Adrenal medullary catecholamines enter the bloodstream and circulate systemically, reaching all tissues including those lacking direct sympathetic innervation such as platelets and circulating leukocytes. Clearance occurs through hepatic MAO and COMT metabolism and renal excretion; Guyton Ch 61 says the hormones are removed from the blood over 2 to 4 minutes, so their effects last 5 to 10 times as long as direct stimulation (their plasma half-life itself is only 10 to 15 seconds, Guyton Ch 75). In contrast, norepinephrine at a sympathetic synapse is cleared within milliseconds by presynaptic reuptake via norepinephrine transporter and local COMT degradation. This systemic distribution is why adrenal medullary stimulation mimics whole-body sympathetic activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COMT and MAO are the two key catecholamine-metabolizing enzymes; their products are metanephrines and VMA, which are measured in urine to diagnose pheochromocytoma. COMLEX: The prolonged systemic effect of circulating catecholamines underpins the physiologic rationale for measuring 24-hour urine collections in screening protocols.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zones and medulla as the functionally distinct neuroendocrine core","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the receptor selectivity and physiologic effects of epinephrine versus norepinephrine.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary receptor selectivity and predominant hemodynamic effect of norepinephrine compared to epinephrine at physiologic plasma concentrations?","choices":{"C":"Norepinephrine preferentially activates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, causing vasoconstriction of most vascular beds and a marked increase in total peripheral resistance that raises both systolic and diastolic blood pressure significantly","A":"Norepinephrine preferentially activates beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors, increasing heart rate and causing vasodilation in skeletal muscle vasculature, thereby producing a higher cardiac output than epinephrine at equivalent plasma concentrations","B":"Norepinephrine preferentially activates beta-1 receptors primarily increasing cardiac contractility with minimal effects on vascular tone, while epinephrine causes the more profound peripheral vasoconstriction through selective alpha-1 receptor activation","D":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine have identical receptor binding affinities but norepinephrine produces greater hemodynamic effects because it is secreted in larger total quantities from both the adrenal medulla and postganglionic sympathetic terminals","E":"Norepinephrine preferentially activates presynaptic alpha-2 receptors, reducing further norepinephrine release from sympathetic terminals and causing a paradoxical decrease in total peripheral resistance at high sustained plasma concentrations"},"explanation":"Norepinephrine has relatively greater alpha-1 receptor activity compared to epinephrine, producing potent systemic vasoconstriction and elevation of total peripheral resistance. This raises both systolic and diastolic blood pressure, which in turn triggers baroreceptor-mediated reflex bradycardia. Epinephrine activates both alpha and beta receptors approximately equally; its beta-2 activity in skeletal muscle vasculature causes some vasodilation that partially offsets its vasoconstrictor effect, resulting in a rise in systolic but sometimes a fall in diastolic blood pressure. Epinephrine produces 5 to 10 times greater metabolic stimulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board distinction: NE raises systolic AND diastolic BP with reflex bradycardia; Epi raises systolic but may lower diastolic due to beta-2 vasodilation, with tachycardia. COMLEX: This receptor selectivity explains why alpha blockade is required before beta blockade in pheochromocytoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — Aldosterone and cortisol receptor mechanisms contrast with adrenergic receptor selectivity of catecholamines","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the receptor selectivity and physiologic effects of epinephrine versus norepinephrine.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why epinephrine produces 5 to 10 times greater stimulation of metabolic rate than norepinephrine at equivalent plasma concentrations?","choices":{"E":"Epinephrine activates both alpha and beta adrenergic receptors, including beta-2 receptors in liver and skeletal muscle, promoting glycogenolysis, lipolysis, and thermogenesis more broadly than norepinephrine, which acts mainly on alpha-1 receptors","A":"Epinephrine selectively activates alpha-1 receptors on hepatocytes that directly stimulate glucose-6-phosphatase, releasing stored glycogen into the bloodstream regardless of concurrent norepinephrine activity or basal insulin concentrations","B":"Epinephrine blocks insulin secretion through presynaptic alpha-2 receptor activation on pancreatic beta cells, preventing glucose uptake by peripheral tissues and thereby causing a larger and more sustained rise in blood glucose than norepinephrine","C":"Epinephrine primarily activates hypothalamic dopaminergic receptors, stimulating the release of growth hormone and thyroid-stimulating hormone, which amplify the metabolic response through downstream endocrine cascades that take several hours to manifest","D":"Epinephrine preferentially binds to glucocorticoid receptors in the hepatocyte nucleus, directly upregulating transcription of phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase and other gluconeogenic rate-limiting enzymes more effectively than norepinephrine"},"explanation":"Epinephrine produces far greater metabolic stimulation than norepinephrine primarily because of its potent beta-2 receptor activation in liver and skeletal muscle, which drives glycogenolysis, and in adipose tissue, which drives lipolysis. Beta-2 activation also increases thermogenesis. Norepinephrine has relatively weak beta-2 activity compared to its alpha-1 selectivity, so its contribution to metabolic rate is smaller. This metabolic difference is clinically relevant in stress responses and is why epinephrine is the catecholamine of choice in anaphylaxis and cardiac arrest.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epi is the classic \"fight or flight\" hormone because it mobilizes glucose (glycogenolysis via beta-2) and fats (lipolysis) while simultaneously supporting cardiac output. NE is more of a pure vasopressor. COMLEX: Understanding this distinction helps explain why exogenous epinephrine rather than norepinephrine is used for anaphylaxis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — Aldosterone and cortisol receptor mechanisms contrast with adrenergic receptor selectivity of catecholamines","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the receptor selectivity and physiologic effects of epinephrine versus norepinephrine.","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmacologist compares two catecholamines. Drug X significantly increases heart rate and cardiac output but causes only a modest rise in mean arterial pressure with a slight decrease in diastolic blood pressure. Drug Y markedly elevates both systolic and diastolic blood pressure with a reflex decrease in heart rate. Which of the following best identifies Drug X and Drug Y?","choices":{"A":"Drug X is epinephrine and Drug Y is norepinephrine, because epinephrine's beta-2 activation in skeletal muscle vasculature partially offsets its vasoconstriction, while norepinephrine's predominantly alpha-1 stimulation raises total peripheral resistance and triggers reflex bradycardia","B":"Drug X is norepinephrine and Drug Y is epinephrine, because norepinephrine's selective beta-1 receptor activation in the heart increases cardiac output with minimal peripheral vasoconstriction, while epinephrine causes profound reflex bradycardia through potent peripheral alpha-1 vasoconstriction","C":"Drug X is dopamine and Drug Y is epinephrine, because low-dose dopamine preferentially stimulates beta-1 receptors in the heart to increase cardiac output without peripheral vasoconstriction, while epinephrine acts exclusively through alpha-1 receptors at all doses","D":"Drug X is epinephrine and Drug Y is dopamine, because epinephrine's powerful beta-1 cardiac stimulation produces tachycardia while high-dose dopamine's selective alpha-1 activation raises peripheral resistance without affecting sinus node automaticity","E":"Drug X is phenylephrine and Drug Y is norepinephrine, because phenylephrine increases cardiac output through selective beta-2 receptor stimulation while norepinephrine's potent alpha-1 activation raises systemic vascular resistance triggering baroreceptor-mediated reflex bradycardia"},"explanation":"Epinephrine (Drug X) increases heart rate and cardiac output through beta-1 and beta-2 cardiac stimulation. Its beta-2 vasodilatory effect in skeletal muscle partially offsets vasoconstriction, so mean arterial pressure rises modestly and diastolic blood pressure may actually fall. Norepinephrine (Drug Y) is predominantly alpha-1, causing marked vasoconstriction, elevating both systolic and diastolic pressures, and triggering baroreceptor-mediated reflex bradycardia. Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist that does not increase cardiac output; dopamine has dose-dependent receptor selectivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The hemodynamic profiles of Epi vs NE are classic board content. Remember: NE = alpha dominant = reflex bradycardia; Epi = mixed alpha/beta = tachycardia + variable BP. COMLEX: This distinction is tested in the context of pheochromocytoma secretory patterns and anaphylaxis pharmacology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — Aldosterone and cortisol receptor mechanisms contrast with adrenergic receptor selectivity of catecholamines","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-","D":"Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-"}},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Review the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma.","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman presents with episodic headaches, palpitations, and profuse diaphoresis occurring several times weekly, each lasting 15 to 20 minutes. Blood pressure during an episode is 220/130 mmHg. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial diagnostic study?","choices":{"B":"24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines and catecholamines or plasma free metanephrines, because episodic severe hypertension with headache, diaphoresis, and palpitations is the classic presentation of a catecholamine-secreting tumor","A":"24-hour urine aldosterone and plasma renin activity, because the episodic nature of her hypertension and associated headaches are most consistent with primary hyperaldosteronism from a unilateral aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma","C":"Serum TSH and free T4, because episodic hypertension with palpitations, diaphoresis, and headache may represent thyroid storm from a hyperfunctioning thyroid nodule releasing excess thyroid hormone into the systemic circulation","D":"Overnight 1-mg dexamethasone suppression test, because central adiposity and episodic hypertension with headaches are characteristic findings of Cushing syndrome from an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma suppressing normally by exogenous glucocorticoids","E":"Plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio, because bilateral adrenal enlargement with episodic hypertension and headache most commonly results from bilateral adrenal hyperplasia producing excess mineralocorticoid that escapes normal regulatory feedback"},"explanation":"The classic triad of pheochromocytoma is episodic headache, diaphoresis, and palpitations with hypertension. The preferred initial test is 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines and catecholamines or plasma free metanephrines, which have high sensitivity. Metanephrines are stable metabolites produced constitutively by intratumoral catecholamine metabolism, making them more sensitive than measuring catecholamines directly. Thyroid storm can mimic this presentation but would show elevated free T4 and low TSH. Hyperaldosteronism causes sustained, not episodic, hypertension and does not produce this triad.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"5 Ps\" of pheochromocytoma: Pressure (HTN), Pain (headache), Perspiration, Palpitations, Pallor. Urine metanephrines are the most sensitive initial test. COMLEX: Also know the FA Rule of 10s: 10% malignant, 10% bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal, 10% calcify, 10% in children; germline mutations are far more common than 10% (30–40% per Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 6 — Pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma pathology, morphology, and management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Hyperaldosteronism causes sust"}},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Review the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a confirmed pheochromocytoma is scheduled for surgical resection in 2 weeks. Which of the following represents the correct sequence of preoperative pharmacologic management?","choices":{"D":"Begin an alpha-adrenergic blocker first to achieve blood pressure control and allow volume expansion, then add a beta-blocker only after alpha blockade is established, because beta blockade first leaves alpha stimulation unopposed and causes hypertensive crisis","A":"Begin a beta-1 selective blocker first to control tachycardia and palpitations, followed by an alpha-1 blocker to reduce blood pressure, because unopposed beta blockade prevents catecholamine-induced cardiac arrhythmias before surgery and reduces perioperative cardiac mortality","B":"Begin a calcium channel blocker first to reduce blood pressure and vasospasm risk, followed by a nonselective alpha-blocker to prevent intraoperative hypertensive surges, because calcium channel blockers are less likely than alpha blockers alone to precipitate paradoxical hypertension","C":"Begin metyrapone to suppress adrenal catecholamine synthesis followed by dose escalation of an alpha-blocker to control residual hypertension, because reducing catecholamine production before adrenergic blockade avoids the paradoxical hypertension caused by receptor upregulation","E":"Begin intravenous hydrocortisone to suppress adrenomedullary function through inhibition of PNMT expression, followed by nonselective adrenergic blockade, because glucocorticoid reduction lowers the catecholamine burden before surgical manipulation of the tumor"},"explanation":"The critical rule in pheochromocytoma management is alpha blockade before beta blockade. If a beta-blocker is given first, the remaining alpha-adrenergic receptors are left unopposed during catecholamine surges, causing severe vasoconstriction and hypertensive crisis. Alpha blockade first (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin) expands intravascular volume and controls blood pressure, after which beta blockade can be safely added to manage reflex tachycardia. Metyrapone inhibits cortisol synthesis, not catecholamine synthesis. Hydrocortisone would not lower catecholamine output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha FIRST, then beta. This sequence is one of the highest-yield management points for pheochromocytoma. Phenoxybenzamine is nonselective and irreversible; prazosin and doxazosin are selective alpha-1 and reversible. COMLEX: Adequate preoperative hydration and alpha blockade reduce perioperative mortality from intraoperative catecholamine storms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 6 — Pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma pathology, morphology, and management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"If a beta-blocker is given fir","C":"Metyrapone inhibits cortisol s","E":"Hydrocortisone would not lower"}},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Review the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following hereditary syndromes is most directly associated with pheochromocytoma arising from a RET proto-oncogene gain-of-function mutation?","choices":{"C":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A, characterized by pheochromocytoma, medullary thyroid carcinoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia due to constitutively activating RET mutations driving tyrosine kinase signaling in neural crest-derived tissues","A":"Neurofibromatosis type 1, characterized by pheochromocytoma arising from loss-of-function mutations in the NF1 tumor suppressor gene encoding neurofibromin on chromosome 17q along with cafe-au-lait macules and Lisch nodules","B":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome, characterized by pheochromocytoma arising from loss-of-function mutations in the VHL tumor suppressor gene, causing upregulation of HIF-1-alpha and creating a pseudohypoxia transcriptional phenotype in chromaffin cells","D":"Hereditary paraganglioma syndrome type 4, characterized by pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma arising from loss-of-function mutations in the SDHB gene encoding a subunit of the mitochondrial succinate dehydrogenase complex with high malignant potential","E":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome, characterized by pheochromocytoma arising from germline mutations in the TP53 tumor suppressor gene causing widespread neoplasia across multiple endocrine and non-endocrine tissues at early onset in affected individuals"},"explanation":"MEN2A (Sipple syndrome) is caused by gain-of-function germline mutations in RET, a receptor tyrosine kinase. This produces the triad of medullary thyroid carcinoma (virtually 100%), pheochromocytoma (40-50%), and parathyroid hyperplasia (10-20%). MEN2B has a similar mutation at a different RET site and adds mucosal neuromas and marfanoid habitus. All patients with medullary thyroid carcinoma should undergo RET testing. VHL and NF1 are loss-of-function tumor suppressor mutations, not proto-oncogene activating mutations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET mutations = gain-of-function proto-oncogene = MEN2A and MEN2B. Compare with MEN1 = loss-of-function of menin tumor suppressor. All MTC patients should have RET sequencing. COMLEX: MEN2A involves the adrenal medulla through pheochromocytoma (adrenal viscerosomatic level T8–T10, OMM atlas Ch 15; sympathetic T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 6 — Pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma pathology, morphology, and management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"VHL and NF1 are loss-of-functi","B":"VHL and NF1 are loss-of-functi"}},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the adrenal medulla as a modified sympathetic ganglion and identify epinephrine as its primary secretory product.","batch":2,"stem":"A first-year medical student dissects the abdominal cavity and identifies a small gland resting superior to the right kidney. His anatomy instructor states that the neural supply to the medulla of this gland is anatomically unique among endocrine organs. Which of the following best describes this unique feature?","choices":{"A":"Preganglionic sympathetic fibers traveling in the greater splanchnic nerves innervate the medullary chromaffin cells directly without first synapsing in a peripheral ganglion, because the chromaffin cells are equivalent to postganglionic neurons","B":"Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve innervate the medullary chromaffin cells directly, causing release of epinephrine during increased vagal tone states such as the postprandial period, deep sleep, and Valsalva maneuver","C":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the sacral outflow synapse on medullary chromaffin cells whose secretory output is therefore inversely correlated with sympathetic nervous system activation and directly proportional to parasympathetic tone","D":"The adrenal medulla receives no direct neural innervation and is regulated entirely by circulating adrenocorticotropic hormone released from the anterior pituitary in response to hypothalamic corticotropin-releasing hormone during physiologic stress","E":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the celiac ganglion synapse onto medullary chromaffin cells, which then release catecholamines in response to local norepinephrine stimulation rather than acetylcholine from preganglionic cholinergic inputs"},"explanation":"The adrenal medulla is unique because preganglionic cholinergic fibers from the thoracic splanchnic nerves (cord segments T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5; T7–T11 in the OMM atlas) pass through the celiac plexus without synapsing and innervate chromaffin cells directly. This is possible because chromaffin cells are developmentally modified postganglionic neurons and retain nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. This allows for rapid, coordinated mass release of catecholamines into the bloodstream during stress. The adrenal cortex, by contrast, is regulated hormonally by ACTH, not by direct neural innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chromaffin cells have nicotinic ACh receptors because they ARE postganglionic neurons that never fully differentiated into axon-bearing neurons. The splanchnic nerves are preganglionic. COMLEX: Adrenal sympathetic levels (T10–L1 per Moore; T7–T11, viscerosomatic T8–T10, per the OMM atlas) are tested in osteopathic somatic dysfunction questions. Chapman reflex for the adrenals (course chart): anterior, about 2.5 in above the kidney point, lateral and superior to the umbilicus at 11 and 1 o'clock; posterior, the T11–T12 intertransverse spaces (An Osteopathic Approach to Diagnosis and Treatment Ch 22: between the T11 spinous and T12 transverse processes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Adrenal medulla as modified sympathetic ganglion with direct preganglionic innervation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"The adrenal cortex, by contras"}},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the adrenal medulla as a modified sympathetic ganglion and identify epinephrine as its primary secretory product.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man undergoes bilateral adrenalectomy and is discharged on full glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement. Three months later, his heart rate and blood pressure rise normally during a treadmill test, and he has no dizziness on standing. Which of the following best explains why his sympathetic responses are largely preserved?","choices":{"E":"Direct sympathetic nerves still release norepinephrine onto the heart and vessels, and these pathways can perform almost all the duties shared with the medullae","A":"Postganglionic sympathetic nerve endings begin methylating their norepinephrine to epinephrine, restoring the circulating epinephrine that the medullae once supplied","B":"Adrenal medullary hormones act exclusively on tissues that lack direct sympathetic fibers, so innervated organs such as the heart never depended on them","C":"Loss of the medullae makes vascular smooth muscle two to four times more sensitive to norepinephrine, the same supersensitivity that follows a sympathectomy","D":"Preganglionic splanchnic fibers store epinephrine in their terminals and release it directly into the blood once the chromaffin cells have been removed"},"explanation":"During sympathetic activation the organs are stimulated in two ways: directly by sympathetic nerves and indirectly by the epinephrine and norepinephrine that the adrenal medullae release into the blood. The two mechanisms support each other, and in most cases either can substitute for the other. Loss of both medullae therefore usually has little effect on sympathetic function, because the direct pathways still perform almost all the necessary duties; this dual arrangement is a safety factor. The methylation step that turns about 80% of the norepinephrine into epinephrine takes place in the adrenal medulla, and postganglionic sympathetic endings go on secreting norepinephrine. Circulating medullary hormones reach all tissues, innervated or not; their added value is that they also reach the many cells that have no direct sympathetic fibers. Denervation supersensitivity develops after the sympathetic nerves to an organ are cut, not after the medullae are removed. Preganglionic fibers, including the splanchnic fibers that end on chromaffin cells, are cholinergic and do not store catecholamines.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion: cholinergic preganglionic splanchnic fibers end directly on chromaffin cells (modified postganglionic neurons), and the output is about 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine. COMLEX: After bilateral adrenalectomy the patient needs lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement; catecholamine replacement is not needed, because the direct sympathetic pathways take over almost all medullary duties.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Epinephrine as primary adrenal medullary product entering systemic circulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"C":"Denervation supersensitivity d"}},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the adrenal medulla as a modified sympathetic ganglion and identify epinephrine as its primary secretory product.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old trauma patient arrives in hemorrhagic shock with blood pressure 70/40 mmHg, heart rate 130 beats per minute, pallor, and diaphoresis. A medical student asks how the adrenal medulla contributes to the cardiovascular compensation observed. Which of the following best explains the adrenal medullary contribution?","choices":{"B":"Preganglionic splanchnic fibers activate chromaffin cells to release epinephrine and norepinephrine into the blood, amplifying sympathetic vasoconstriction and cardiac stimulation, even in organs lacking direct sympathetic innervation","A":"The adrenal medulla releases aldosterone directly in response to hemorrhage-induced renin release, causing rapid sodium retention and plasma volume expansion that maintains perfusion pressure to vital organs within minutes of blood loss","C":"The adrenal medulla releases corticotropin-releasing hormone into the adrenal portal circulation, which stimulates the anterior pituitary to produce ACTH and increase cortisol within minutes of the hemorrhagic insult","D":"The adrenal medulla is suppressed during hemorrhagic shock through baroreceptor-mediated withdrawal of sympathetic tone, limiting catecholamine release and allowing peripheral vasodilation to redistribute blood flow to vital organs","E":"Postganglionic adrenergic fibers from the celiac plexus stimulate adrenal medullary secretion through alpha-1 receptor activation on chromaffin cells, bypassing the hypothalamus and allowing immediate catecholamine release without central nervous system involvement"},"explanation":"In hemorrhagic shock, baroreceptor unloading activates the sympathetic nervous system, including splanchnic preganglionic outflow to chromaffin cells. The resulting epinephrine and norepinephrine surge produces tachycardia, increased contractility, and vasoconstriction of skin and visceral beds (explaining pallor and diaphoresis) while preserving flow to heart and brain. The adrenal medulla uniquely supports non-innervated tissues because catecholamines travel through the bloodstream. Aldosterone release from the zona glomerulosa occurs but takes hours to affect plasma volume, while catecholamine effects are immediate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: During hemorrhage, catecholamines act in seconds while RAAS takes minutes-to-hours and cortisol takes hours. The immediate cardiovascular compensation is catecholamine-mediated. COMLEX: Splanchnic preganglionic sympathetics (T10–L1 for the suprarenal gland per Moore Ch 5; T7–T11 per OMM atlas Ch 15) are the efferent limb for adrenomedullary activation in shock states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Adrenal medullary catecholamine release amplifying sympathetic stress response","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Aldosterone release from the z"}},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the receptor selectivity and physiologic effects of epinephrine versus norepinephrine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man is admitted for a hypertensive emergency with blood pressure 245/145 mmHg and heart rate 48 beats per minute. CT imaging shows a right adrenal mass and biochemical testing confirms a catecholamine-secreting tumor. Which of the following catecholamines is most likely predominating based on these hemodynamic findings?","choices":{"C":"Norepinephrine predominantly secreted by the tumor, causing severe hypertension through potent alpha-1-mediated systemic vasoconstriction, with baroreceptor-mediated reflex bradycardia in response to the rise in total peripheral resistance","A":"Epinephrine predominantly secreted by the tumor, causing hypertension and tachycardia through potent beta-1 adrenergic cardiac stimulation combined with beta-2-mediated skeletal muscle vasodilation that partially offsets the vasoconstrictor component","B":"Dopamine predominantly secreted by an extra-adrenal paraganglioma, causing hypertension through central dopaminergic receptor stimulation while simultaneously producing reflex bradycardia through direct inhibition of sinoatrial nodal automaticity","D":"Equal secretion of both epinephrine and norepinephrine by the tumor, with epinephrine's beta-2 vasodilatory effects canceling norepinephrine's alpha-1 vasoconstriction, producing an unusual hemodynamic profile with hypertension and simultaneous bradycardia","E":"Cortisol co-secreted with norepinephrine by the adrenal cortex adjacent to the tumor, causing hypertension through mineralocorticoid-mediated sodium retention rather than through catecholamine-mediated adrenergic receptor activation"},"explanation":"The combination of severe hypertension with reflex bradycardia points to predominantly norepinephrine secretion. Norepinephrine's potent alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises total peripheral resistance so dramatically that baroreceptors detect the elevated blood pressure and trigger vagally-mediated bradycardia. If epinephrine were predominating, beta-1 stimulation would produce tachycardia. Some pheochromocytomas secrete predominantly NE, particularly extra-adrenal paragangliomas. The urinary NE to Epi ratio can help characterize tumor secretory pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: If HTN + bradycardia = NE predominant (alpha-1, reflex brady). If HTN + tachycardia = Epi predominant (beta-1). Knowing this distinction helps identify secretory pattern from clinical findings. COMLEX: Extra-adrenal paragangliomas are more likely to secrete NE than adrenal pheochromocytomas, because they lack the cortisol-driven PNMT enzyme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — Differential receptor selectivity of NE versus epinephrine producing distinct hemodynamic profiles","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"If epinephrine were predominat"}},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the receptor selectivity and physiologic effects of epinephrine versus norepinephrine.","batch":2,"stem":"In an anesthetized-animal experiment, an intravenous bolus of epinephrine raises arterial pressure. After pretreatment with phentolamine, the same dose of epinephrine lowers arterial pressure, whereas norepinephrine given after phentolamine no longer raises pressure. Which of the following best explains why epinephrine now lowers arterial pressure?","choices":{"A":"Epinephrine is a much stronger beta-1 agonist than norepinephrine, so once alpha receptors are blocked its cardiac stimulation triggers a baroreflex fall in pressure","B":"Epinephrine also activates beta-2 receptors in skeletal muscle vessels, and alpha blockade unmasks this vasodilation, whereas norepinephrine has little beta-2 effect","C":"Epinephrine acts on presynaptic alpha-2 receptors that phentolamine leaves unblocked, so it suppresses norepinephrine release from vascular sympathetic nerves","D":"Epinephrine activates dopamine D1 receptors in renal and mesenteric arterioles, and their vasodilation becomes visible once vascular alpha-1 receptors are blocked","E":"With alpha receptors blocked, epinephrine acts on arteriolar beta-1 receptors, which relax vascular smooth muscle in the same way that they act on the heart"},"explanation":"This is epinephrine reversal. Epinephrine is an agonist at alpha-1, alpha-2, beta-1 and beta-2 receptors, with beta-1 and beta-2 about equal. Norepinephrine acts at alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors and at beta-1 receptors with potency similar to epinephrine, but it has relatively little effect on beta-2 receptors. Normally the alpha-mediated vasoconstriction of epinephrine outweighs its beta-2 dilation of skeletal muscle vessels. When phentolamine blocks the alpha receptors, the beta-2 vasodilation is unmasked and the pressor response becomes a depressor response; norepinephrine, with little beta-2 activity, simply loses its pressor effect. The two catecholamines are about equally potent at beta-1 receptors, so a beta-1-driven reflex cannot explain the difference. Phentolamine is a competitive antagonist at both alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors, so it does not spare alpha-2. Epinephrine is not a dopamine-receptor agonist, and vasodilation is a beta-2 effect; beta-1 receptors mediate cardioacceleration and increased myocardial strength.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epinephrine reversal: after an alpha blocker, epinephrine lowers blood pressure because its beta-2 vasodilation is unmasked, whereas norepinephrine (beta-1 much greater than beta-2) only loses its pressor effect. Receptor profiles: norepinephrine alpha-1 = alpha-2, beta-1 >> beta-2; epinephrine alpha-1 = alpha-2, beta-1 = beta-2. COMLEX: Alpha blockers remove sympathetic vasoconstriction and can cause orthostatic hypotension with reflex tachycardia, a practical concern during alpha blockade before pheochromocytoma surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — Epinephrine vs norepinephrine receptor profiles and differential hemodynamic effects","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The two catecholamines are abo","C":"Phentolamine is a competitive ","D":"Epinephrine is not a dopamine-","E":"Epinephrine is not a dopamine-"}},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the receptor selectivity and physiologic effects of epinephrine versus norepinephrine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with acute onset throat tightness, diffuse urticaria, wheezing, and blood pressure 72/40 mmHg after eating shellfish. Which of the following best explains why epinephrine is the drug of choice for this condition rather than norepinephrine?","choices":{"B":"Epinephrine's beta-2 activation in bronchial smooth muscle produces rapid bronchodilation, its alpha-1 activation reverses peripheral vasodilation, and its beta-1 activation increases cardiac output, addressing all three threats simultaneously","A":"Epinephrine acts exclusively through alpha-1 receptors to produce peripheral vasoconstriction that restores blood pressure and simultaneously inhibits mast cell degranulation by blocking IgE receptor cross-linking on circulating basophils","C":"Epinephrine preferentially activates muscarinic receptors in bronchial smooth muscle, reversing acetylcholine-mediated bronchoconstriction more effectively than norepinephrine, which lacks any activity at muscarinic receptor subtypes in the airway","D":"Norepinephrine would be equally effective but is not used because its half-life in plasma is 5 to 10 times longer than epinephrine, making it difficult to titrate the dose and creating risk of prolonged hypertension after anaphylaxis resolves","E":"Epinephrine directly inhibits the release of histamine and leukotrienes from mast cells by binding to alpha-2 presynaptic receptors on mast cell surfaces and reducing intracellular cAMP, thereby addressing the root cause of the allergic reaction"},"explanation":"Anaphylaxis involves three simultaneous threats: bronchospasm, vasodilation and hypotension, and cardiovascular compromise. Epinephrine addresses all three: beta-2 bronchodilation reverses airway obstruction, alpha-1 vasoconstriction restores blood pressure, and beta-1 increases heart rate and contractility. Norepinephrine lacks meaningful beta-2 activity, so it would not bronchodilate. Epinephrine also has some mast cell-stabilizing effects through beta-2 receptor increase in cAMP, but this is secondary to its adrenergic actions on the airway and cardiovascular system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epinephrine is the only drug that addresses all three components of anaphylaxis (bronchospasm, hypotension, cardiac depression). IM auto-injector into the outer thigh is first-line; IV only for refractory cases. COMLEX: Beta-2 bronchodilation through epinephrine connects to adrenal physiology and explains why exercise-induced epinephrine release can temporarily relieve asthma symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — Epinephrine alpha and beta receptor pharmacology applied to clinical management of anaphylaxis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Review the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a pheochromocytoma with liver and bone metastases and is not a candidate for surgery. Despite phenoxybenzamine and a beta-blocker, he still has frequent paroxysms. A second drug is added, and over the next 2 weeks his urinary metanephrines and normetanephrines fall by about half and the paroxysms become less frequent. He then develops a shuffling gait, cogwheel rigidity and drooling. Which action of the added drug best explains both the improvement and the new movement disorder?","choices":{"C":"Competitive inhibition of tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting step for dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine synthesis","A":"Irreversible covalent blockade of alpha receptors on vascular smooth muscle, extended to the alpha receptors of the basal ganglia","B":"Inhibition of dopamine beta-hydroxylase, which stops the conversion of dopamine to norepinephrine in the tumor and in the brain","D":"Antagonism of dopamine D2 receptors in the striatum and on the tumor cells, which suppresses exocytosis of stored catecholamines","E":"Inhibition of catechol-O-methyltransferase, which slows the breakdown of circulating catecholamines into their metanephrines"},"explanation":"The added drug is metyrosine, alpha-methyltyrosine. It is a competitive inhibitor of tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting step in the synthesis of dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine, so tumor catecholamine output and the urinary metabolites fall. Katzung notes that it is especially useful in symptomatic patients with inoperable or metastatic pheochromocytoma, and that because it enters the CNS it can cause extrapyramidal effects from reduced dopamine, which explains the parkinsonian signs. Irreversible covalent alpha blockade describes phenoxybenzamine, which he already takes; it attenuates catecholamine-induced vasoconstriction but does not lower catecholamine production or metanephrine excretion. Blocking dopamine beta-hydroxylase acts one step after dopamine is made, so it would not deplete striatal dopamine. Dopamine D2 antagonists such as the neuroleptics can cause extrapyramidal signs, but they do not reduce tumor catecholamine synthesis. Catechol-O-methyltransferase is the enzyme that forms the metanephrines; inhibiting it would lower metanephrines while raising circulating catecholamines, and it would not cause parkinsonism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metyrosine (alpha-methyltyrosine) blocks tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting step of catecholamine synthesis; it is used for inoperable or metastatic pheochromocytoma and can cause extrapyramidal effects because it also lowers brain dopamine. COMLEX: Phenoxybenzamine remains useful for chronic treatment of inoperable or metastatic disease, and beta-blockers are added only after alpha blockade is established.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Metyrosine blockade of tyrosine hydroxylase for inoperable or metastatic pheochromocytoma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Blocking dopamine beta-hydroxy"}},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Review the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man reports 6 months of pounding headaches, palpitations and sweating that come on during urination. Blood pressure during an episode is 210/120 mm Hg. Plasma free normetanephrine is markedly elevated, but CT of the abdomen shows two normal adrenal glands. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"B":"An iodine-123 thyroid uptake scan, because a hyperfunctioning thyroid nodule best explains attacks of palpitations and sweating","A":"An overnight 1-mg dexamethasone suppression test, because his attacks most likely come from autonomous adrenal cortisol secretion","C":"Cystoscopic biopsy of the bladder wall, because a tissue diagnosis should be made before any further imaging or drug treatment","D":"A 131I-MIBG scan, because this norepinephrine transporter substrate is taken up and concentrated by catecholamine-secreting cells","E":"A beta-blocker now and a repeat adrenal CT in 6 months, because a tumor too small to see on CT is unlikely to cause his symptoms"},"explanation":"The biochemistry already confirms a catecholamine-secreting tumor, and with normal adrenal glands it must be extra-adrenal, a paraganglioma. Robbins notes that urinary bladder paragangliomas can set off a paroxysm during micturition. Once the diagnosis is made biochemically, Katzung lists CT, MRI and scanning with 131I-meta-iodobenzylguanidine (MIBG) to localize the tumor; MIBG is a norepinephrine transporter substrate that tumor cells take up, so it finds the tumor wherever it lies. Biopsy is the wrong move: physical pressure and manipulation can release stored catecholamines and precipitate a hypertensive crisis, and the tumor is removed only after preoperative alpha blockade. A beta-blocker given before alpha blockade leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can raise blood pressure further. Radioactive iodine scanning images thyroid follicular cells and does not explain an elevated normetanephrine. A dexamethasone suppression test screens for cortisol excess, which produces sustained rather than paroxysmal findings and does not raise normetanephrine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diagnose pheochromocytoma or paraganglioma biochemically first (plasma or urine metanephrines and normetanephrines), then localize with CT or MRI and MIBG scintigraphy. FA Rule of 10s: about 10% are extra-adrenal, for example in the bladder wall or the organ of Zuckerkandl. COMLEX: Avoid biopsy or other manipulation of a suspected pheochromocytoma; start alpha blockade before surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Localizing a biochemically confirmed pheochromocytoma or paraganglioma with MIBG imaging","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Radioactive iodine scanning im","C":"Biopsy is the wrong move: phys","E":"A beta-blocker given before al"}},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Review the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with known MEN2A and a confirmed pheochromocytoma is scheduled for adrenalectomy. He also requires thyroidectomy for medullary thyroid carcinoma. His resting blood pressure is 150/95 mmHg. Which of the following best describes the most critical management consideration regarding the sequencing of his surgeries?","choices":{"D":"The pheochromocytoma must be resected first after adequate preoperative alpha-adrenergic blockade, because performing thyroidectomy first risks a potentially fatal hypertensive crisis from catecholamine release during anesthesia or manipulation","A":"Intravenous hydrocortisone must be administered before either surgery because germline RET mutations impair ACTH receptor signaling in the adrenal cortex, creating relative glucocorticoid deficiency that may precipitate adrenal crisis during general anesthesia","B":"Deferral of both surgeries until urine metanephrine levels normalize on a low-catecholamine diet is appropriate, because pheochromocytoma secretion is primarily diet-dependent and blood pressure can be controlled with dietary restriction alone before elective resection","C":"Bilateral adrenalectomy must be performed simultaneously with thyroidectomy to prevent intraoperative catecholamine release from the contralateral gland, which has a 50% chance of harboring an occult pheochromocytoma in all MEN2A patients","E":"Thyroidectomy should be performed first because medullary thyroid carcinoma invades local structures rapidly, and the pheochromocytoma can be safely managed with nonselective beta-blockade alone until thyroid surgery is complete and recovery achieved"},"explanation":"The principle that the pheochromocytoma must be addressed before any other elective surgery is absolute. Anesthetic induction, surgical manipulation, or external pressure on the tumor can trigger massive catecholamine release causing hypertensive crisis, arrhythmias, and death. In MEN2A with both MTC and pheochromocytoma, the pheochromocytoma is resected first after alpha blockade, then thyroidectomy follows after adequate recovery. Bilateral pheos are present in up to 50% of familial cases, so imaging of the contralateral gland is mandatory, but not routine bilateral adrenalectomy. Hydrocortisone is not indicated for RET mutations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In any patient with concurrent pheochromocytoma and another surgical condition, the pheochromocytoma is addressed first. This principle applies to incidentally found pheo before any elective surgery. COMLEX: Bilateral pheos in MEN2A are common; adrenal-sparing surgery is preferred to avoid permanent Addison disease requiring lifelong steroid replacement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma management priority before concurrent surgical procedures in MEN2A","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Hydrocortisone is not indicate"}}],"20":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following synthetic glucocorticoids has the highest anti-inflammatory potency relative to cortisol with essentially zero mineralocorticoid activity, making it the preferred agent for diagnostic suppression testing?","choices":{"D":"Dexamethasone, with approximately 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol, zero mineralocorticoid activity, and a duration of action of 24 to 36 hours that keeps ACTH suppressed overnight, so the 8 AM cortisol reflects endogenous adrenal secretion only","A":"Prednisone, with 4 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol and moderate mineralocorticoid activity that is sufficient to cause sodium retention and mild hypertension, requiring monitoring of blood pressure and electrolytes during prolonged therapeutic courses","B":"Triamcinolone, with 5 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol and no clinically significant mineralocorticoid activity, commonly used for local injection into joints and tendons and for topical dermatologic preparations rather than for systemic endocrine testing","C":"Fludrocortisone, with approximately 10 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol and 125 to 250 times the mineralocorticoid potency of cortisol, which makes it the preferred drug for mineralocorticoid replacement in primary adrenal insufficiency","E":"Hydrocortisone, with equivalent anti-inflammatory potency to endogenous cortisol and mild mineralocorticoid activity, preferred for physiologic glucocorticoid replacement in adrenal insufficiency because it most closely mimics the natural cortisol secretory pattern"},"explanation":"Dexamethasone has approximately 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol and virtually zero mineralocorticoid activity. Its long duration of action (24-36 hours, Katzung Table 39–1) ensures ACTH suppression lasts overnight, so any cortisol measured at 8 AM reflects endogenous secretion only. Critically, dexamethasone does not cross-react with the standard cortisol immunoassay, making it ideal for suppression testing. Prednisone (4x), triamcinolone (5x), and hydrocortisone (1x) all have lower potency. Fludrocortisone is reserved for mineralocorticoid replacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Relative potencies: hydrocortisone 1x, prednisone 4x, triamcinolone 5x, dexamethasone 30x. Mineralocorticoid activity: hydrocortisone > prednisone > triamcinolone = dexamethasone (zero). COMLEX: Dexamethasone is also used for croup, raised ICP, and fetal lung maturation in some protocols.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis and circadian cortisol secretion provide the physiologic baseline on which steroid pharmacokinetics builds","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Prednisone (4x), triamcinolone","B":"Prednisone (4x), triamcinolone","C":"Fludrocortisone is reserved fo","E":"Prednisone (4x), triamcinolone"}},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with Addison disease who underwent bilateral adrenalectomy requires lifelong mineralocorticoid replacement. Which of the following agents is most appropriate because of its dominant mineralocorticoid potency relative to its glucocorticoid effects?","choices":{"A":"Fludrocortisone, a synthetic mineralocorticoid with 125 to 250 times the sodium-retaining potency of cortisol and a prolonged duration of action, used as the standard mineralocorticoid replacement in primary adrenal insufficiency and in salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","B":"Hydrocortisone, a synthetic form of cortisol with equivalent anti-inflammatory potency to cortisol and modest mineralocorticoid activity insufficient for full mineralocorticoid replacement, which must be combined with fludrocortisone for adequate aldosterone effect in primary adrenal insufficiency","C":"Dexamethasone, a highly potent synthetic glucocorticoid with 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol and virtually zero mineralocorticoid activity, making it completely inappropriate for mineralocorticoid replacement therapy in patients with primary adrenal insufficiency","D":"Triamcinolone, a synthetic glucocorticoid with 5 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol and no clinically significant mineralocorticoid activity, used primarily for local injection and topical application rather than for systemic mineralocorticoid replacement therapy","E":"Prednisone, a prodrug converted to prednisolone in the liver with 4 times the anti-inflammatory potency of cortisol and moderate but insufficient mineralocorticoid activity, requiring combination with fludrocortisone for adequate aldosterone effect in bilateral adrenalectomy patients"},"explanation":"Fludrocortisone is the only clinically available mineralocorticoid replacement agent, with 125-250 times the sodium-retaining potency of cortisol. It has only modest glucocorticoid effects at replacement doses. In Addison disease and CAH requiring mineralocorticoid replacement, fludrocortisone is given once daily while hydrocortisone or other glucocorticoids are given separately for cortisol replacement. No other listed agent provides adequate mineralocorticoid replacement at clinically safe doses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fludrocortisone = the mineralocorticoid replacement drug. Used in primary AI (Addison), CAH with salt-wasting, and some cases of orthostatic hypotension. COMLEX: Monitoring signs of over-replacement: hypertension, hypokalemia, edema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis and circadian cortisol secretion provide the physiologic baseline on which steroid pharmacokinetics builds","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"In Addison disease and CAH req","C":"No other listed agent provides","D":"No other listed agent provides","E":"No other listed agent provides"}},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy with moderate persistent asthma is prescribed an inhaled corticosteroid. His mother asks why this formulation minimizes systemic side effects compared to oral prednisone at doses producing equivalent airway anti-inflammatory effect. Which of the following best explains this pharmacokinetic advantage?","choices":{"B":"Inhaled corticosteroids such as beclomethasone and budesonide undergo extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism of any systemically absorbed fraction, rapidly inactivating the drug before it reaches peripheral tissues and greatly reducing systemic bioavailability","A":"Inhaled corticosteroids bind glucocorticoid receptors exclusively within bronchial epithelial cells and cannot diffuse through the alveolar basement membrane into systemic circulation, preventing any measurable plasma drug concentrations or extrapulmonary receptor activation at recommended doses","C":"Inhaled corticosteroids are chemically modified to act through beta-2 adrenergic receptors in airway smooth muscle rather than through glucocorticoid receptors, using a completely separate anti-inflammatory pathway that does not affect the HPA axis or peripheral glucocorticoid-sensitive tissues","D":"Inhaled corticosteroids form irreversible covalent bonds with mucus glycoproteins in the airway lumen on first contact, permanently sequestering the drug in airway secretions and preventing any transcellular absorption across the bronchial or alveolar epithelial surfaces","E":"Inhaled corticosteroids are degraded within seconds by mast cell-derived tryptase enzymes abundant in bronchial mucosa, limiting their anti-inflammatory effect to the luminal surface where tryptase concentration is lowest and preventing all systemic absorption"},"explanation":"Inhaled corticosteroids like budesonide and fluticasone have high topical potency in the airways. Any swallowed fraction undergoes extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism, and any absorbed pulmonary fraction is also rapidly cleared by the liver. This high first-pass extraction means systemic bioavailability is very low despite meaningful local airway concentrations. Oral prednisone bypasses this first-pass effect and distributes systemically. At equivalent anti-inflammatory airway doses, inhaled steroids cause far less HPA axis suppression, growth retardation, and other systemic effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First-pass metabolism is the key pharmacokinetic feature of inhaled corticosteroids that limits systemic toxicity. Fluticasone and budesonide have the highest first-pass extraction. COMLEX: Even inhaled steroids can cause adrenal suppression at very high doses; children should be monitored for growth velocity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis and circadian cortisol secretion provide the physiologic baseline on which steroid pharmacokinetics builds","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the mechanism of action of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which glucocorticoids alter gene expression in target cells?","choices":{"E":"Glucocorticoids diffuse across the cell membrane and bind cytosolic glucocorticoid receptors, displacing Hsp90 chaperone protein and causing receptor dimerization and nuclear translocation, where the complex binds glucocorticoid response elements in DNA promoter regions to increase transcription of regulatory proteins","A":"Glucocorticoids bind transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinases on the cell surface, activating the JAK-STAT signaling pathway and causing direct phosphorylation of transcription factors that upregulate anti-inflammatory gene expression within minutes of hormone binding","B":"Glucocorticoids bind nuclear G-protein-coupled receptors that activate adenylyl cyclase and increase intracellular cAMP, leading to PKA-mediated phosphorylation of CREB and subsequent transcription of glucocorticoid-responsive inflammatory regulatory genes","C":"Glucocorticoids diffuse into the nucleus directly and bind to DNA at promoter regions without requiring a cytosolic receptor, triggering RNA polymerase recruitment and immediate transcription of target gene proteins within the first 30 minutes of exposure","D":"Glucocorticoids bind membrane-associated receptors that undergo endocytosis after ligand binding, with the receptor-ligand complex directly stimulating ribosomal translation of pre-existing anti-inflammatory mRNA transcripts already present in the cytoplasm"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids are lipophilic steroid hormones that diffuse passively across cell membranes and bind cytosolic glucocorticoid receptors (GRs). In the unbound state, GR is complexed with Hsp90, Hsp70, and other chaperones. Ligand binding displaces Hsp90, the GR dimerizes, and the complex is translocated to the nucleus. There, it binds specific glucocorticoid response elements (GREs) in promoter regions of target genes, altering transcription. This genomic mechanism produces delayed effects because new proteins must be synthesized.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The GR-Hsp90 complex → ligand binding → Hsp90 displacement → nuclear translocation → GRE binding → transcription is the classic glucocorticoid mechanism. Compare with rapid non-genomic effects at membrane receptors that can occur within seconds-to-minutes but are not the primary mechanism. COMLEX: The nuclear receptor mechanism also applies to mineralocorticoids, sex steroids, thyroid hormone, and vitamin D.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Cortisol anti-inflammatory mechanisms at the molecular level are the basis for glucocorticoid receptor pharmacology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the mechanism of action of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmacologist notes that glucocorticoids require several hours to achieve full anti-inflammatory effects despite rapid receptor binding. Which of the following best explains this delayed onset of action?","choices":{"C":"The glucocorticoid-receptor complex binds glucocorticoid response elements to induce new mRNA, which must then be translated into effector proteins such as lipocortin that mediate anti-inflammatory actions, and this sequence takes several hours to produce enough protein","A":"Glucocorticoids must first be hydroxylated by adrenocortical cytochrome P450 enzymes before the active metabolite can diffuse across target cell membranes and bind to intracellular glucocorticoid receptors, requiring several hours for complete peripheral tissue conversion","B":"The glucocorticoid-receptor complex must be phosphorylated by nuclear kinases before it can bind DNA regulatory elements, and the phosphorylation cascade requires multiple enzymatic steps that collectively take 6 to 8 hours to complete in most anti-inflammatory target tissues","D":"Glucocorticoids must saturate all available mineralocorticoid receptors before binding to glucocorticoid receptors because mineralocorticoid receptors have higher cortisol binding affinity, and achieving therapeutic glucocorticoid receptor occupancy is therefore delayed by hours in most peripheral tissues","E":"Glucocorticoids accumulate in plasma membranes for several hours before achieving sufficient intracellular concentrations to displace Hsp90 from cytosolic glucocorticoid receptors and permit the receptor conformational change required for nuclear translocation"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids act through a genomic mechanism: receptor binding triggers transcription of new mRNAs that must be translated into proteins before anti-inflammatory effects manifest. Key induced proteins include lipocortin (which inhibits phospholipase A2), IkappaB (which inhibits NF-kappaB), and anti-inflammatory cytokines. This transcription-translation sequence takes hours before there is a meaningful clinical effect. This is why glucocorticoids cannot replace short-acting bronchodilators in acute bronchospasm; they prevent worsening but do not provide immediate relief.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid clinical effects are delayed by hours because of the genomic mechanism (new protein synthesis required). For acute rescue, use beta-2 agonists. For prevention and long-term control, glucocorticoids are first-line. COMLEX: Rapid non-genomic effects (within seconds-minutes) through membrane glucocorticoid receptors do exist but are not the primary clinical mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Cortisol anti-inflammatory mechanisms at the molecular level are the basis for glucocorticoid receptor pharmacology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the mechanism of action of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why the same glucocorticoid molecule produces different gene expression responses in kidney versus liver despite both tissues expressing structurally identical glucocorticoid receptors?","choices":{"B":"The transcriptional response in each tissue is determined by tissue-specific co-regulators, histone-modifying enzymes, and chromatin accessibility, which together control which glucocorticoid response elements the receptor can bind and which target genes can be activated in that cell type","A":"Renal and hepatic cells express different isoforms of the glucocorticoid receptor encoded by separate gene loci on different chromosomes, and these structurally distinct isoforms bind to different DNA sequences to activate fundamentally different transcriptional programs in each tissue","C":"The kidney converts cortisol to mineralocorticoid-active metabolites through 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, redirecting the entire glucocorticoid signal through mineralocorticoid receptor pathways rather than through glucocorticoid receptor-mediated transcriptional activation","D":"Hepatic cells contain a unique second messenger system that converts the glucocorticoid-receptor binding signal into cAMP, while renal cells use IP3 and diacylglycerol as second messengers, producing fundamentally different downstream phosphorylation cascades from identical receptor binding events","E":"Glucocorticoids activate mineralocorticoid receptors exclusively in the kidney because cortisol has equal affinity for both receptor types and the kidney entirely lacks glucocorticoid receptors, directing the complete renal glucocorticoid response through mineralocorticoid transcriptional pathways"},"explanation":"The glucocorticoid receptor is identical across tissues, but the transcriptional outcome is determined by the local chromatin environment, tissue-specific transcription factor co-activators and co-repressors, and which GREs are accessible in the open chromatin state of each cell type. For example, hepatocytes have accessible GREs near gluconeogenic enzyme genes (PEPCK, G6Pase), so cortisol induces gluconeogenesis in liver. Kidney mineralocorticoid-sensitive cells inactivate cortisol via 11-beta-HSD2 to prevent activation of mineralocorticoid receptors that are co-expressed with GRs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tissue specificity of steroid hormones = epigenetic regulation (chromatin accessibility, tissue-specific co-regulators). This principle applies to all steroid hormones. COMLEX: Understanding tissue-specific GR effects explains why some steroids cause osteoporosis (bone) and glucose elevation (liver/muscle) while also being anti-inflammatory (immune cells).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Cortisol anti-inflammatory mechanisms at the molecular level are the basis for glucocorticoid receptor pharmacology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the physiologic and metabolic effects of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis has been taking prednisone 40 mg daily for 3 months and develops a fasting blood glucose of 210 mg per dL without a prior history of diabetes. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of glucocorticoid-induced hyperglycemia?","choices":{"A":"Glucocorticoids stimulate hepatic gluconeogenesis by inducing PEPCK and other enzymes, promote protein catabolism that supplies amino acid substrates, and cause peripheral insulin resistance by impairing post-receptor signaling in muscle and adipose tissue","B":"Glucocorticoids directly inhibit pancreatic beta cell glucose transporter proteins, preventing glucose uptake by insulin-secreting cells and abolishing insulin secretion entirely, independently of changes in peripheral insulin sensitivity or hepatic glucose production","C":"Glucocorticoids activate glucagon receptors on hepatocytes and adipocytes, causing simultaneous glycogenolysis and lipolysis that rapidly depletes glycogen stores and releases fatty acids that then inhibit insulin signaling in peripheral target tissues","D":"Glucocorticoids inhibit renal glucose reabsorption by competing with glucose transporter proteins in the proximal tubule, causing glucosuria that stimulates compensatory hepatic glycogenolysis and an adaptive rise in circulating blood glucose concentration","E":"Glucocorticoids activate the sympathoadrenal axis through hypothalamic adrenergic receptor upregulation, causing norepinephrine-mediated glycogenolysis in the liver and epinephrine-mediated inhibition of peripheral glucose uptake through beta-2 receptor activation"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids cause hyperglycemia through three converging mechanisms: (1) induction of hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes (PEPCK, G6Pase, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase) increasing hepatic glucose output; (2) stimulation of muscle protein catabolism, providing alanine and other gluconeogenic amino acids to the liver; and (3) impairment of insulin signaling in peripheral tissues, reducing glucose uptake. The net effect is increased hepatic glucose production with decreased peripheral utilization. Insulin secretion from beta cells is actually stimulated secondarily by the hyperglycemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid-induced diabetes = increased gluconeogenesis + insulin resistance + peripheral catabolism. Post-meal glucose elevation is often more pronounced than fasting hyperglycemia (pattern differs from type 2 DM). COMLEX: Monitor blood glucose in any patient started on systemic steroids; patients may require insulin therapy during high-dose treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Cortisol metabolic effects on carbohydrate, protein, and fat are amplified and prolonged by pharmacologic glucocorticoids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Insulin secretion from beta ce"}},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the physiologic and metabolic effects of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman on long-term oral corticosteroid therapy for systemic lupus erythematosus develops central obesity, moon face, and a buffalo hump with thin arms and legs. Which of the following best explains this regional fat redistribution pattern?","choices":{"C":"Glucocorticoids activate cortisol-specific receptors in visceral and neck adipocytes that convert cortisol to an anabolic metabolite causing regional lipid accumulation, while peripheral adipocytes metabolize cortisol through a catabolic pathway producing net fat loss in the extremities","A":"Glucocorticoids selectively upregulate lipoprotein lipase in peripheral fat depots of the extremities while simultaneously inhibiting lipoprotein lipase in central and facial depots, causing adipose tissue depletion in peripheral regions and excess fat accumulation centrally through lipoprotein-mediated lipid storage","B":"Glucocorticoids directly stimulate adipocyte differentiation and lipid storage exclusively in the omentum, mesenteric fat, and posterior neck through PPAR-gamma receptors expressed only in these anatomically specific adipose tissue depots and not in peripheral extremity adipocytes","D":"Glucocorticoids reduce insulin sensitivity uniformly across all adipose tissue but extremity depots are inherently more insulin-sensitive than central depots, so the extremities lose adipose tissue first through lipolysis while trunk adiposity is preserved through residual insulin-driven lipogenesis","E":"Glucocorticoids promote both lipolysis and lipogenesis simultaneously, with regional differences in adipocyte sensitivity producing net fat mobilization in the extremities where lipolysis predominates and net fat deposition centrally in the trunk, face, and posterior neck where insulin-driven lipogenesis is dominant"},"explanation":"The paradoxical fat redistribution of Cushingoid syndrome results from glucocorticoids simultaneously activating both lipolysis (via beta-adrenergic sensitization) and lipogenesis (via insulin stimulation secondary to hyperglycemia). Peripheral fat (limbs) is predominantly regulated by lipolysis and shows net fat loss. Trunk, facial, and nuchal fat depots have more insulin receptors and greater lipogenic capacity, causing net fat gain in those locations. The result is the characteristic central obesity, moon face, and buffalo hump with limb wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic Cushing body habitus = central obesity + moon face + buffalo hump + thin extremities. Fat redistribution is from peripheral to central depots. COMLEX: Distinguishing Cushingoid body habitus from simple obesity: striae, thin skin, proximal muscle weakness, and easy bruising suggest glucocorticoid excess.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Cortisol metabolic effects on carbohydrate, protein, and fat are amplified and prolonged by pharmacologic glucocorticoids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the physiologic and metabolic effects of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman at 32 weeks gestation is at high risk for preterm delivery. Her obstetrician administers betamethasone intramuscularly. Which of the following best explains the primary clinical purpose of this intervention?","choices":{"E":"Betamethasone crosses the placenta and binds fetal lung glucocorticoid receptors, inducing transcription of surfactant proteins in type II pneumocytes and accelerating pulmonary maturation to reduce the risk of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","A":"Betamethasone suppresses uterine contractions by activating beta-2 adrenergic receptors in myometrial smooth muscle, reducing intracellular calcium concentration and preventing premature labor long enough for the fetus to reach adequate pulmonary maturation","B":"Betamethasone promotes placental progesterone synthesis through glucocorticoid receptor activation in syncytiotrophoblast cells, maintaining progesterone levels that suppress uterine contractility and prolong gestation past the critical period of fetal lung immaturity","C":"Betamethasone reduces fetal metabolic demand by suppressing thyroid hormone synthesis in the fetal thyroid gland, lowering fetal oxygen requirements and improving tolerance of the relatively hypoxic neonatal environment during the transition to extrauterine life","D":"Betamethasone accelerates fetal cardiovascular maturation by upregulating atrial natriuretic peptide production in fetal cardiomyocytes, improving fetal cardiac contractility and reducing the hemodynamic compromise immediately after preterm birth"},"explanation":"Antenatal betamethasone is given to mothers when delivery is anticipated before 34 weeks of gestation to accelerate fetal lung maturation (Katzung Ch 39). Betamethasone and dexamethasone are preferred over hydrocortisone or prednisone because they cross the placenta and are not inactivated by placental 11-beta-HSD2. They induce surfactant phospholipid synthesis in type II pneumocytes, reducing the risk of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease). The usual regimen is betamethasone 12 mg IM, then a second 12-mg dose 18-24 hours later (Katzung Ch 39).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antenatal betamethasone for fetal lung maturation - standard when delivery is anticipated before 34 weeks of gestation. Betamethasone and dexamethasone cross placenta (unlike hydrocortisone and prednisone which are inactivated by placental 11-beta-HSD2). COMLEX: This is a classic pharmacology-obstetrics intersection question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Cortisol metabolic effects on carbohydrate, protein, and fat are amplified and prolonged by pharmacologic glucocorticoids","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the concept of anabolic and catabolic effects of steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman who has taken prednisone 15 mg daily for 5 years for giant cell arteritis develops a fragility fracture of her left hip after a minor fall. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis?","choices":{"D":"Glucocorticoids inhibit osteoblast activity and promote osteoblast apoptosis while reducing intestinal calcium absorption and increasing renal calcium excretion, leading to bone loss that disproportionately affects trabecular bone in the spine and hip","A":"Glucocorticoids increase parathyroid hormone secretion by directly activating glucocorticoid receptors in parathyroid chief cells, causing PTH-mediated osteoclast activation and secondary hyperparathyroidism that accelerates trabecular bone resorption throughout the axial skeleton","B":"Glucocorticoids directly activate osteoclast surface receptors that stimulate bone resorption through RANKL-independent mechanisms, causing progressive cortical and trabecular bone loss disproportionate to the degree of concurrent osteoblast suppression","C":"Glucocorticoids increase intestinal phosphate absorption while simultaneously reducing calcium absorption, producing chronic hyperphosphatemia that precipitates calcium phosphate crystals in bone matrix and progressively weakens trabecular microarchitecture","E":"Glucocorticoids inhibit growth hormone secretion from the anterior pituitary, reducing IGF-1 levels in circulation and thereby suppressing chondrocyte and osteoblast proliferation in a dose-dependent manner proportional to the degree of GH suppression"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis results from dual mechanisms: decreased bone formation (osteoblast inhibition and apoptosis, reduced osteocalcin, IGF-1 suppression) and increased bone resorption (indirect via reduced gonadal hormones and secondary hyperparathyroidism from reduced calcium absorption). Glucocorticoids also reduce intestinal calcium absorption (anti-vitamin D3 effect) and increase urinary calcium excretion. Trabecular-rich bone (vertebrae, femoral neck) is most affected. Prevention includes calcium, vitamin D, and bisphosphonates for long-term users.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid osteoporosis = osteoblast inhibition + reduced calcium absorption + secondary hyperparathyroidism. Trabecular bone affected most. Bisphosphonates + Ca + Vit D for prevention in long-term users. COMLEX: Even low doses taken long term cause significant bone loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome clinical features directly reflect the catabolic and lipogenic effects of chronic glucocorticoid excess","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the concept of anabolic and catabolic effects of steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following clinical findings in a child receiving long-term corticosteroid therapy for nephrotic syndrome is best explained by the catabolic effects of glucocorticoids on structural tissues?","choices":{"C":"Reduced linear growth velocity, muscle wasting, and impaired wound healing during prolonged therapy, explained by suppression of chondrocyte and osteoblast activity, skeletal muscle protein catabolism, and inhibition of fibroblast proliferation and collagen synthesis","A":"Hypoglycemia and hypotension developing after sudden steroid discontinuation, explained by HPA axis suppression that prevents the body from mounting an adequate endogenous cortisol response to physiologic stressors following abrupt corticosteroid withdrawal","B":"Moon facies, buffalo hump, and central adiposity developing over several months of therapy, explained by glucocorticoid-mediated lipogenesis in trunk and facial adipose depots with concurrent lipolysis in peripheral extremity depots producing the characteristic Cushingoid appearance","D":"Hypertension, hypokalemia, and mild metabolic alkalosis developing during high-dose therapy, explained by glucocorticoid binding to renal mineralocorticoid receptors at supraphysiologic concentrations causing sodium retention and potassium wasting in the collecting duct","E":"Secondary diabetes mellitus and dyslipidemia developing during prolonged therapy, explained by glucocorticoid induction of hepatic lipase activation and adipocyte lipolysis that floods the portal circulation with free fatty acids and glycerol substrates for dyslipidemia"},"explanation":"The catabolic effects of glucocorticoids on structural tissues include: (1) growth suppression in children via inhibition of GH axis and direct chondrocyte/osteoblast suppression; (2) skeletal muscle wasting via protein catabolism through the ubiquitin-proteasome system; and (3) impaired wound healing via fibroblast inhibition and reduced collagen synthesis. These are distinct from the metabolic (hyperglycemia), adipose redistribution, and mineralocorticoid-like effects, which are also seen with prolonged steroid use but reflect different mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Catabolic effects = muscle wasting, growth suppression, thin skin, poor wound healing, osteoporosis. Distinct from metabolic effects (hyperglycemia) and fat redistribution (lipogenic). COMLEX: Growth suppression in children is a critical concern; alternate-day dosing and lowest effective dose minimize this.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome clinical features directly reflect the catabolic and lipogenic effects of chronic glucocorticoid excess","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"These are distinct from the me","D":"These are distinct from the me","E":"These are distinct from the me"}},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the concept of anabolic and catabolic effects of steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man who has been taking dexamethasone 4 mg daily for 3 months for cerebral edema is scheduled to discontinue therapy. Which of the following best explains the critical reason for gradual tapering rather than abrupt cessation?","choices":{"A":"Prolonged exogenous glucocorticoid administration suppresses CRH and ACTH secretion through negative feedback on the HPA axis, causing adrenocortical atrophy, and abrupt withdrawal leaves the patient without endogenous cortisol capacity to respond adequately to physiologic stressors","B":"Prolonged exogenous glucocorticoids upregulate glucocorticoid receptor expression in peripheral tissues, making them hypersensitive to cortisol, and abrupt withdrawal causes a severe rebound inflammatory response as the body overreacts to its own physiologic cortisol levels","C":"Prolonged glucocorticoid use depletes adrenal stores of cholesterol and steroidogenic enzymes, and tapering is required to allow adequate time for adrenal tissue to replenish steroidogenic substrate reserves before the exogenous steroid is fully withdrawn from the system","D":"Prolonged glucocorticoid therapy activates 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 in peripheral tissues, converting systemic cortisone to active cortisol, and abrupt withdrawal eliminates this peripheral activation causing sudden cortisol deficiency in metabolically active organs","E":"Prolonged glucocorticoid use stimulates CRH and ACTH hypersecretion through positive feedback on the HPA axis, and tapering prevents a surge of endogenous cortisol that would otherwise occur when exogenous steroid is removed from the feedback circuit"},"explanation":"Exogenous glucocorticoids suppress the HPA axis through negative feedback at the hypothalamus (reduced CRH) and pituitary (reduced ACTH), causing adrenal cortical atrophy from lack of ACTH trophic support. After more than 2 weeks of corticosteroid therapy, adrenal suppression may occur (Katzung Ch 39), and recovery can take months. Abrupt withdrawal in this setting produces adrenal insufficiency, which is potentially fatal if the patient faces physiologic stress such as surgery, infection, or trauma. Tapering allows gradual restoration of HPA axis function and adrenal cortical mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Never abruptly stop steroids taken for more than 2 weeks at supraphysiologic doses. Adrenal suppression can persist for months after stopping. Signs of withdrawal: fatigue, weakness, hypotension, nausea, hypoglycemia. COMLEX: Stress dosing with IV hydrocortisone is required for any major physiologic stress in patients on chronic steroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — Cushing syndrome clinical features directly reflect the catabolic and lipogenic effects of chronic glucocorticoid excess","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl20_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism by which glucocorticoids simultaneously suppress both prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis, in contrast to NSAIDs?","choices":{"E":"Glucocorticoids induce lipocortin (annexin-1), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and blocks arachidonic acid release from membrane phospholipids, preventing synthesis of all eicosanoids, including prostaglandins via COX and leukotrienes via the lipoxygenase pathway","A":"Glucocorticoids directly inhibit cyclooxygenase-1 and cyclooxygenase-2 enzyme activity through irreversible acetylation of the active site serine residue, preventing arachidonic acid conversion to prostaglandin precursors while leaving the lipoxygenase leukotriene pathway completely unaffected","B":"Glucocorticoids reduce thromboxane synthase and prostacyclin synthase activity by competitively binding to their heme prosthetic groups, blocking terminal prostaglandin synthesis from PGG2 while allowing leukotriene production to continue through an intact lipoxygenase pathway","C":"Glucocorticoids activate NLRP3 inflammasome suppressor proteins that block caspase-1-mediated cleavage of pro-interleukin-1-beta, preventing the IL-1-beta signaling that normally activates both cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathway enzyme gene transcription","D":"Glucocorticoids block interleukin-6 receptor signaling in immune cells, preventing the upregulation of both cyclooxygenase-2 and 5-lipoxygenase expression that IL-6 normally drives in activated macrophages and neutrophils at sites of tissue inflammation"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids induce lipocortin-1 (annexin-1), which inhibits phospholipase A2 (PLA2). PLA2 is the enzyme that liberates arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids. Because arachidonic acid is the common precursor for both the COX pathway (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, prostacyclin) and the LOX pathway (leukotrienes), PLA2 inhibition blocks both simultaneously. This is why glucocorticoids have broader anti-inflammatory effects than NSAIDs, which only inhibit COX. Glucocorticoids also decrease COX-2 mRNA directly, adding to prostaglandin suppression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoids vs NSAIDs: GCs block PLA2 via lipocortin = blocks prostaglandins AND leukotrienes. NSAIDs block only COX = prostaglandins only (leukotrienes still produced, which is why aspirin-exacerbated asthma occurs). COMLEX: This broader eicosanoid suppression explains why glucocorticoids work in asthma (block both bronchospastic prostaglandins and leukotrienes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Cortisol anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms are pharmacologically amplified and exploited therapeutically","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl20_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient started on high-dose prednisone is found on CBC to have a WBC of 15,000 cells per microliter with 92% neutrophils, 3% lymphocytes, 0% eosinophils, and 1% monocytes without clinical evidence of infection. Which of the following best explains this leukocyte pattern?","choices":{"D":"Prednisone activates colony-stimulating factor receptors on neutrophil progenitors in the bone marrow stimulating excess granulopoiesis independent of any cytokine signaling, while redirecting lymphoid progenitors to differentiate into granulocytes through lineage switching in the marrow","A":"Prednisone activates the NLRP3 inflammasome in bone marrow progenitor cells, causing excessive granulopoiesis and neutrophil release while simultaneously triggering lymphocyte apoptosis through activation of the intrinsic mitochondrial apoptosis pathway in lymphoid progenitors","B":"Prednisone upregulates L-selectin and ICAM-1 on neutrophil surfaces causing increased neutrophil adhesion to vascular endothelium, while simultaneously inhibiting lymphocyte production from thymic precursors through glucocorticoid receptor-mediated suppression of T cell development","C":"Prednisone causes neutrophilia through demargination of neutrophils from vessel walls and prolonged neutrophil survival, while causing lymphopenia and eosinopenia by redistributing lymphocytes to lymphoid tissues and promoting apoptosis of lymphocytes, eosinophils, and monocytes","E":"Prednisone inhibits neutrophil phagocytosis and oxidative burst, causing compensatory bone marrow release of immature neutrophils into the bloodstream, while suppressing B and T lymphocyte cytokine production required for the normal maintenance of peripheral lymphocyte populations"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids produce a characteristic CBC pattern: neutrophilia from demargination (release from vessel walls) and prolonged survival; lymphopenia from redistribution to lymphoid organs and apoptosis; eosinopenia and monocytopenia from apoptosis and redistribution. This pattern is so characteristic that it can suggest unsuspected glucocorticoid excess (endogenous or exogenous). The neutrophilia does NOT reflect improved immunity; glucocorticoids impair neutrophil function (chemotaxis, phagocytosis, oxidative burst) while increasing their count.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid CBC pattern: neutrophilia + lymphopenia + eosinopenia + monocytopenia. Classic boards question. Also: despite neutrophilia, infection risk increases because of functional impairment. COMLEX: Eosinopenia with steroids was historically used clinically (\"Thorn test\"); its absence in Addison confirms adrenal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Cortisol anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms are pharmacologically amplified and exploited therapeutically","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl20_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why glucocorticoids are included in the treatment regimen for multiple myeloma and lymphoma?","choices":{"C":"Glucocorticoids are directly lymphotoxic to malignant plasma cells and lymphoma cells, activating intrinsic apoptotic pathways through receptor-mediated transcriptional changes that downregulate survival signals such as Bcl-2 and upregulate pro-apoptotic proteins","A":"Glucocorticoids inhibit the mTOR pathway in plasma cells and lymphoma cells, preventing protein synthesis and inducing cell cycle arrest in G1 phase, an effect additive with proteasome inhibitor activity used concurrently in standard myeloma treatment regimens","B":"Glucocorticoids induce expression of VEGF inhibitors in neoplastic lymphoid cells, reducing tumor angiogenesis and starving rapidly proliferating myeloma cells of the vascular supply needed to sustain their high metabolic demands within the bone marrow microenvironment","D":"Glucocorticoids reduce the production of immunoglobulins by malignant plasma cells through direct suppression of immunoglobulin heavy chain gene transcription, reducing the paraprotein burden causing hyperviscosity and cast nephropathy in myeloma patients","E":"Glucocorticoids activate natural killer cell cytotoxicity against tumor cells by upregulating perforin and granzyme gene expression in NK cells, enabling immune-mediated tumor killing that is selectively deficient in the immunocompromised bone marrow microenvironment of myeloma"},"explanation":"Lymphoid cells, including malignant plasma cells, B cell lymphoma cells, and T cell lymphoma cells, are particularly sensitive to glucocorticoid-induced apoptosis. GR activation in these cells downregulates pro-survival proteins like Bcl-2 and upregulates pro-apoptotic proteins, triggering mitochondrial pathway apoptosis. This lymphotoxic property makes dexamethasone and prednisone cornerstones of myeloma (dexamethasone-containing regimens) and lymphoma (the prednisone in CHOP) treatment. The effect is selective for lymphoid cells because other cell types have different apoptotic thresholds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoids = lymphotoxic. Used in CHOP for lymphoma, dexamethasone-based combinations for myeloma, and ALL induction. Dexamethasone is preferred in myeloma because of less fluid retention than prednisone. COMLEX: Glucocorticoids are also used for autoimmune cytopenias (ITP, AIHA) through immunosuppressive rather than direct lymphotoxic mechanisms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — Cortisol anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive mechanisms are pharmacologically amplified and exploited therapeutically","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl20_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is started on oral prednisone 40 mg for a severe asthma exacerbation. His pharmacist notes that prednisone itself is pharmacologically inactive and must be converted in the body. Which of the following best describes the required activation step and its clinical implication?","choices":{"B":"Prednisone is hydroxylated at the 11-beta position by intestinal mucosal CYP3A4 during first-pass absorption, converting it to prednisolone before it reaches the portal circulation, and impaired intestinal CYP3A4 activity reduces bioavailability in patients on azole antifungals","A":"Prednisone is a prodrug converted to active prednisolone by hepatic 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1; patients with severe hepatic insufficiency may have impaired conversion, reduced plasma prednisolone concentrations, and a diminished clinical response to therapy","C":"Prednisone is deesterified by plasma esterases in the bloodstream immediately after absorption, forming active prednisolone within 30 minutes of oral administration in virtually all patients, with essentially no variability in conversion efficiency between individuals","D":"Prednisone is activated exclusively by adrenocortical 17-alpha-hydroxylase to form prednisolone, and patients with congenital adrenal hyperplasia involving this enzyme require higher prednisone doses to achieve therapeutic prednisolone concentrations in peripheral tissues","E":"Prednisone is converted to prednisolone exclusively within lymphocyte cytosol by glucocorticoid-activating reductase enzymes, which explains why its primary therapeutic effects appear first in immune cells before systemic anti-inflammatory benefits develop in other tissues"},"explanation":"Prednisone is an inactive 11-keto compound (like cortisone) that requires reduction at the 11-beta position to the active 11-hydroxy form (prednisolone, analogous to cortisol). This conversion occurs mainly in the liver via 11-beta-HSD1. In patients with severe hepatic disease (cirrhosis, fulminant hepatitis), this conversion is impaired, and the active prednisolone levels may be significantly lower than expected. In such patients, prednisolone is preferred over prednisone to bypass this hepatic conversion step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prednisone and cortisone are prodrugs (11-keto); prednisolone and cortisol are active (11-hydroxy). Conversion by 11-beta-HSD1 in liver. Use prednisolone in severe liver disease. COMLEX: 11-beta-HSD2 in kidney inactivates cortisol to cortisone to protect mineralocorticoid receptors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — Pharmacokinetics of glucocorticoid prodrug activation and hepatic first-pass metabolism","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl20_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with suspected Cushing syndrome undergoes a 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test. She takes 1 mg at 11 PM and her 8 AM plasma cortisol returns at 22 mcg per dL without suppression. Which property of dexamethasone makes it uniquely suited for this diagnostic test rather than other synthetic glucocorticoids?","choices":{"A":"Dexamethasone has approximately 30 times the glucocorticoid potency of cortisol, does not cross-react with the standard cortisol immunoassay, and has a prolonged half-life sufficient to maintain hypothalamic and pituitary suppression overnight so that any detectable cortisol at 8 AM reflects only autonomous endogenous adrenal secretion","B":"Dexamethasone has zero mineralocorticoid activity and is rapidly cleared from circulation within 4 to 6 hours, making it safe for outpatient testing without risk of sodium retention, fluid overload, or clinically significant blood pressure elevation in patients with hypertension","C":"Dexamethasone is the only synthetic glucocorticoid measured directly by the standard cortisol immunoassay at a known cross-reactivity factor, allowing the laboratory to subtract its known contribution from the 8 AM plasma cortisol result to calculate true endogenous cortisol","D":"Dexamethasone has the same half-life as cortisol but a higher glucocorticoid receptor binding affinity, ensuring that even very small doses maintain high receptor occupancy throughout the overnight period and provide robust suppression of CRH and ACTH secretion","E":"Dexamethasone is selectively concentrated in pituitary corticotroph cells by an active membrane transport mechanism, allowing its small 1-mg dose to achieve selective pituitary ACTH suppression without producing any systemic glucocorticoid effects in peripheral tissues"},"explanation":"Dexamethasone is ideal for suppression testing because: (1) it has ~30x cortisol potency, meaning the small 1-mg dose produces adequate HPA suppression; (2) its long duration of action (24-36 hours, Katzung Table 39–1) ensures overnight suppression; and (3) most cortisol assays do not detect dexamethasone, so the morning plasma cortisol measurement reflects only endogenous adrenal output. If the pituitary-adrenal axis is normal, morning cortisol is usually <2 mcg/dL; in Cushing syndrome it is usually >5 mcg/dL (Katzung Ch 39). Failure to suppress indicates endogenous Cushing syndrome of any cause (pituitary, ectopic, or adrenal); ACTH level and high-dose dexamethasone testing then localize it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal DST response: morning cortisol usually less than 2 mcg/dL after 1 mg overnight dexamethasone (Katzung Ch 39). Failure to suppress = Cushing syndrome until proven otherwise. False positives: depression, alcoholism, obesity, rapid metabolizers. COMLEX: High-dose DST (8 mg) helps distinguish pituitary (suppresses) from ectopic ACTH (does not suppress).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — Dexamethasone pharmacokinetics enabling diagnostic HPA axis suppression testing","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl20_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician must select corticosteroids for three patients: one with primary adrenal insufficiency needing mineralocorticoid replacement, one needing a potent topical preparation for plaque psoriasis with minimal systemic absorption, and one at 31 weeks gestation at risk for preterm delivery. Which of the following correctly matches agents to indications?","choices":{"C":"Fludrocortisone for mineralocorticoid replacement because of its sodium-retaining potency; a potent topical corticosteroid such as clobetasol for psoriasis; and betamethasone or dexamethasone for fetal lung maturation because they cross the placenta and induce surfactant genes","A":"Fludrocortisone for adrenal insufficiency, triamcinolone oral for psoriasis, and hydrocortisone for fetal lung maturation, because hydrocortisone has an intermediate plasma half-life allowing sustained fetal tissue exposure during the final weeks of lung maturation","B":"Hydrocortisone for adrenal insufficiency, prednisone topical for psoriasis, and dexamethasone for fetal lung maturation, because prednisone requires hepatic conversion to prednisolone which occurs too slowly for effective topical glucocorticoid receptor saturation in skin","D":"Dexamethasone for adrenal insufficiency supplemented by high sodium diet, beclomethasone inhaled for psoriasis because of its inhaled anti-inflammatory properties, and fludrocortisone for fetal lung maturation because its high mineralocorticoid potency provides strongest surfactant induction","E":"Prednisone for adrenal insufficiency because its moderate mineralocorticoid activity may provide both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement together, aldosterone topical for psoriasis because mineralocorticoid skin receptors mediate anti-inflammatory effects, and hydrocortisone for fetal lung maturation as the most physiologic glucocorticoid"},"explanation":"Matching steroids to indications: (1) Fludrocortisone is the only clinically available dedicated mineralocorticoid for Addison disease replacement. (2) Topical high-potency corticosteroids (clobetasol, betamethasone dipropionate) are used for psoriasis; first-pass hepatic metabolism of any absorbed fraction limits systemic effects. (3) Betamethasone or dexamethasone for antenatal fetal lung maturation; these are preferred because they cross the placenta without being inactivated by 11-beta-HSD2. Hydrocortisone and prednisolone are inactivated at the placenta.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key glucocorticoid selections: Fludrocortisone = mineralocorticoid replacement; topical corticosteroids (potency ranked: clobetasol > betamethasone > triamcinolone > hydrocortisone); antenatal = betamethasone or dexamethasone. COMLEX: Topical steroid potency matters; highest-potency agents are avoided on face and intertriginous areas due to skin atrophy risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — Glucocorticoid selection based on potency, mineralocorticoid activity, and clinical indication","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Hydrocortisone and prednisolon","E":"Hydrocortisone and prednisolon"}},{"id":"sdl20_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the mechanism of action of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with severe acute asthma exacerbation receives intravenous methylprednisolone in the emergency department. Her bronchospasm does not improve significantly in the first 30 to 60 minutes but shows marked improvement at 4 to 6 hours. Which of the following best explains the delayed clinical response to the intravenous steroid?","choices":{"D":"Methylprednisolone binds cytosolic glucocorticoid receptors that translocate to the nucleus and alter gene transcription, but the resulting changes in inflammatory protein levels such as reduced phospholipase A2 inhibitor synthesis and decreased cytokine production require hours of sequential mRNA transcription and protein translation before clinical improvement occurs","A":"Methylprednisolone must first be transported to the adrenal gland to amplify endogenous cortisol secretion, and the delay reflects the time required for the HPA axis to increase cortisol production to the supraphysiologic concentrations needed for bronchial smooth muscle relaxation","B":"Methylprednisolone accumulates in the plasma membrane lipid bilayer for several hours before diffusing into the cytosol, and this membrane sequestration phase delays receptor binding and prevents clinical benefit until adequate intracellular drug concentrations are achieved","C":"Methylprednisolone competitively displaces endogenous cortisol from glucocorticoid receptors in bronchial smooth muscle, and this displacement requires several hours because endogenous cortisol has very high receptor binding affinity and does not readily dissociate from occupied receptors","E":"Methylprednisolone requires hepatic conversion to an active sulfated metabolite before crossing the blood-bronchial epithelial barrier, and this biotransformation is rate-limited by available CYP3A4 enzyme capacity in hepatocytes, causing the delayed onset relative to inhaled bronchodilators"},"explanation":"The delayed onset of glucocorticoid action (hours) reflects the genomic mechanism: nuclear translocation, GRE binding, mRNA transcription, and protein translation must all occur before anti-inflammatory proteins are present at meaningful concentrations. For asthma, key induced proteins include lipocortin-1 (inhibits PLA2), IkappaB-alpha (inhibits NF-kappaB), and glucocorticoid-induced leucine zipper protein. For acute bronchospasm, short-acting beta-2 agonists provide immediate relief, while steroids are given simultaneously to prevent worsening and recurrence hours later.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoids take hours to work (genomic mechanism, new protein synthesis). Always combine with rapid-acting bronchodilator in acute asthma. IV steroids are not faster-acting than oral in equivalent doses; both work through the same nuclear receptor mechanism. COMLEX: Some rapid non-genomic effects exist (membrane GR) but these are not the basis of clinical anti-inflammatory effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — Glucocorticoid nuclear receptor mechanism causing delayed anti-inflammatory protein synthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl20_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the mechanism of action of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A nephrologist notes that pharmacologic doses of cortisol cause sodium retention in some patients despite cortisol binding primarily to glucocorticoid rather than mineralocorticoid receptors. Which enzymatic mechanism explains why endogenous cortisol can act as a mineralocorticoid in the renal collecting duct when present in excess?","choices":{"A":"Renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 normally converts cortisone to active cortisol in peripheral tissues, and overexpression of this enzyme in collecting duct cells at supraphysiologic cortisol concentrations amplifies the glucocorticoid signal beyond glucocorticoid receptor capacity, spilling over to mineralocorticoid receptor activation","B":"Renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 normally converts cortisol to inactive cortisone in mineralocorticoid-sensitive collecting duct cells, protecting the receptors; when this enzyme is deficient or overwhelmed by cortisol excess, cortisol directly activates mineralocorticoid receptors and causes sodium retention","C":"CYP11B2 aldosterone synthase in the zona glomerulosa is stimulated by supraphysiologic cortisol concentrations reaching the adrenal via the portal circulation, causing secondary aldosterone hypersecretion that acts on collecting duct mineralocorticoid receptors independently of the direct cortisol stimulus","D":"Supraphysiologic cortisol doses saturate plasma cortisol-binding globulin capacity entirely, releasing free cortisol at concentrations that overwhelm renal tubular cell membrane transport proteins and cause extracellular cortisol accumulation that mimics aldosterone through a non-receptor-mediated ion channel mechanism","E":"Mineralocorticoid receptors in the collecting duct are not expressed under basal conditions and require upregulation by prior aldosterone exposure; supraphysiologic cortisol induces this receptor expression, allowing subsequent cortisol-mediated sodium retention through newly expressed mineralocorticoid receptors"},"explanation":"11-beta-HSD2 is expressed in mineralocorticoid-sensitive tissues (kidney, colon, salivary glands) specifically to protect MRs from cortisol. Because cortisol circulates at ~2000x the concentration of aldosterone and has equal MR binding affinity, without 11-beta-HSD2, cortisol would dominate all MR binding. When 11-beta-HSD2 is inhibited (licorice/carbenoxolone, which inhibit the enzyme) or overwhelmed (ectopic ACTH syndrome with very high cortisol), cortisol acts as a potent mineralocorticoid. This is called apparent mineralocorticoid excess syndrome when congenital.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 11-beta-HSD2 = \"cortisol shield\" in kidney; converts cortisol to inactive cortisone to protect mineralocorticoid receptors. Classic drug that inhibits 11-beta-HSD2: licorice/glycyrrhizin. Congenital 11-beta-HSD2 deficiency = apparent mineralocorticoid excess syndrome. COMLEX: Ectopic ACTH producing very high cortisol levels causes hypokalemia and HTN through this mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — Tissue-specific cortisol inactivation by 11-beta-HSD2 controlling mineralocorticoid receptor selectivity","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl20_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the mechanism of action of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with Cushing syndrome from an adrenal adenoma undergoes successful laparoscopic adrenalectomy. Her postoperative course is complicated by fatigue, hypotension, and hyponatremia with a normal serum potassium. Which of the following best explains her postoperative adrenal insufficiency despite removal of only a cortisol-secreting tumor?","choices":{"B":"Chronic cortisol excess from the adenoma suppressed CRH and ACTH, causing atrophy of the zona fasciculata and reticularis of the contralateral gland, so cortisol output is inadequate until the HPA axis recovers; aldosterone is preserved because angiotensin II and K+ drive the zona glomerulosa","A":"The adrenal adenoma suppressed the zona fasciculata and zona reticularis but also damaged the adjacent zona glomerulosa through paracrine cortisol effects, leaving the remaining adrenal tissue permanently unable to produce aldosterone for adequate mineralocorticoid homeostasis","C":"Cortisol at the concentrations produced by the adenoma was continuously occupying mineralocorticoid receptors throughout the body, replacing aldosterone function entirely, and removal of this cortisol source left collecting duct mineralocorticoid receptors without any available ligand for activation","D":"The zona glomerulosa requires cortisol as an obligate cofactor for aldosterone synthesis because PNMT converts a cortisol-derived intermediate to an aldosterone precursor in the mineralocorticoid biosynthetic pathway, and resection removes this essential cortisol cofactor","E":"Glucocorticoid receptor activation in the liver by supraphysiologic cortisol upregulated angiotensinogen synthesis to pathologically high levels, and after adrenalectomy the sudden decrease in angiotensin II leaves the RAAS transiently unable to stimulate adequate aldosterone production"},"explanation":"Chronic cortisol excess from the adenoma suppresses CRH and ACTH, and the ACTH-dependent zona fasciculata and reticularis of the contralateral gland atrophy. Once the adenoma is removed, the remaining cortex cannot make enough cortisol, so she needs hydrocortisone until the HPA axis recovers, which takes months. The zona glomerulosa is regulated mainly by angiotensin II and potassium rather than ACTH, so aldosterone production continues and potassium stays normal; ACTH-suppression (secondary) adrenal insufficiency causes hypotension and hyponatremia without hyperkalemia. Cortisol does not damage the glomerulosa by paracrine action, PNMT is a medullary enzyme with no role in aldosterone synthesis, and the receptor-occupancy and angiotensinogen explanations do not account for a cortisol deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After resecting a cortisol-secreting adrenal adenoma, patients need glucocorticoid replacement because the contralateral zona fasciculata is atrophied from ACTH suppression; aldosterone is usually preserved (RAAS-driven), so hyperkalemia is not expected (FA: secondary AI has no hyperkalemia). Recovery of the HPA axis takes months. COMLEX: Serial morning cortisol and ACTH stimulation testing monitors recovery of adrenal function before tapering replacement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — Glucocorticoid receptor negative feedback causing HPA axis suppression and adrenal atrophy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Cortisol does not damage the g","C":"Cortisol does not damage the g","D":"Cortisol does not damage the g","E":"Cortisol does not damage the g"}},{"id":"sdl20_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the physiologic and metabolic effects of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"An experimental animal undergoes bilateral adrenalectomy and is maintained on a mineralocorticoid with saline drinking water. Compared with intact animals, which of the following responses is diminished and returns to normal when physiologic, rather than pharmacologic, amounts of cortisol are given?","choices":{"A":"The responses of vascular and bronchial smooth muscle to catecholamines, together with catecholamine-driven lipolysis in fat cells","B":"Release of vasopressin from the posterior pituitary, so that the animal can no longer concentrate its urine when deprived of water","C":"Secretion of ACTH by anterior pituitary corticotrophs, because these cells need cortisol in order to synthesize proopiomelanocortin","D":"The blood counts of lymphocytes and eosinophils, because cortisol is needed to move these cells from lymphoid tissue into the blood","E":"Insulin-stimulated glucose uptake by skeletal muscle, because cortisol is needed for glucose transporters to reach the cell membrane"},"explanation":"Many effects of glucocorticoids grow with the dose, but some are permissive: they need only physiologic amounts of cortisol, and without them normal functions become deficient. Katzung gives the classic examples: the response of vascular and bronchial smooth muscle to catecholamines is diminished in the absence of cortisol and restored by physiologic amounts, and the lipolytic responses of fat cells to catecholamines, ACTH and growth hormone are likewise attenuated without glucocorticoids. The other choices move in the wrong direction. Cortisol deficiency augments vasopressin secretion and impairs excretion of a water load. Removing the adrenals removes cortisol negative feedback, so ACTH secretion rises rather than falls. Cortisol lowers the blood lymphocyte and eosinophil counts, largely by moving these cells out of the vascular bed into lymphoid tissue, so their counts do not fall when cortisol is absent. Glucocorticoids inhibit glucose uptake by muscle, so their absence does not reduce it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Permissive effects of cortisol: physiologic cortisol is needed for normal vascular and bronchial smooth-muscle responses to catecholamines and for catecholamine-, ACTH- and GH-stimulated lipolysis. Cortisol deficiency also raises vasopressin and impairs free-water excretion. COMLEX: Glucocorticoids are necessary in acute adrenal insufficiency with life-threatening shock; fluids and a mineralocorticoid do not replace them.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — Permissive effects of physiologic cortisol on catecholamine responses of smooth muscle and fat","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Cortisol deficiency augments v","C":"Removing the adrenals removes "}},{"id":"sdl20_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the physiologic and metabolic effects of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman taking naproxen for knee osteoarthritis is prescribed prednisone 20 mg daily for polymyalgia rheumatica. Two weeks later she presents with epigastric pain and a positive fecal occult blood test. Which of the following best explains the markedly elevated gastrointestinal bleeding risk with this drug combination?","choices":{"E":"Prednisone stimulates gastric acid secretion and impairs mucosal prostaglandin synthesis by inhibiting phospholipase A2, and concurrent naproxen further suppresses prostaglandins through COX inhibition, creating a synergistic loss of mucosal cytoprotection beyond either drug alone","A":"Prednisone directly binds prostaglandin E2 receptors on gastric parietal cells as a competitive antagonist, blocking prostaglandin-mediated bicarbonate secretion and leaving the gastric mucosa without the alkaline layer required to buffer luminal acid independent of naproxen effects","B":"Prednisone increases portal venous pressure through hepatic sinusoidal fibrosis, leading to portal hypertension, gastric mucosal venous congestion, and increased susceptibility of the upper gastrointestinal tract to bleeding from minor mechanical trauma during normal peristalsis","C":"Prednisone stimulates mast cell degranulation in gastric mucosa through glucocorticoid receptor-mediated histamine release, causing parietal cell stimulation that increases hydrochloric acid production independently of any prostaglandin effects and synergizes with naproxen-induced reduced mucosal blood flow","D":"Naproxen inhibits platelet COX-1 only, impairing thromboxane-mediated platelet aggregation, while prednisone depletes clotting factors through hepatic protein synthesis inhibition, creating a combined coagulopathy that produces spontaneous gastric mucosal hemorrhage without a true ulcer"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids impair gastric mucosal defense by inhibiting PLA2 and reducing prostaglandin production, which normally maintain the mucus-bicarbonate barrier, stimulate mucosal blood flow, and inhibit acid secretion. NSAIDs independently reduce prostaglandins through COX inhibition. Together, they produce synergistic depletion of mucosal prostaglandins, creating dramatically increased peptic ulcer and GI bleeding risk. The combination is one of the highest-risk drug interactions in clinical medicine; proton pump inhibitors are routinely co-prescribed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid + NSAID combination = dramatically elevated GI bleed risk. Always add a PPI when combining steroids and NSAIDs. Steroids alone do not cause GI bleeding without risk factors, but the combination is synergistic. COMLEX: Misoprostol (PGE1 analog) can substitute for the lost prostaglandin cytoprotection as an alternative to PPI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — Glucocorticoid gastrointestinal effects amplified by concurrent NSAID-mediated prostaglandin depletion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl20_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the physiologic and metabolic effects of natural and synthetic steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with ectopic ACTH syndrome from a small cell lung carcinoma presents with severe hypokalemia at 2.4 mEq per L, metabolic alkalosis, and hypertension. Her aldosterone level is low. Which of the following best explains the cause of her electrolyte abnormalities?","choices":{"B":"Extremely high cortisol concentrations from ectopic ACTH stimulation overwhelm the capacity of renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 to convert cortisol to inactive cortisone, allowing excess cortisol to directly activate mineralocorticoid receptors in the collecting duct and cause sodium retention and potassium wasting independent of aldosterone","A":"Excess ACTH directly binds mineralocorticoid receptors in the renal collecting duct through molecular mimicry of aldosterone, activating sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion through ENaC channels independently of cortisol levels or aldosterone concentrations","C":"Excess cortisol stimulates hepatic angiotensinogen production to pathologically high levels, causing angiotensin II accumulation that directly activates zona glomerulosa cells producing hyperaldosteronism that is not detected by direct aldosterone measurement because of immunoassay interference","D":"Excess ACTH stimulates adrenocortical production of 11-deoxycorticosterone, which has potent intrinsic mineralocorticoid activity through distinct ENaC channel activation independent of the aldosterone pathway and is not measured by the standard aldosterone immunoassay","E":"Excess cortisol reduces SGK-1 expression in collecting duct cells causing constitutive ENaC sodium channel activation through a mineralocorticoid receptor-independent pathway that is not detectable with aldosterone measurements but is equally responsive to spironolactone"},"explanation":"In ectopic ACTH syndrome, ACTH levels are extremely high, driving adrenal cortisol production very high. This overwhelms the capacity of 11-beta-HSD2 in the kidney to inactivate cortisol. Because cortisol has equal affinity for mineralocorticoid receptors as aldosterone, excess cortisol activates collecting duct MRs, causing Na retention, K wasting, hypertension, and metabolic alkalosis (apparent mineralocorticoid excess). Aldosterone itself is actually suppressed by the high cortisol. DOC excess can contribute in ectopic ACTH syndrome, but DOC acts through the mineralocorticoid receptor rather than through a separate ENaC pathway, and 11-beta-HSD2 overflow is the principal mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ectopic ACTH syndrome = very high cortisol = overwhelmed 11-beta-HSD2 = cortisol activates MR = hypokalemia, alkalosis, HTN, low aldosterone. Classic boards: why is aldosterone LOW in a patient with mineralocorticoid excess signs? Because cortisol is acting as the mineralocorticoid. COMLEX: Treatment includes cortisol synthesis inhibitors like metyrapone or ketoconazole plus addressing the underlying malignancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — Glucocorticoid mineralocorticoid receptor cross-activation at supraphysiologic cortisol concentrations","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Aldosterone itself is actually","D":"DOC excess can contribute in e"}},{"id":"sdl20_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the concept of anabolic and catabolic effects of steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with polymyositis has been treated with prednisone 60 mg daily for 6 months. He now reports progressive proximal muscle weakness. His inflammatory markers are at their nadir, suggesting excellent disease control. Which of the following best explains this new weakness?","choices":{"E":"Steroid myopathy results from glucocorticoid-induced muscle protein catabolism through ubiquitin-proteasome upregulation and inhibition of anabolic protein synthesis, causing selective type 2b fast-twitch fiber atrophy that preferentially affects proximal muscles","A":"Polymyositis has progressed despite apparent biochemical improvement because prednisone suppresses inflammatory markers while masking ongoing cytotoxic T cell attack on muscle fibers, causing continued fiber destruction that only becomes symptomatic when more than 50 percent of fibers are destroyed","B":"Prolonged prednisone use has activated the complement system in muscle, causing complement-mediated destruction of type 1 slow-twitch fibers that are selectively susceptible to glucocorticoid-induced complement deposition at the neuromuscular junction","C":"Prednisone has caused secondary hypothyroidism through HPA axis feedback suppression of TRH and TSH release, and the resulting low thyroid hormone levels produce myxedematous infiltration and reduced ATP production in proximal muscle groups","D":"Prednisone has induced rhabdomyolysis by activating phospholipase C in sarcolemmal membranes, releasing diacylglycerol that causes pathologic calcium influx into type 2 fast-twitch muscle fibers particularly vulnerable to lipid-mediated membrane disruption"},"explanation":"Steroid myopathy is a dose- and duration-dependent catabolic complication of glucocorticoid therapy. It affects type 2b (fast-twitch, glycolytic) fibers selectively, causing proximal muscle wasting through two mechanisms: (1) protein catabolism via ubiquitin-proteasome pathway upregulation; and (2) suppression of protein synthesis (reduced IGF-1, reduced mTOR signaling). The clinical challenge is distinguishing steroid myopathy from active disease flare: in steroid myopathy, CK is normal, EMG may be normal, and reducing steroid dose improves weakness. In polymyositis flare, CK is elevated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroid myopathy = proximal weakness + normal CK + type 2 fiber atrophy on biopsy. Improving with dose reduction distinguishes it from disease relapse. COMLEX: Fluorinated steroids (dexamethasone, triamcinolone) cause more myopathy than non-fluorinated (prednisone, hydrocortisone) because fluorination increases lipophilicity and muscle uptake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — Glucocorticoid catabolic effects on skeletal muscle protein causing steroid myopathy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl20_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the concept of anabolic and catabolic effects of steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman taking methylprednisolone for 2 years for a connective tissue disorder presents with purplish striae on her abdomen and inner thighs, easy bruising, and extremely thin skin. Which of the following mechanisms best explains these integumentary findings?","choices":{"D":"Methylprednisolone inhibits fibroblast proliferation and collagen synthesis in the dermis and promotes protein catabolism that thins the connective tissue matrix and weakens capillary walls, producing striae from torn collagen and bruising from fragile capillaries","A":"Methylprednisolone causes dermal calcium phosphate deposits by inhibiting renal calcium reabsorption and elevating serum phosphate, and deposited calcium phosphate disrupts collagen fiber alignment in the dermis causing striae formation when the skin is mechanically stretched","B":"Methylprednisolone activates dermal mast cell degranulation through glucocorticoid receptors on skin mast cells, releasing histamine and tryptase that degrade type IV collagen in the dermal basement membrane and cause spontaneous purpura from capillary fragility","C":"Methylprednisolone upregulates matrix metalloproteinase expression in all dermal fibroblasts uniformly, causing active degradation of collagen and elastin throughout the dermis with thinning proportional to cumulative steroid dose without the preferential impact on striae-prone areas","E":"Methylprednisolone suppresses epidermal keratinocyte proliferation by blocking growth factor receptor signaling, causing epidermal thinning and complete loss of stratum corneum that exposes subdermal capillaries to minor mechanical trauma producing easy bruising"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids produce dermal catabolic effects through: (1) inhibition of fibroblast proliferation and collagen/glycosaminoglycan synthesis, thinning the dermal connective tissue matrix; (2) protein catabolism reducing structural protein content; and (3) weakening of dermal capillary walls, causing easy bruising. Striae form when skin is mechanically stressed over an atrophied dermis, tearing the thin collagen bundles. The purple color reflects visible dermal capillaries through the thinned, transparent skin. These changes are dose- and duration-dependent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cushing syndrome skin findings: purple striae, thin skin, easy bruising (ecchymoses), poor wound healing. Striae in Cushing are wider and more violaceous than pregnancy striae. COMLEX: Chronic topical steroid application causes similar dermal atrophy, telangiectasias, and striae in the applied area.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — Glucocorticoid catabolic effects on dermal collagen and connective tissue producing skin manifestations","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl20_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the concept of anabolic and catabolic effects of steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man on chronic prednisone therapy undergoes elective inguinal hernia repair. Postoperatively his surgeon notes poor wound healing with partial dehiscence at 2 weeks. Which of the following best explains the glucocorticoid-mediated impairment of this wound?","choices":{"B":"Glucocorticoids impair wound healing by inhibiting fibroblast proliferation and collagen synthesis, suppressing the early inflammatory response that normally attracts growth factor-secreting macrophages to the wound bed, and reducing angiogenesis needed for granulation tissue formation during the proliferative phase of wound repair","A":"Glucocorticoid-induced neutrophilia impairs the early inflammatory phase of wound healing by causing excessive neutrophil-mediated tissue damage and oxidative injury that destroys nascent granulation tissue before fibroblasts can begin laying down new collagen matrix","C":"Prednisone causes zinc and vitamin C deficiency by increasing urinary zinc excretion and reducing intestinal ascorbic acid absorption, both essential cofactors for prolyl hydroxylase in collagen cross-linking, and this nutritional depletion is the primary cause of wound dehiscence","D":"Prednisone-induced hyperglycemia creates an osmotic gradient in wound tissue that draws water away from the healing incision site, desiccating the wound edge and preventing the cell migration required for epithelial closure and dermal fibroblast proliferation","E":"Prednisone reduces hepatic synthesis of fibronectin, an extracellular matrix glycoprotein scaffolding for initial fibroblast migration, and without this scaffolding fibroblasts cannot orient properly within the fibrin clot matrix during the early proliferative healing phase"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids impair all phases of wound healing: (1) inflammatory phase: suppress macrophage function, reducing growth factor (PDGF, TGF-beta, FGF) production needed to recruit fibroblasts; (2) proliferative phase: inhibit fibroblast proliferation and migration, reduce collagen type I and III synthesis, impair angiogenesis; (3) remodeling phase: reduce collagen cross-linking and tensile strength. The result is delayed wound closure, reduced tensile strength, and increased dehiscence risk. Vitamin A supplementation partially reverses glucocorticoid wound healing impairment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroids impair all three wound healing phases (inflammation, proliferation, remodeling). Vitamin A can partially reverse steroid-induced wound healing impairment. Clinically relevant: perioperative steroid management and wound care. COMLEX: Hold topical steroids around wound areas; systemic steroids may require stress dosing perioperatively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — Glucocorticoid catabolic effects on fibroblast function and wound healing phases","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl20_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has an acutely swollen, warm right knee with a large effusion. She is started on high-dose prednisone, and within 24 hours the swelling and warmth begin to subside. Which of the following early actions of cortisol most directly reduces the leakage of plasma into her inflamed joint tissues?","choices":{"E":"It raises the circulating lymphocyte count, so that more T cells reach the joint and clear away the damaged synovial tissue faster","A":"It blocks the reaction between antigen and antibody in the synovium, so that no immune complexes form to set off the inflammation","B":"It irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase in synovial cells, so that no prostaglandin is made until new enzyme has been synthesized","C":"It dilates the synovial arterioles, washing histamine and bradykinin out of the joint before they can act on the local capillaries","D":"It stabilizes lysosomal membranes, so injured cells release less proteolytic enzyme, and capillary permeability falls as a result"},"explanation":"Guyton lists the actions by which cortisol prevents inflammation. Stabilizing lysosomal membranes is described as one of the most important: the membranes of the intracellular lysosomes rupture far less easily, so damaged cells release much smaller amounts of the proteolytic enzymes that drive inflammation. Cortisol also decreases capillary permeability, probably as a secondary effect of this reduced enzyme release, and this limits the loss of plasma into the tissues. Katzung adds that glucocorticoids reduce capillary permeability by decreasing histamine release from basophils and mast cells. Cortisol does not affect the basic reaction between antigen and antibody; it blocks the inflammatory response that follows. Irreversible acetylation of cyclooxygenase is the action of aspirin; glucocorticoids instead reduce prostaglandin and leukotriene formation by inhibiting phospholipase A2 and lowering COX-2 expression. Increased blood flow to the inflamed area is one of the stages of inflammation, not an anti-inflammatory action. Cortisol lowers the blood lymphocyte count and suppresses T-cell reproduction rather than raising them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's anti-inflammatory actions of cortisol: stabilizes lysosomal membranes, decreases capillary permeability, decreases white-cell migration and phagocytosis, suppresses lymphocyte (especially T-cell) reproduction, and attenuates fever by reducing interleukin-1 release. COMLEX: Glucocorticoids usually begin to quiet severe local inflammation, as in rheumatoid arthritis or acute glomerulonephritis, within about 24 hours, but they do not correct the underlying disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — Cortisol stabilization of lysosomal membranes and reduced capillary permeability in early inflammation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"C":"Increased blood flow to the in"}},{"id":"sdl20_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who received a cadaveric kidney transplant is maintained on tacrolimus, mycophenolate mofetil, and prednisone. Six months after his prednisone is tapered and discontinued, his serum creatinine rises from 1.3 to 2.8 mg per dL. A biopsy reveals lymphocytic infiltration of tubules consistent with acute cellular rejection. Which aspect of glucocorticoid immunosuppression was most responsible for the protection lost after prednisone discontinuation?","choices":{"D":"Glucocorticoid-mediated suppression of IL-2 and other T cell cytokines required for lymphocyte proliferation, combined with inhibition of leukocyte migration into the allograft and suppression of effector T cell-mediated tubular injury, was lost upon steroid discontinuation","A":"Glucocorticoid-mediated suppression of complement activation in the transplanted kidney prevented membrane attack complex deposition on graft endothelial cells, and this was the primary anti-rejection mechanism not replaced by tacrolimus or mycophenolate in the absence of prednisone","B":"Glucocorticoid-mediated inhibition of natural killer cell cytotoxicity against donor HLA-mismatched tubular epithelial cells was the dominant anti-rejection pathway that tacrolimus and mycophenolate inadequately suppress, leading to NK cell-mediated tubular injury after prednisone withdrawal","C":"Glucocorticoid-mediated inhibition of B cell antibody production against donor HLA antigens prevented antibody-mediated rejection with C4d peritubular capillary deposition and endothelialitis, a form of rejection not controlled by tacrolimus or mycophenolate monotherapy","E":"Glucocorticoid-mediated upregulation of regulatory T cell function in the graft created operational tolerance that was lost when prednisone was tapered, allowing donor-specific T cell clones that were suppressed but not deleted to become fully reactivated and attack the graft"},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids contribute to transplant immunosuppression by: (1) blocking cytokine gene transcription (IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, TNF-alpha, IFN-gamma) through NF-kappaB inhibition and GRE-mediated transcriptional suppression; (2) inhibiting leukocyte migration to the graft through reduced adhesion molecule expression; (3) promoting lymphocyte apoptosis; and (4) suppressing macrophage antigen-presenting function. The acute cellular rejection pattern (lymphocytic tubulitis) seen here reflects T cell-mediated allograft injury, which glucocorticoids suppress through anti-T cell mechanisms. Tacrolimus and mycophenolate provide complementary but incomplete coverage without steroid synergy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroid-free transplant protocols have become more common but carry increased rejection risk. Glucocorticoids in transplant = anti-T cell (anti-cytokine, anti-migration) = prevent acute cellular rejection. COMLEX: Rejection treatment uses high-dose IV methylprednisolone pulses as first-line therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — Glucocorticoid immunosuppression preventing T cell-mediated allograft rejection","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl20_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressing effects of steroids.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man taking prednisone 40 mg daily for polymyalgia rheumatica develops a productive cough, and a chest radiograph shows right lower lobe consolidation. His highest recorded temperature is 37.6°C (99.7°F). His physician explains that high-dose glucocorticoids blunt the febrile response to infection. Which mechanism best explains this blunting?","choices":{"C":"Prednisone reduces release of interleukin-1 from white blood cells, removing a main excitant of the hypothalamic temperature-control center","A":"Prednisone blocks the antigen-antibody reaction itself, so the immune complexes that would act as pyrogens are not formed in the infected lung","B":"Prednisone increases capillary permeability in the inflamed lung, so circulating pyrogens leak out of the blood before they can reach the brain","D":"Prednisone causes neutropenia by suppressing the bone marrow, removing the neutrophils that release pyrogens at the site of the infection","E":"Prednisone irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase in the hypothalamus, so prostaglandin E2 can no longer raise the temperature set point"},"explanation":"Cortisol attenuates fever mainly because it reduces the release of interleukin-1 from white blood cells; interleukin-1 is one of the principal excitants of the hypothalamic temperature-control system, and the lower temperature in turn reduces vasodilation. Patients on high-dose glucocorticoids can therefore have a serious infection with little fever. The basic antigen-antibody reaction is not affected by cortisol; what cortisol blocks is the inflammatory response that follows it. Cortisol decreases capillary permeability rather than increasing it. Glucocorticoids raise the neutrophil count while lowering lymphocytes, eosinophils, basophils and monocytes. Irreversible acetylation of cyclooxygenase is how aspirin acts; glucocorticoids instead induce an inhibitor of phospholipase A2 and decrease the mRNA for cyclooxygenase-2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoid anti-inflammatory actions: stabilize lysosomal membranes, decrease capillary permeability, decrease leukocyte migration and phagocytosis, suppress T lymphocytes, and reduce interleukin-1 release (blunting fever); biochemically they induce a phospholipase A2 inhibitor, decrease COX-2 mRNA, and lower IL-2, IL-3 and platelet-activating factor. COMLEX: High-dose glucocorticoids depress immunity and can allow fulminating infection, including reactivation of previously arrested tuberculosis, with few warning signs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — Glucocorticoid immunosuppressive effects on leukocyte distribution and survival patterns","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"Glucocorticoids raise the neut"}}],"21":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain clinically relevant anatomy, histology and physiology of the adrenal gland.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the hormone product and primary regulatory stimulus of the outermost zone of the adrenal cortex?","choices":{"E":"The zona glomerulosa produces aldosterone and is regulated primarily by angiotensin II and elevated serum potassium rather than by ACTH.","A":"The zona glomerulosa produces cortisol and is regulated primarily by ACTH from the anterior pituitary rather than by angiotensin II.","B":"The zona fasciculata produces aldosterone and is regulated primarily by angiotensin II rather than by pituitary-derived ACTH stimulation.","C":"The zona reticularis produces aldosterone and is regulated primarily by renin released from juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney.","D":"The zona glomerulosa produces DHEA and is regulated primarily by ACTH from the anterior pituitary rather than by the RAAS."},"explanation":"The adrenal cortex is divided into three concentric zones. The outermost zona glomerulosa produces mineralocorticoids, primarily aldosterone, and is regulated by angiotensin II and hyperkalemia — not by ACTH. The middle zona fasciculata produces glucocorticoids (cortisol) under ACTH control. The innermost zona reticularis produces androgens (DHEA, androstenedione) also under ACTH control. The mnemonic \"GFR\" (glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis) paired with \"Salt, Sugar, Sex\" maps to the three zones from outer to inner.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The zona glomerulosa is the only cortical zone that does NOT require ACTH — it is governed by the RAAS and serum K+. A classic trap: exogenous steroid use suppresses ACTH but does NOT suppress aldosterone. COMLEX: Adrenal pathology can produce viscerosomatic reflexes along the adrenal sympathetic supply (T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5; the OMM atlas gives sympathetic T7–T11 and an adrenal viscerosomatic level of T8–T10); Chapman reflex for the adrenals (course chart): anterior, about 2.5 in above the kidney point, lateral and superior to the umbilicus at 11 and 1 o'clock; posterior, the T11–T12 intertransverse spaces (An Osteopathic Approach to Diagnosis and Treatment Ch 22: between the T11 spinous and T12 transverse processes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Three zones of the adrenal cortex and medulla as distinct neuroendocrine structures","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain clinically relevant anatomy, histology and physiology of the adrenal gland.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the embryologic origin of adrenal medullary chromaffin cells and their primary secretory product?","choices":{"C":"Chromaffin cells are derived from neural crest ectoderm and constitute the adrenal medulla, which produces catecholamines — primarily epinephrine.","A":"Chromaffin cells are derived from lateral plate mesoderm and constitute the adrenal medulla, which produces catecholamines — primarily norepinephrine.","B":"Chromaffin cells are derived from intermediate mesoderm and constitute the adrenal medulla, which produces cortisol under direct neural stimulation.","D":"Chromaffin cells are derived from endoderm and constitute the adrenal cortex, which produces aldosterone under neural stimulation from preganglionic fibers.","E":"Chromaffin cells are derived from surface ectoderm and constitute the adrenal medulla, which produces corticotropin-releasing hormone under hypothalamic control."},"explanation":"The adrenal medulla is composed of chromaffin cells derived from neural crest ectoderm — the same lineage as postganglionic sympathetic neurons. They are innervated by preganglionic cholinergic fibers and secrete catecholamines directly into the bloodstream. In humans, approximately 80% of medullary output is epinephrine and 20% is norepinephrine. The adrenal cortex, by contrast, arises from intermediate mesoderm (coelomic epithelium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neural crest origin is a classic exam distinction — phaeochromocytoma, neuroblastoma, and paraganglioma all arise from neural crest derivatives. Adrenal cortical tumors arise from mesoderm. COMLEX: The adrenal medulla functions as a modified sympathetic ganglion; preganglionic fibers from the thoracic splanchnic nerves (cord segments T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5; T7–T11 in the OMM atlas) synapse directly on chromaffin cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Three zones of the adrenal cortex and medulla as distinct neuroendocrine structures","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"In humans, approximately 80% o"}},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain clinically relevant anatomy, histology and physiology of the adrenal gland.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the secretory product and histologic appearance of the zona reticularis, the innermost zone of the adrenal cortex?","choices":{"D":"The zona reticularis produces adrenal androgens such as DHEA and androstenedione, and its cells form irregular anastomosing cords with compact eosinophilic cytoplasm.","A":"The zona reticularis produces aldosterone and histologically shows cells arranged in rounded glomerular clusters with pale cytoplasm under angiotensin II control.","B":"The zona reticularis produces cortisol and histologically shows cells arranged in long straight parallel cords with abundant clear lipid-rich vacuolated cytoplasm.","C":"The zona reticularis produces epinephrine and histologically shows polygonal chromaffin cells arranged in nests surrounded by fibrovascular sustentacular stroma.","E":"The zona reticularis produces mineralocorticoids including fludrocortisone and histologically shows cells arranged in irregular reticular networks with foamy cytoplasm."},"explanation":"The zona reticularis is the innermost cortical zone, lying immediately above the medulla. It produces androgens (DHEA and androstenedione) under ACTH regulation; these androgens can be converted peripherally to testosterone and estrogens. Histologically it shows cells in irregular anastomosing (reticular) cords with compact, lipid-poor eosinophilic cytoplasm — contrasting with the lipid-laden vacuolated cells of the middle zona fasciculata. The zona glomerulosa produces aldosterone, and the medulla produces epinephrine/norepinephrine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adrenal androgens from the zona reticularis drive adrenarche and contribute to PCOS. In CAH with 21-hydroxylase deficiency, shunting into this pathway explains virilization. COMLEX: Adrenal disease can produce viscerosomatic findings along the adrenal sympathetic supply (T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5; the OMM atlas gives sympathetic T7–T11 and an adrenal viscerosomatic level of T8–T10); Chapman reflex for gonads is relevant when adrenal androgens are elevated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Three zones of the adrenal cortex and medulla as distinct neuroendocrine structures","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"The zona glomerulosa produces ","B":"Histologically it shows cells ","C":"The zona glomerulosa produces "}},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pituitary-adrenal axis and its control and how plasma concentrations of cortisol and aldosterone are controlled.","batch":1,"stem":"CRH released from the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus acts on which anterior pituitary cell type, and what is the immediate result of that interaction?","choices":{"C":"CRH binds receptors on gonadotrophs of the anterior pituitary, stimulating LH and FSH secretion which indirectly suppress adrenal androgen production.","A":"CRH binds receptors on somatotrophs of the anterior pituitary, stimulating growth hormone secretion that synergizes with cortisol to regulate gluconeogenesis.","B":"CRH binds receptors on thyrotrophs of the anterior pituitary, stimulating TSH secretion which cross-reacts with adrenal ACTH receptors at high concentrations.","D":"CRH binds receptors on corticotrophs of the anterior pituitary, stimulating POMC cleavage to ACTH, which is then released into the systemic circulation.","E":"CRH binds receptors on lactotrophs of the anterior pituitary, stimulating prolactin secretion that enhances adrenal responsiveness to ACTH."},"explanation":"CRH is released from the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus and travels through the hypophyseal portal system to bind CRH receptors on corticotrophs of the anterior pituitary. This stimulates processing of POMC (pro-opiomelanocortin) into ACTH, beta-endorphin, and MSH. ACTH then travels to the adrenal cortex where it stimulates cortisol synthesis and secretion from the zona fasciculata. Cortisol exerts negative feedback at both the hypothalamus (suppressing CRH) and the pituitary (suppressing ACTH).","boardPrep":"Step 1: POMC is the precursor cleaved into ACTH and MSH — which is why ACTH excess in Addison disease causes hyperpigmentation (MSH co-release). This is the classic trap: high ACTH, not low cortisol, causes the tan skin. COMLEX: The CRH-ACTH-cortisol axis is essential for the body's response to somatic and visceral stressors; osteopathic understanding of neuroendocrine-immune axis connects to HPA axis dysregulation in chronic pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis CRH/ACTH/cortisol negative feedback loop and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pituitary-adrenal axis and its control and how plasma concentrations of cortisol and aldosterone are controlled.","batch":1,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has hypertension, and his father also has hypertension. Plasma aldosterone is elevated and plasma renin activity is suppressed. Several weeks of low-dose dexamethasone normalize his aldosterone and blood pressure. Genetic testing shows a chimeric gene formed by a rearrangement on chromosome 8. Which mechanism explains how aldosterone secretion is controlled in this patient?","choices":{"B":"Aldosterone synthase is placed under the ACTH-responsive CYP11B1 promoter, so ACTH drives aldosterone and glucocorticoid feedback lowers it","A":"A somatic KCNJ5 mutation depolarizes glomerulosa cells, so aldosterone secretion is autonomous and does not change with dexamethasone","C":"Aldosterone synthase is placed under an angiotensin II-responsive promoter, so ACE inhibitors rather than glucocorticoids suppress aldosterone","D":"Loss of 11-beta-HSD2 lets cortisol occupy mineralocorticoid receptors, so dexamethasone lowers blood pressure by suppressing cortisol","E":"21-hydroxylase deficiency shunts precursors into aldosterone synthesis, so dexamethasone corrects it by restoring cortisol feedback on ACTH"},"explanation":"Robbins describes familial hyperaldosteronism, about 5 percent of primary hyperaldosteronism, with type I, glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism, as the most common subtype. It stems from a chromosome 8 rearrangement that places CYP11B2, the gene for aldosterone synthase, under the control of the corticotropin-responsive CYP11B1 promoter, so corticotropin stimulates aldosterone synthase from the chimeric gene. Suppressing ACTH with a glucocorticoid therefore lowers aldosterone, which normally is regulated mainly by angiotensin II and potassium rather than ACTH. Somatic KCNJ5 mutations occur in up to half of aldosterone-producing adenomas, are not inherited, and are not glucocorticoid-suppressible. An angiotensin II-responsive promoter is the normal arrangement, not a chimeric defect. Loss of 11-beta-HSD2 causes mineralocorticoid excess with low aldosterone. 21-hydroxylase deficiency lowers aldosterone rather than raising it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary hyperaldosteronism (high aldosterone, low renin) causes (Robbins): bilateral idiopathic hyperplasia (about 60%), aldosterone-producing adenoma or Conn syndrome (about 35%; up to 50% carry KCNJ5 mutations), familial forms (about 5%; type I = glucocorticoid-remediable, CYP11B2 under the CYP11B1 promoter). COMLEX: Primary hyperaldosteronism is the most common cause of secondary hypertension (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — aldosterone secretion is regulated mainly by angiotensin II and potassium","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"An angiotensin II-responsive p"}},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pituitary-adrenal axis and its control and how plasma concentrations of cortisol and aldosterone are controlled.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the normal circadian pattern of cortisol secretion in a healthy adult?","choices":{"A":"Cortisol peaks in early morning around 8 AM and reaches its nadir in late evening, driven by circadian CRH and ACTH pulses that peak before awakening.","B":"Cortisol peaks at midnight and reaches its nadir in the afternoon, driven by nocturnal growth hormone pulses that directly stimulate adrenal cortisol synthesis.","C":"Cortisol is secreted uniformly throughout the day with no circadian variation because ACTH is released tonically under constant hypothalamic CRH stimulation.","D":"Cortisol peaks at noon in response to meal-stimulated gastric hormones and reaches its nadir in the early morning before the first meal of the day.","E":"Cortisol peaks in late evening at 9 PM and reaches its nadir at midnight, driven by sleep-stage-related cortical activity patterns in the hypothalamus."},"explanation":"Cortisol follows a robust circadian rhythm controlled by the suprachiasmatic nucleus. CRH and ACTH pulses rise in the early morning, so plasma cortisol peaks at about 20 μg/dL an hour before arising (Guyton Ch 78). The nadir, about 5 μg/dL, occurs around midnight. This pattern has clinical importance: morning cortisol tests should ideally be drawn at 8 AM. Loss of this diurnal rhythm (e.g., persistently elevated midnight cortisol) is an early sign of Cushing syndrome. Exogenous glucocorticoids blunt this rhythm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of diurnal variation is one of the earliest signs of Cushing syndrome. Late-night salivary cortisol is a sensitive screening test. The 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test gives dexamethasone at 11 PM and measures cortisol the next morning; normal is usually below 2 mcg/dL (Katzung Ch 39). COMLEX: Adrenal fatigue and HPA axis dysregulation are frequent topics in osteopathic preventive medicine; circadian disruption from shift work elevates cortisol nadirs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis CRH/ACTH/cortisol negative feedback loop and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme deficiency is responsible for the most common form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, and which two hormones are deficient as a result?","choices":{"C":"21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common form of CAH, causing deficiency of both cortisol and aldosterone and accumulation of 17-hydroxyprogesterone.","A":"11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common form of CAH, causing deficiency of cortisol only while aldosterone is overproduced from accumulated DOC precursors.","B":"17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common form of CAH, causing deficiency of cortisol and sex steroids while aldosterone is overproduced from DOC.","D":"3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency is the most common form of CAH, causing deficiency of all adrenal steroids including cortisol, aldosterone, and androgens.","E":"Cholesterol desmolase deficiency is the most common form of CAH, causing lipoid hyperplasia with deficiency of all steroid hormones including epinephrine."},"explanation":"21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2) deficiency accounts for over 90% of CAH cases (Robbins Ch 24). This enzyme is required to convert 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol (in the cortisol pathway) and progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone (in the aldosterone pathway). Its absence blocks both cortisol and aldosterone synthesis, causing cortisol deficiency (with compensatory ACTH rise) and, in the classic salt-wasting form, mineralocorticoid deficiency. Precursors accumulate and are shunted into the androgen pathway, causing virilization. 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency is the second most common form and causes hypertension due to DOC accumulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-yield lab in 21-hydroxylase deficiency: markedly elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone — this is the diagnostic marker. Newborn screening now tests for 17-OHP. Treatment is hydrocortisone (replaces cortisol, suppresses ACTH, and reduces virilization). Salt-wasting crises in neonates require both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid (fludrocortisone) replacement. COMLEX: CAH can produce somatic dysfunction along the adrenal sympathetic supply (T10–L1 per Moore Ch 5; the OMM atlas gives sympathetic T7–T11 and an adrenal viscerosomatic level of T8–T10) through viscerosomatic reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zonation; CAH disrupts zona fasciculata and glomerulosa biosynthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of laboratory findings is most characteristic of the classic salt-wasting form of 21-hydroxylase deficiency in a neonate?","choices":{"A":"Hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia, and markedly elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone are characteristic of salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency.","B":"Hypernatremia, hypokalemia, normal glucose, and elevated 11-deoxycortisol are characteristic of salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency.","C":"Hyponatremia, hypokalemia, hypoglycemia, and elevated androstenedione with normal 17-hydroxyprogesterone are characteristic of this enzyme deficiency.","D":"Hypernatremia, hyperkalemia, normal glucose, and elevated cortisol with suppressed ACTH are characteristic of classic salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency.","E":"Hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hyperglycemia, and suppressed ACTH with elevated aldosterone are characteristic of classic salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency."},"explanation":"In salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the inability to synthesize both cortisol and aldosterone produces: hyponatremia and hyperkalemia from aldosterone deficiency (inability to reabsorb Na+ or excrete K+), hypoglycemia from cortisol deficiency (impaired gluconeogenesis), and hypotension from volume depletion. Elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone is the diagnostic biomarker, as this precursor accumulates proximal to the blocked step. ACTH is markedly elevated due to absent cortisol feedback. Newborns present with vomiting, poor feeding, shock, and adrenal crisis in the first 2 weeks of life.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish from hypoaldosteronism alone (type 4 RTA, which is hyperkalemia + metabolic acidosis without hypoglycemia). The triad of hyponatremia + hyperkalemia + hypoglycemia in a neonate should immediately trigger evaluation for CAH. COMLEX: Salt-wasting crisis presents with dehydration, muscle weakness, and hypotension; osteopathic examination may reveal adrenal Chapman reflex tenderness. Chapman reflex for the adrenals (course chart): anterior, about 2.5 in above the kidney point, lateral and superior to the umbilicus at 11 and 1 o'clock; posterior, the T11–T12 intertransverse spaces (An Osteopathic Approach to Diagnosis and Treatment Ch 22: between the T11 spinous and T12 transverse processes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zonation; CAH disrupts zona fasciculata and glomerulosa biosynthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"In a 46,XX neonate with ambiguous genitalia and salt-wasting CAH due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency, which mechanism is most directly responsible for the virilization of the external genitalia?","choices":{"D":"Cortisol and aldosterone precursors such as 17-hydroxyprogesterone accumulate behind the block and are shunted into androgen synthesis, causing androgen excess in utero.","A":"Elevated ACTH directly binds androgen receptors in the developing external genitalia, causing clitoromegaly and labial fusion independent of adrenal steroid biosynthesis.","B":"Cortisol deficiency permits CRH to directly stimulate adrenal androgen production through a CRH receptor expressed on zona reticularis cells bypassing ACTH.","C":"Mineralocorticoid excess from DOC accumulation cross-reacts with androgen receptors in the urogenital sinus, causing labial fusion and clitoromegaly during organogenesis.","E":"Elevated hypothalamic CRH directly stimulates gonadal testosterone production by cross-reacting with LH receptors during the critical window of genital differentiation."},"explanation":"When 21-hydroxylase is absent, the synthetic pathway for cortisol and aldosterone is blocked, causing massive accumulation of upstream precursors: progesterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone. These precursors are shunted into the only remaining open pathway — androgen synthesis — via 17,20-lyase to androstenedione and then testosterone. In a 46,XX fetus, this androgen excess during the critical window of external genitalia formation (weeks 7 to 12, Langman Ch 16) causes clitoromegaly and variable labial fusion, producing ambiguous genitalia at birth. The adrenal glands are also grossly enlarged (hyperplasia) due to chronic ACTH hyperstimulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The key concept is enzymatic shunting — a blockade at one step increases substrate for alternative pathways. This is why 21-hydroxylase deficiency causes virilization (androgen excess) despite cortisol deficiency. 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency also causes virilization by the same mechanism, plus hypertension from DOC accumulation. COMLEX: Ambiguous genitalia is a medical emergency requiring urgent karyotype, hormonal, and imaging evaluation; delay in diagnosis risks adrenal crisis in salt-wasters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zonation; CAH disrupts zona fasciculata and glomerulosa biosynthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss the following adrenal disorders in terms of etiology, pathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and management: Chronic adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), Acute adrenal apoplexy (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome), Cushing syndrome, Primary aldosteronism, Secondary aldosteronism.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) results in which characteristic combination of electrolyte and hormonal abnormalities?","choices":{"B":"Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia result from aldosterone deficiency, while hypoglycemia and hypotension result from cortisol deficiency, and ACTH is markedly elevated.","A":"Hypernatremia and hypokalemia result from excessive ACTH stimulation of residual mineralocorticoid receptors, and cortisol is paradoxically elevated due to receptor hypersensitivity.","C":"Hypernatremia and hyperkalemia result from excess CRH driving residual zona glomerulosa activity, and cortisol is low while ACTH is suppressed by a pituitary adenoma.","D":"Hyponatremia and hypokalemia result from cortisol deficiency impairing renal free water clearance, while aldosterone is preserved and ACTH is only mildly elevated.","E":"Hypernatremia and hypokalemia result from elevated ACTH cross-reacting with mineralocorticoid receptors, causing sodium retention despite absent cortisol secretion."},"explanation":"In primary adrenal insufficiency, destruction of the adrenal cortex eliminates both cortisol and aldosterone. Aldosterone deficiency causes hyponatremia (loss of Na+ in urine), hyperkalemia (K+ retention), and volume depletion/hypotension. Cortisol deficiency impairs gluconeogenesis (hypoglycemia), reduces vascular responsiveness to catecholamines, and impairs free water excretion (contributing to hyponatremia). With no cortisol feedback, both CRH and ACTH rise markedly. The elevated ACTH and co-released MSH cause the characteristic bronze hyperpigmentation of Addison disease — distinguishing it from secondary adrenal insufficiency where ACTH is low.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperpigmentation differentiates primary from secondary adrenal insufficiency. In secondary (pituitary), ACTH is low so there is no MSH-driven pigmentation, and aldosterone is relatively preserved (RAAS intact). Classic Addison triad: weakness, hyperpigmentation, hyponatremia/hyperkalemia. COMLEX: Addison crisis can be triggered by surgery, infection, or trauma; osteopathic perioperative assessment should include adrenal stress-dose coverage for patients on chronic steroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis suppression by exogenous steroids and RAAS regulation of aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss the following adrenal disorders in terms of etiology, pathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and management: Chronic adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), Acute adrenal apoplexy (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome), Cushing syndrome, Primary aldosteronism, Secondary aldosteronism.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination of findings is present in primary adrenal insufficiency but absent in secondary adrenal insufficiency, and explains the distinction between these two conditions?","choices":{"C":"Hyperpigmentation and mineralocorticoid deficiency with hyperkalemia occur only in primary adrenal insufficiency because ACTH and MSH are elevated and both cortex zones are destroyed.","A":"Hypoglycemia and fatigue are present only in primary adrenal insufficiency because secondary adrenal insufficiency involves isolated ACTH deficiency without any glucocorticoid reduction.","B":"Elevated ACTH levels distinguish secondary adrenal insufficiency because the pituitary compensates maximally when cortisol falls below a critical threshold in pituitary disease.","D":"Low ACTH levels occur only in primary adrenal insufficiency because the hypothalamus also becomes deficient in CRH from negative feedback when adrenal cortisol is absent.","E":"Hyponatremia and weakness occur only in secondary adrenal insufficiency because SIADH develops from posterior pituitary dysfunction in combined anterior-posterior pituitary disease."},"explanation":"Primary adrenal insufficiency uniquely features both glucocorticoid AND mineralocorticoid deficiency (hyperkalemia, salt-wasting) plus hyperpigmentation from elevated ACTH/MSH. Secondary adrenal insufficiency (pituitary origin) has low ACTH and low cortisol, but aldosterone is relatively preserved because the RAAS remains intact. Therefore hyperkalemia is NOT a feature of secondary adrenal insufficiency. Hyperpigmentation also does not occur because ACTH (and co-released MSH) is low in secondary disease. Hyponatremia can occur in both, but via different mechanisms: salt-wasting (primary) vs. SIADH-like impaired water excretion (secondary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-yield table: Primary AI — low Na, high K, high ACTH, hyperpigmentation, hypotension. Secondary AI — low-normal Na, normal K, low ACTH, no pigmentation, no aldosterone deficiency. Tertiary AI (hypothalamic) = same as secondary. COMLEX: Secondary AI from pituitary disease often accompanies other pituitary hormone deficiencies; evaluate the whole axis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis suppression by exogenous steroids and RAAS regulation of aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Secondary adrenal insufficienc","D":"Secondary adrenal insufficienc","E":"Hyponatremia can occur in both"}},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss the following adrenal disorders in terms of etiology, pathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and management: Chronic adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), Acute adrenal apoplexy (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome), Cushing syndrome, Primary aldosteronism, Secondary aldosteronism.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome encountered in clinical practice, and how does it differ mechanistically from Cushing disease?","choices":{"A":"Exogenous glucocorticoid use is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome overall; it suppresses ACTH, unlike Cushing disease, which is caused by an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma.","B":"A pituitary ACTH-secreting corticotroph adenoma is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome overall, and it is identified by an elevated ACTH that fails to suppress on high-dose dexamethasone testing.","C":"An adrenal cortisol-secreting adenoma is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome overall, and it is characterized by elevated cortisol with appropriately suppressed ACTH from negative feedback.","D":"Ectopic ACTH secretion from small cell lung carcinoma is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome overall, and it presents with severe hypokalemia and does not suppress on dexamethasone.","E":"Bilateral adrenocortical hyperplasia is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome overall, and it is driven by ACTH-independent bilateral nodular expansion of the zona fasciculata."},"explanation":"Exogenous glucocorticoids (prescribed for inflammatory or autoimmune conditions) are by far the most common cause of Cushing syndrome — this is an important clinical distinction from the endogenous causes. Among endogenous causes, Cushing disease (pituitary corticotroph tumor secreting ACTH) is most common (60% to 70% of endogenous cases per Robbins Ch 24), followed by primary adrenal neoplasms and ectopic ACTH. Cushing disease has elevated ACTH and partially suppresses on high-dose dexamethasone. Adrenal adenoma has suppressed ACTH. Ectopic ACTH (e.g., small cell lung CA) has very high ACTH and does not suppress on high-dose dexamethasone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The dexamethasone suppression testing algorithm is heavily tested. Low-dose failure = Cushing syndrome confirmed. High-dose: suppression suggests pituitary source (Cushing disease); no suppression suggests ectopic or adrenal source. The ectopic source classically presents with severe hypokalemia from cortisol excess overwhelming 11-beta-HSD2. COMLEX: Cushing syndrome from chronic steroid use is common; osteopathic management includes addressing vertebral compression fractures and avascular necrosis as complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — HPA axis suppression by exogenous steroids and RAAS regulation of aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of adrenal cortical tumors.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gross and histologic features are most characteristic of a benign adrenocortical adenoma?","choices":{"E":"Adrenocortical adenoma appears bright yellow due to lipid-rich content, is well-encapsulated, usually weighs less than 30 grams, and shows uniform clear vacuolated cells with low nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio.","A":"Adrenocortical adenoma appears bright yellow with soft necrotic areas, is poorly encapsulated with infiltrative margins, and typically weighs more than 500 grams with a high mitotic rate.","B":"Adrenocortical adenoma appears tan-brown with central necrosis and dystrophic calcification, weighs more than 200 grams, and shows broad fibrous bands traversing the tumor on histology.","C":"Adrenocortical adenoma appears dark brown with hemorrhage and fibrous capsule invasion, weighs more than 100 grams, and shows vascular invasion with tumor thrombus into adrenal vein.","D":"Adrenocortical adenoma appears gray-white with areas of central necrosis, weighs less than 50 grams, and shows broad fibrous bands with high mitotic figures and atypical mitoses."},"explanation":"Adrenocortical adenomas are well-circumscribed, encapsulated nodules with bright yellow color from abundant intracellular lipid (cholesterol esters used for steroid synthesis). Most weigh less than 30 g (Robbins Ch 24). Histologically they show uniform clear lipid-laden cells (resembling zona fasciculata cells) with a low nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, rare mitoses, and no vascular invasion. Features favoring malignancy include large size (carcinomas frequently exceed 200 to 300 g, Robbins Ch 24), necrosis, vascular invasion, broad fibrous bands, high mitotic rate, atypical mitoses, and capsular invasion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Histologic scoring systems for adrenocortical tumors weigh several of these malignant features together. Key Weiss criteria: high mitotic rate, atypical mitoses, necrosis, vascular invasion, capsular invasion, diffuse architecture, high nuclear grade, clear cell content less than 25%. COMLEX: Adrenal incidentalomas have a population prevalence of about 4% on imaging (Robbins Ch 24); management requires hormonal workup for subclinical Cushing, primary aldosteronism, and pheochromocytoma before characterizing as benign.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis governs ACTH-dependent versus autonomous cortisol in adrenal tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Features favoring malignancy i"}},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of adrenal cortical tumors.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic features best distinguish adrenocortical carcinoma from a benign adrenocortical adenoma on microscopic examination?","choices":{"A":"Granular eosinophilic cytoplasm with mild nuclear pleomorphism but intact capsule, no vascular invasion, and fewer than 3 mitoses per 50 HPF favor adrenocortical carcinoma.","B":"Uniform small cells with abundant clear cytoplasm, intact capsule, rare mitoses less than 1 per 50 HPF, and no necrosis are histologic features that favor adrenocortical carcinoma.","C":"Lipid-laden vacuolated cells with low nuclear-cytoplasmic ratio, no capsular invasion, no vascular invasion, and weight less than 30 grams favor adrenocortical carcinoma on histology.","D":"Broad fibrous bands traversing the tumor, geographic necrosis, vascular invasion with tumor thrombus, and a high mitotic rate with atypical mitoses favor adrenocortical carcinoma.","E":"Compact cells with dark cytoplasm arranged in nests without fibrous bands, no necrosis, and a tumor weight under 50 grams are the primary histologic features of adrenocortical carcinoma."},"explanation":"Adrenocortical carcinoma is distinguished from adenoma by the Weiss criteria. Major malignant features include: vascular invasion into capsular or intratumoral vessels, capsular invasion with infiltrative growth, broad fibrous bands, confluent necrosis, a high mitotic rate (higher Ki-67 index), atypical mitotic figures, high nuclear grade, and diffuse growth pattern. ACC frequently exceeds 200 to 300 grams and may produce hormonal syndromes including Cushing syndrome or virilization. Prognosis is poor, with a median survival of about 2 years (Robbins Ch 24).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACC is a rare but aggressive tumor; most are functional (producing cortisol, androgens, or both). Paradoxically, mixed Cushing plus virilization syndrome should raise suspicion for ACC over adenoma, since functional adenomas usually secrete only one steroid type. Mitotane is the primary adjuvant agent; it is adrenolytic and requires glucocorticoid replacement. COMLEX: Size matters — adrenal masses greater than 4 cm are more likely to be cancer (Robbins Ch 24); a small adrenal mass is unlikely to be ACC.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis governs ACTH-dependent versus autonomous cortisol in adrenal tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of adrenal cortical tumors.","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has a 2 cm adrenal mass found incidentally on CT for kidney stones. Cortisol, DHEAS, aldosterone, and fractionated urine catecholamines are all within normal limits. The mass has imaging characteristics consistent with a lipid-rich adenoma. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"D":"Surveillance with repeat imaging is the most appropriate management for a hormonally inactive incidentaloma under 4 cm with benign radiographic features.","A":"Immediate surgical adrenalectomy is indicated because all adrenal incidentalomas require resection regardless of hormonal status or imaging characteristics to exclude occult malignancy.","B":"MRI with gadolinium is required immediately because CT is insufficient to characterize any adrenal mass and MRI must confirm lipid content before hormonal workup is interpreted.","C":"Serum ACTH stimulation testing should be performed first to exclude subclinical primary adrenal insufficiency before determining the management of this incidentaloma.","E":"Fine needle aspiration biopsy should be performed immediately to exclude adrenocortical carcinoma by histopathology before deciding between surgery and observation."},"explanation":"Adrenal incidentalomas less than 4 cm with benign imaging characteristics (low unenhanced HU on CT, lipid-rich) and no hormonal activity are managed conservatively with imaging surveillance. Surgery is indicated for: masses greater than 4 cm (higher malignancy risk), hormonally active masses (including subclinical Cushing, primary aldosteronism, or pheochromocytoma), or imaging features suspicious for malignancy (irregular margins, heterogeneity, high HU). Fine needle aspiration is generally avoided unless metastatic disease is suspected, as it cannot distinguish adenoma from carcinoma and risks tumor seeding. The overnight 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test should be done in all incidentalomas to rule out subclinical Cushing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pheochromocytoma must be excluded BEFORE any biopsy or surgery on an adrenal mass. Biopsy of an unrecognized pheo can precipitate a lethal hypertensive crisis. COMLEX: Incidentalomas have a population prevalence of about 4% on imaging (Robbins Ch 24); the clinical challenge is risk-stratifying without over-treating. Shared decision-making with the patient is emphasized in osteopathic management principles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis governs ACTH-dependent versus autonomous cortisol in adrenal tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Surgery is indicated for: mass"}},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.","batch":1,"stem":"The classical \"rule of 10s\" for pheochromocytoma states that 10% are bilateral. Which other three features are described by this rule?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 10% are malignant, 10% are extra-adrenal (paraganglioma), and 10% are calcified in the classic rule of 10s for pheochromocytoma.","B":"Approximately 10% produce norepinephrine only, 10% produce dopamine as the primary catecholamine, and 10% are biochemically silent on testing.","C":"Approximately 10% arise in the renal pelvis, 10% are multicentric within one gland, and 10% co-secrete cortisol with their catecholamines.","D":"Approximately 10% occur in MEN 2A, 10% occur in MEN 2B, and 10% occur in VHL syndrome as the three major hereditary pheochromocytoma syndromes.","E":"Approximately 10% are associated with neurofibromatosis type 1, 10% are cystic on imaging, and 10% occur exclusively in pediatric patients under age 12."},"explanation":"The rule of 10s as given in First Aid 2025: 10% malignant, 10% bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal (paraganglioma, e.g., bladder wall or organ of Zuckerkandl), 10% calcify, and 10% occur in children. Older versions also listed '10% familial,' but that figure is outdated: Robbins notes that as many as 30% to 40% of pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas carry a germline mutation (RET in MEN 2A/2B, VHL, NF1, SDH genes). Secretion of norepinephrine or dopamine, renal-pelvis origin, multicentricity, cortisol co-secretion, NF1 association, cystic change, and an exclusively pediatric 10% are not part of the rule. The classic symptom triad is episodic headache, diaphoresis, and palpitations with hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The rule of 10s is frequently tested but increasingly recognized as outdated — as many as 30% to 40% of patients carry a germline mutation (Robbins Ch 24). Know the hereditary syndromes: MEN 2A (pheo + medullary thyroid CA + parathyroid hyperplasia), MEN 2B (pheo + MTC + marfanoid + mucosal neuromas), VHL (pheo + hemangioblastoma + RCC), SDH mutations (extra-adrenal paragangliomas). COMLEX: Episodic hypertension with the classic triad warrants 24-hour urine catecholamines; OMM examination should note hypertensive findings at T1-T5 cardiac somatic referral regions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma: clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and surgical treatment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Neuroblastoma most commonly arises from which anatomic location, and what is the typical age of presentation?","choices":{"B":"Neuroblastoma most commonly arises from the adrenal medulla or paravertebral sympathetic ganglia and typically presents in young children, median age 18 months.","A":"Neuroblastoma most commonly arises from the posterior mediastinum as an isolated thoracic mass and typically presents in adolescents between the ages of 10 and 16 years.","C":"Neuroblastoma most commonly arises from the organ of Zuckerkandl near the aortic bifurcation and typically presents in adults between 30 and 50 years of age.","D":"Neuroblastoma most commonly arises from the carotid body in the neck as a solitary chemoreceptor tumor and typically presents in young children under age 2 with stridor.","E":"Neuroblastoma most commonly arises from the urinary bladder wall or pelvic sympathetic plexus and typically presents in neonates within the first week of life."},"explanation":"Neuroblastoma arises from neural crest-derived sympathoadrenal cells and most commonly originates in the adrenal medulla (about 40% of childhood cases, Robbins Ch 10) or paravertebral sympathetic ganglia. It is the most common extracranial solid tumor of childhood and typically seen in children younger than 4 years (FA). The median age at diagnosis is 18 months (Robbins Ch 10). Presentation includes an abdominal mass (may cross midline, distinguishing from Wilms tumor which does not), hypertension, dancing eyes-dancing feet (opsoclonus-myoclonus), and elevated urinary catecholamine metabolites (HVA, VMA). Thoracic neuroblastoma (posterior mediastinal mass) is the second most common location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neuroblastoma vs Wilms tumor: neuroblastoma crosses the midline, Wilms does not. Neuroblastoma = elevated urine HVA/VMA; Wilms = hematuria. N-MYC amplification in neuroblastoma is a poor prognostic marker. Spontaneous regression can occur in stage 4S (liver, skin, bone marrow) in infants under 18 months without N-MYC amplification. COMLEX: Screening for adrenal masses in children with unexplained hypertension should include urine catecholamines and HVA/VMA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma: clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and surgical treatment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which biochemical test has the highest sensitivity for the initial diagnosis of pheochromocytoma in a patient with episodic hypertension, headaches, and diaphoresis?","choices":{"D":"Plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines have the highest sensitivity for diagnosing pheochromocytoma and are the recommended first-line tests.","A":"A 24-hour urine vanillylmandelic acid alone has the highest sensitivity and is the gold standard single test for diagnosing pheochromocytoma due to its specificity in outpatient settings.","B":"Serum chromogranin A alone has the highest sensitivity at greater than 99% and is the recommended gold standard first-line test for pheochromocytoma diagnosis in all settings.","C":"Random plasma epinephrine measured in the fasting seated state has the highest sensitivity because it directly measures the pathologic hormone rather than a metabolite.","E":"Serum ACTH stimulation combined with plasma norepinephrine is the gold standard because pheochromocytoma suppresses the HPA axis allowing diagnosis via cortisol nadir."},"explanation":"Plasma free metanephrines (normetanephrine + metanephrine) and 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines are the recommended first-line biochemical tests for pheochromocytoma diagnosis because of their high sensitivity. Metanephrines are continuously produced by O-methylation of catecholamines within chromaffin cells, making them detectable even between episodic secretory bursts. Urinary VMA has lower sensitivity and is no longer the gold standard. Chromogranin A has moderate sensitivity but low specificity. Imaging (CT/MRI) is performed only after biochemical confirmation to localize the tumor. Pre-surgical alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine) is required before any surgical intervention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plasma free metanephrines are the test of choice for high-risk patients or suspected pheo. Urinary fractionated metanephrines are acceptable for average-risk patients. False positives occur with tricyclic antidepressants, labetalol, and acetaminophen. COMLEX: Alpha-blockade must be established BEFORE beta-blockade because unopposed alpha stimulation from catecholamines causes severe hypertension if beta receptors are blocked first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma: clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and surgical treatment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Urinary VMA has lower sensitiv","B":"Chromogranin A has moderate se"}},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain clinically relevant anatomy, histology and physiology of the adrenal gland.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with septic shock is intubated in the ICU. Despite high-dose vasopressors, she remains persistently hypotensive. Random cortisol is 5 mcg/dL and ACTH stimulation test shows a delta cortisol of 6 mcg/dL. Which specific cortical zone is failing to respond and which hormone is most critical to restore for hemodynamic recovery?","choices":{"E":"The zona reticularis is failing to produce androgens; DHEA conversion to cortisol in peripheral adipose tissue normally maintains sufficient glucocorticoid activity during septic shock.","A":"The zona glomerulosa is failing to produce aldosterone; aldosterone is the most critical hormone because it retains sodium in the proximal tubule and directly restores intravascular volume during septic shock.","B":"The zona fasciculata is failing to produce cortisol; cortisol is critical because it maintains vascular responsiveness to catecholamines and supports gluconeogenesis during septic shock.","C":"The adrenal medulla is failing to produce epinephrine; epinephrine is the most critical hormone because it directly mediates cardiac acceleration and peripheral vasoconstriction in septic shock.","D":"The zona glomerulosa is failing to produce corticosterone; corticosterone rather than cortisol is the primary glucocorticoid supporting vascular tone in critical illness states."},"explanation":"Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (CIRCI) involves failure of the zona fasciculata to produce adequate cortisol during physiologic stress. Cortisol is permissive for catecholamine action: it upregulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle and enables norepinephrine to maintain systemic vascular resistance. Without cortisol, vasopressors lose efficacy. Hydrocortisone is used for vasopressor-refractory septic shock. The ACTH stimulation test defines relative adrenal insufficiency in this context. The zona fasciculata receives the most arterial blood flow in the centripetal pattern and is most vulnerable to ischemia-related dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoids permit catecholamine vasoconstriction at the receptor level. This is why mineralocorticoid replacement alone does not reverse vasopressor-dependent shock. COMLEX: In osteopathic critical care, HPA axis status should be assessed in any patient with unexplained vasopressor requirements; empiric stress-dose steroids are appropriate when adrenal insufficiency cannot be excluded rapidly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — Three zones of the adrenal cortex and medulla as distinct neuroendocrine structures","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain clinically relevant anatomy, histology and physiology of the adrenal gland.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man underwent resection of a right adrenal medullary paraganglioma 6 months ago. His adrenal cortex is intact on post-operative imaging and all hormonal panels are normal. He now feels well. Supine blood pressure is 128/80 mmHg; on standing it is 124/82 mmHg with an appropriate rise in heart rate. Which mechanism best explains his preserved orthostatic response?","choices":{"B":"Baroreflex vasoconstriction and tachycardia on standing are driven mainly by norepinephrine from postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals, which medullary loss does not affect.","A":"Cortisol from the intact cortex directly activates cardiac and vascular adrenergic receptors, replacing the catecholamine signal lost with the resected medulla and sustaining the reflex.","C":"Medullary dopamine production shifts to the proximal tubule after resection, where D1 stimulation expands plasma volume enough to prevent any fall in standing blood pressure.","D":"The remaining left adrenal medulla doubles its epinephrine output on standing, and this circulating epinephrine is the main driver of reflex vasoconstriction and tachycardia.","E":"Chromaffin cells regenerate from the intact adrenal cortex within months, restoring the medullary epinephrine surge that every orthostatic adjustment requires."},"explanation":"Orthostatic compensation is a baroreceptor reflex: standing lowers venous return, baroreceptor firing falls, and sympathetic outflow rises. The effector is norepinephrine released from postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals onto arterioles, veins, and the heart, which produces vasoconstriction and tachycardia within seconds. Adrenal medullary epinephrine adds little to this response; even loss of both adrenal medullae usually has little effect, because the direct sympathetic pathways perform almost all the necessary duties. The remaining medulla therefore does not need to compensate, cortisol is permissive for catecholamine action but is not an adrenergic agonist, dopamine does not maintain standing blood pressure, and chromaffin cells derive from neural crest rather than regenerating from cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal orthostasis depends on the sympathetic nervous system (norepinephrine from nerve terminals); adrenal medullary epinephrine is a minor contributor. Bilateral adrenalectomy patients need steroid replacement but usually maintain orthostatic BP via intact sympathetic nerve terminals. COMLEX: Orthostatic hypotension assessment includes baroreceptor reflex testing; somatic dysfunction at T1-T5 can impair sympathetic outflow to the heart and vasculature.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — Three zones of the adrenal cortex and medulla as distinct neuroendocrine structures","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The remaining medulla therefor","C":"The remaining medulla therefor","D":"The remaining medulla therefor"}},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain clinically relevant anatomy, histology and physiology of the adrenal gland.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with primary hyperaldosteronism is scheduled for adrenal vein sampling, in which a catheter is advanced from the inferior vena cava into each suprarenal vein to compare aldosterone levels. Which description of the venous drainage of the suprarenal glands is correct?","choices":{"C":"The right suprarenal vein drains into the portal vein through the liver, while the left drains directly into the inferior vena cava","A":"The right suprarenal vein is short and drains directly into the inferior vena cava, while the left drains into the left renal vein","B":"The right suprarenal vein drains into the right renal vein, while the left joins the left gonadal vein before entering the vena cava","D":"Both suprarenal veins drain into the left renal vein, so the right vein must cross in front of the aorta to reach that vessel","E":"The right suprarenal vein drains into the azygos vein above the diaphragm, while the left drains into the hemiazygos vein"},"explanation":"Moore (Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Abdomen) states that the suprarenal glands drain via large suprarenal veins: the short right suprarenal vein drains into the inferior vena cava, whereas the longer left suprarenal vein, often joined by the inferior phrenic vein, empties into the left renal vein. Pawlina (Ch 21) gives the same pattern. The arterial supply comes from three sources: superior suprarenal arteries from the inferior phrenic arteries, middle suprarenal arteries from the aorta, and inferior suprarenal arteries from the renal arteries. The right vein does not drain into the right renal vein or the portal system, and the azygos and hemiazygos systems do not receive the suprarenal veins. The left renal vein, not the right suprarenal vein, crosses anterior to the aorta, and it receives the left gonadal vein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Suprarenal veins: right → IVC directly (short); left → left renal vein, often joined by the inferior phrenic vein (Moore). The left renal vein also receives the left gonadal vein. COMLEX: The adrenal cortex (glomerulosa, a broad fasciculata of about 75%, reticularis) and the medulla are in essence two glands in one capsule (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — primary aldosteronism diagnosis, including adrenal vein sampling to lateralize the source","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"The left renal vein, not the r"}},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pituitary-adrenal axis and its control and how plasma concentrations of cortisol and aldosterone are controlled.","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients undergo adrenalectomy for a unilateral adrenocortical adenoma. Patient 1's adenoma secreted cortisol and caused Cushing syndrome; patient 2's secreted aldosterone and caused Conn syndrome. In patient 1 the adjacent and contralateral adrenal cortex is atrophic, while in patient 2 it is not. Which best explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"Tumor cortisol suppresses ACTH by negative feedback, depriving the normal cortex of ACTH, while aldosterone does not suppress ACTH","B":"Tumor aldosterone stimulates ACTH release through hypokalemia, which keeps the normal cortex growing, while cortisol has no effect on ACTH","C":"Cortisol-secreting adenomas compress the normal cortex on both sides, while aldosterone-secreting adenomas stay encapsulated","D":"Tumor cortisol suppresses renin, which removes angiotensin II support from the cortex, while aldosterone raises renin and keeps it intact","E":"Cortisol-secreting adenomas release inhibin that blocks ACTH receptors in the normal cortex, while aldosterone-secreting adenomas lack it"},"explanation":"Robbins states that, in contrast to cortical adenomas associated with Cushing syndrome, those associated with hyperaldosteronism do not usually suppress corticotropin secretion, so the adjacent adrenal cortex and that of the contralateral gland are not atrophic. A cortisol-secreting adenoma suppresses CRH and ACTH through negative feedback, and ACTH maintains the size of the zona fasciculata and zona reticularis (Guyton Ch 76, Table 76-1), so the non-tumorous cortex atrophies. Aldosterone does not stimulate ACTH, and hypokalemia lowers rather than raises adrenal stimulation. Atrophy of the contralateral gland cannot be explained by local compression. Aldosterone suppresses renin rather than raising it, and renin does not maintain the fasciculata. Inhibin is a gonadal hormone that acts on FSH, not on adrenal ACTH receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACTH-independent Cushing syndrome (adrenal adenoma or carcinoma) → low ACTH → atrophy of adjacent and contralateral cortex; ACTH-dependent Cushing → bilateral hyperplasia; Conn adenomas leave ACTH and the remaining cortex unchanged (Robbins). COMLEX: Exogenous glucocorticoids cause the same ACTH suppression and cortical atrophy, which is why abrupt withdrawal can cause adrenal insufficiency (SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis: CRH/ACTH/cortisol negative feedback loop and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Atrophy of the contralateral g","E":"Inhibin is a gonadal hormone t"}},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pituitary-adrenal axis and its control and how plasma concentrations of cortisol and aldosterone are controlled.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with profound muscle weakness, severe hypokalemia of 2.4 mEq/L, metabolic alkalosis, and markedly elevated serum cortisol of 68 mcg/dL. ACTH is 1,450 pg/mL and does not suppress with high-dose dexamethasone. He has lost 18 pounds in 3 months. Chest imaging shows a right hilar mass with mediastinal adenopathy. Which mechanism best explains the severe hypokalemia in this patient?","choices":{"E":"Ectopic ACTH from a pulmonary neuroendocrine tumor drives massive cortisol production that overwhelms renal 11-beta-HSD2, so cortisol activates mineralocorticoid receptors and causes profound potassium wasting.","A":"A pituitary corticotroph adenoma is producing ACTH at levels that overwhelm negative feedback; the hypokalemia results from cortisol stimulating renal H/K-ATPase rather than a mineralocorticoid receptor mechanism.","B":"Autonomous aldosterone secretion from bilateral adrenal hyperplasia driven by very high ACTH levels independently activates mineralocorticoid receptors causing potassium wasting.","C":"Autoimmune adrenal hyperplasia from anti-ACTH receptor antibodies drives cortisol to saturate SHBG, leaving free cortisol to directly inhibit renal potassium channels.","D":"Primary aldosteronism coexisting with the lung mass is causing the hypokalemia; the elevated ACTH reflects pituitary compensation for feedback suppression by aldosterone."},"explanation":"In ectopic ACTH syndrome, ACTH levels are extremely high and drive massive cortisol production. At very high cortisol concentrations, the renal enzyme 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (11-beta-HSD2), which normally inactivates cortisol to cortisone in the kidney, is overwhelmed. Unmetabolized cortisol accumulates and binds mineralocorticoid receptors (cortisol has equal affinity for these receptors), acting like aldosterone to cause sodium retention, hypertension, and severe potassium wasting. This explains why ectopic ACTH characteristically presents with severe hypokalemia, hypertension, and metabolic alkalosis. The non-suppression on high-dose dexamethasone and the smoking history with hilar mass point to small cell lung cancer as the ectopic source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ectopic ACTH syndrome classic features: very high ACTH, severe hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, weight loss, rapid onset without classic Cushingoid features. The mechanism of hypokalemia (11-beta-HSD2 saturation by excess cortisol) is a high-yield testable concept. COMLEX: Small cell lung cancer is the most common ectopic ACTH source; carcinoid is second. Ectopic sources do not suppress on high-dose DST.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis: CRH/ACTH/cortisol negative feedback loop and circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pituitary-adrenal axis and its control and how plasma concentrations of cortisol and aldosterone are controlled.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis and tense ascites has hypokalemia, with serum potassium of 3.1 mEq/L. Plasma aldosterone is elevated, and plasma renin activity is also markedly elevated. Which best explains his hyperaldosteronism?","choices":{"D":"Cirrhosis-induced hypokalemia, which directly stimulates both renin and aldosterone secretion from the kidney and the adrenal cortex","A":"Primary hyperaldosteronism from bilateral glomerulosa hyperplasia, in which autonomous aldosterone release raises renin through hypokalemia","B":"Primary hyperaldosteronism from an aldosterone-producing adenoma, which in cirrhosis begins to secrete renin together with aldosterone","C":"Secondary hyperaldosteronism, in which low effective arterial volume activates the renin-angiotensin system and angiotensin II drives aldosterone","E":"Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism, in which ACTH drives aldosterone and renin rises because cortisol stimulates the kidney"},"explanation":"Robbins explains that in secondary hyperaldosteronism aldosterone release occurs in response to physiologic activation of the renin-angiotensin system and is characterized by increased plasma renin. It occurs with decreased renal perfusion (arteriolar nephrosclerosis, renal artery stenosis), arterial hypovolemia and edema (congestive heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome), and pregnancy. Primary hyperaldosteronism, whether from bilateral hyperplasia, an adenoma, or a familial form, is autonomous and suppresses renin, so a high renin rules it out. Adenomas do not secrete renin. Hypokalemia decreases aldosterone secretion rather than stimulating it, since a rise in potassium is a strong stimulus (Guyton Ch 78). Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism is a primary form with suppressed renin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High aldosterone with low renin = primary hyperaldosteronism; high aldosterone with high renin = secondary hyperaldosteronism (renal artery stenosis, heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, pregnancy) (Robbins). COMLEX: Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists such as spironolactone block aldosterone action in the collecting duct (SDL 17 Obj 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — regulation of aldosterone secretion by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Adenomas do not secrete renin.","D":"Hypokalemia decreases aldoster"}},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old girl presents with premature pubarche since age 7, clitoromegaly, and advanced bone age. Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone is markedly elevated at 14,000 ng/dL. Electrolytes are normal. ACTH is 210 pg/mL. Adrenal CT shows bilateral glandular enlargement. Adrenal tissue from a similar patient shows a thickened cortex with diffuse and nodular expansion of lipid-depleted cells, without hemorrhage, inflammation, or necrosis. Which pathologic process best accounts for these findings?","choices":{"B":"Bilateral adrenocortical hyperplasia driven by chronic ACTH oversecretion, from absent cortisol negative feedback in 21-hydroxylase deficiency","A":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome, with massive bilateral adrenal hemorrhage destroying cortical architecture from DIC-associated vascular occlusion","C":"Primary pigmented nodular adrenocortical disease, with ACTH-independent cortisol secretion and bilateral nodular expansion in a non-classic CAH pattern","D":"Adrenocortical carcinoma, with vascular invasion, broad fibrous bands, necrosis, and frequent mitoses arising against normal cortical parenchyma","E":"Autoimmune adrenalitis, with lymphocytic infiltration and progressive fibrosis replacing normal cortical parenchyma in polyglandular autoimmune syndrome"},"explanation":"In 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the blocked cortisol synthesis removes negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary, causing sustained ACTH hypersecretion. Chronically elevated ACTH drives bilateral proliferation of the adrenal cortex, producing the bilateral adrenocortical hyperplasia (nodular or diffuse) seen on imaging and histology. The thickened cortex with diffuse and nodular expansion represents ACTH-driven hypertrophy and hyperplasia; the absence of hemorrhage, inflammation, and necrosis excludes Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome, autoimmune adrenalitis, and carcinoma, and PPNAD is ACTH-independent, which does not fit an ACTH of 210 pg/mL. This is the pathologic basis for the bilateral adrenal enlargement on CT seen in CAH patients with non-classic or poorly controlled classic disease. Treatment with hydrocortisone suppresses ACTH, gradually reducing gland size.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This histology reflects the adrenal response to chronic ACTH stimulation. In untreated or poorly controlled CAH, chronic ACTH excess can produce adrenal rest tumors (ectopic adrenal tissue in testes or ovaries that hypertrophies). Late-onset non-classic CAH presents with premature pubarche, acne, and hirsutism — diagnosed by elevated 17-OHP before and after ACTH stimulation. COMLEX: CAH management in children requires careful glucocorticoid dosing to suppress ACTH (reducing virilization) without causing iatrogenic Cushing syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zonation; CAH disrupts zona fasciculata and glomerulosa biosynthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"The thickened cortex with diff","D":"The thickened cortex with diff","E":"The thickened cortex with diff"}},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman has had hirsutism, acne, and irregular menses since mid-adolescence. She had normal external genitalia at birth, a normal newborn screen, and no episodes of salt wasting. Blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Basal 17-hydroxyprogesterone is mildly elevated. Which best explains her presentation?","choices":{"D":"Nonclassic 21-hydroxylase deficiency, in which a partial enzyme defect leaves enough cortisol and aldosterone but produces later androgen excess","A":"Classic salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency, in which a total enzyme defect causes androgen excess that was missed at birth and appears at puberty","B":"11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, in which deoxycorticosterone builds up and causes androgen excess together with hypertension and hypokalemia","C":"17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency, in which blocked sex steroid synthesis raises 17-hydroxyprogesterone and causes androgen excess in adolescence","E":"Simple virilizing 21-hydroxylase deficiency, in which enough aldosterone prevents salt wasting but genital ambiguity is present at birth"},"explanation":"Robbins describes three syndromes of 21-hydroxylase deficiency, which varies with the CYP21A2 mutation: salt-wasting classic CAH, simple virilizing adrenogenitalism without salt wasting (about one-third, presenting with genital ambiguity), and nonclassic or late-onset hyperandrogenism. The nonclassic form is significantly more common than the classic patterns, results from a partial deficiency that accounts for the later onset, presents with mild manifestations such as hirsutism, acne, and menstrual irregularity, and cannot be diagnosed on routine newborn screening. Classic salt-wasting disease presents soon after birth with salt loss and, in girls, virilization at birth. 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causes hypertension, which she does not have. 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency blocks androgen synthesis, so it does not cause hirsutism. Simple virilizing disease would have produced genital ambiguity at birth.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 21-hydroxylase deficiency (CYP21A2, more than 90% of CAH): salt-wasting classic, simple virilizing (genital ambiguity without salt crisis), and nonclassic late-onset hyperandrogenism; all cases show bilateral adrenal hyperplasia from high ACTH (Robbins). COMLEX: Glucocorticoid treatment both replaces cortisol and suppresses ACTH, reducing excess androgen synthesis (Robbins).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zonation; CAH disrupts zona fasciculata and glomerulosa biosynthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Classic salt-wasting disease p","E":"Simple virilizing disease woul"}},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl presents with primary amenorrhea, hypertension, and hypokalemia. She has no axillary or pubic hair and her breast development is absent. Laboratory studies show: cortisol 2 mcg/dL, ACTH 340 pg/mL, aldosterone 1 ng/dL, serum 11-deoxycorticosterone elevated at 22 ng/dL, estrogen very low, and testosterone undetectable. Karyotype is 46,XX. Which enzyme deficiency best explains this complete picture?","choices":{"E":"17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency explains this presentation because neither cortisol nor sex steroids can be made; DOC accumulates causing hypertension, and absent sex steroids cause sexual infantilism.","A":"21-hydroxylase deficiency explains this presentation because cortisol is low and androgens accumulate causing virilization, but in non-classic variants amenorrhea and absent secondary sex characteristics can occur.","B":"11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency explains this presentation because 11-deoxycortisol accumulates causing virilization in females and hypertension but does not typically affect sex steroid synthesis.","C":"3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency explains this presentation because DHEA accumulates causing partial virilization in females along with mineralocorticoid deficiency and salt-wasting.","D":"Cholesterol desmolase deficiency explains this presentation because no steroidogenesis occurs, all hormone levels are undetectable, and lipid accumulation is visible on adrenal imaging."},"explanation":"17-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP17A1) is required for cortisol synthesis (converting pregnenolone to 17-hydroxypregnenolone) and sex steroid synthesis (the 17,20-lyase reaction producing androgens). Its deficiency blocks both pathways. Consequences: cortisol deficiency (elevated ACTH), androgen and estrogen deficiency (sexual infantilism, primary amenorrhea, absent secondary sex characteristics in 46,XX females and pseudohermaphroditism in 46,XY males), and accumulation of 11-deoxycorticosterone (DOC) via the mineralocorticoid pathway, which acts as a potent mineralocorticoid causing hypertension and hypokalemia — while paradoxically aldosterone itself is low (because DOC-mediated sodium retention and volume expansion suppress renin and angiotensin II, the main drive for aldosterone synthase). This is the CAH variant that causes hypertension instead of the typical salt-wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The two CAH forms with hypertension are 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency (virilization + HTN) and 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency (sexual infantilism + HTN). The classic mnemonic: 11-beta-hydroxylase = virilization (virilizes females), 17-alpha-hydroxylase = no sex steroids (no virilization, no puberty). Both cause DOC accumulation leading to hypertension. COMLEX: A 46,XY patient with 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency appears phenotypically female at birth; this is a critical counseling challenge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — Adrenal cortex zonation; CAH disrupts zona fasciculata and glomerulosa biosynthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss the following adrenal disorders in terms of etiology, pathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and management: Chronic adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), Acute adrenal apoplexy (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome), Cushing syndrome, Primary aldosteronism, Secondary aldosteronism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student is brought to the emergency department with fever of 41.2C, confusion, and a rapidly spreading purpuric rash on his trunk and extremities. He develops refractory shock and dies within 14 hours despite aggressive resuscitation. Autopsy reveals both adrenal glands converted to hemorrhagic masses, with histology showing massive hemorrhagic necrosis of the cortex. Which organism is most commonly responsible for this presentation, and through what mechanism does adrenal destruction occur?","choices":{"D":"Haemophilus influenzae type B is the most common cause; its lipooligosaccharide directly activates adrenal chromaffin cells, causing massive catecholamine release that overwhelms adrenal vasculature.","A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause; it produces a polysaccharide capsule that directly invades adrenal sinusoids, causing bilateral cortical necrosis through complement-mediated cytotoxicity.","B":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause; it produces bilateral adrenal septic emboli from valvular endocarditis, causing hematogenous seeding and hemorrhagic necrosis.","C":"Neisseria meningitidis is the most common cause; it triggers a DIC-associated coagulopathy with bilateral adrenal vascular occlusion, infarction, and hemorrhage producing Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome.","E":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common cause; it produces ecthyma gangrenosum by secreting elastase that destroys adrenal vascular walls in immunocompromised pediatric patients."},"explanation":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is bilateral adrenal hemorrhage producing acute adrenal crisis, most classically caused by Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcemia). The mechanism involves: bacteremia triggering a massive systemic inflammatory response, activation of the coagulation cascade producing disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), fibrin microthrombi occluding the adrenal sinusoids, and subsequent hemorrhagic infarction of both glands. The adrenals have a unique centripetal blood supply that makes them particularly vulnerable to DIC-mediated ischemia. The rapidly spreading petechial-to-purpuric rash is the clinical hallmark of meningococcemia. The sudden loss of both cortisol and aldosterone precipitates an acute adrenal crisis with refractory hypotension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome = meningococcemia + DIC + bilateral adrenal hemorrhage + acute adrenal crisis. Pathology shows bilateral massive hemorrhage with destruction of normal cortical architecture. Any patient with purpuric rash and shock should receive immediate empiric ceftriaxone plus corticosteroids without awaiting confirmation. COMLEX: Adrenal apoplexy from anticoagulation therapy is another cause of acute adrenal insufficiency; recognize the triad of abdominal/flank pain, hypotension, and new anticoagulant use.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis suppression by exogenous steroids and RAAS regulation of aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss the following adrenal disorders in terms of etiology, pathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and management: Chronic adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), Acute adrenal apoplexy (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome), Cushing syndrome, Primary aldosteronism, Secondary aldosteronism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with known type 1 diabetes mellitus and Hashimoto thyroiditis presents with a 6-month history of fatigue, weight loss, nausea, and hyperpigmentation of her buccal mucosa, gingiva, and palmar creases. Labs: sodium 129 mEq/L, potassium 5.9 mEq/L, 8 AM cortisol 2.4 mcg/dL, ACTH 920 pg/mL. Which histologic pattern would an adrenal biopsy most likely show in this condition?","choices":{"C":"Cortical parenchyma replaced by fibrous tissue with a dense lymphocytic infiltrate, while the adrenal medulla remains relatively preserved, is the pattern of autoimmune adrenalitis.","A":"Bilateral adrenal enlargement with areas of hemorrhage, fibrin thrombi, and vascular occlusion replacing normal cortical architecture is the pattern consistent with this patient's condition.","B":"Bilateral adrenal replacement by caseating granulomas with central necrosis, Langhans giant cells, and acid-fast bacilli is the pattern consistent with this patient's condition.","D":"Diffuse cortical hypertrophy with large lipid-laden cells in parallel cords driven by excess ACTH stimulation without inflammatory infiltrate is the pattern consistent with this condition.","E":"Bilateral adrenal enlargement with sheets of metastatic carcinoma replacing cortical tissue and sparing the medulla is the pattern consistent with this patient's adrenal histology."},"explanation":"Autoimmune adrenalitis is the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) in developed countries, accounting for 80% to 90% of cases of primary adrenal insufficiency in high-income countries (Robbins Ch 24). Pathogenesis involves autoantibodies against 21-hydroxylase and T-lymphocyte-mediated destruction of the adrenal cortex. Histologically, the cortex is progressively replaced by fibrous connective tissue with a dense lymphocytic and plasma cell infiltrate, while the medulla is characteristically preserved (since it is not an autoimmune target). This patient has polyglandular autoimmune syndrome type 2 (Schmidt syndrome): Addison disease + type 1 DM + autoimmune thyroid disease. Autoantibodies to 21-hydroxylase and 17-hydroxylase are detected in most patients (Robbins Ch 24).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Autoimmune adrenalitis is the most common cause of Addison disease in developed countries; tuberculosis was historically the most common cause worldwide. In TB, both cortex and medulla are destroyed and the glands are calcified on imaging. In autoimmune adrenalitis, medulla is spared and glands may be small (atrophic). Anti-21-hydroxylase antibodies confirm autoimmune etiology. COMLEX: Schmidt syndrome (Type 2 APS) = Addison + type 1 DM + autoimmune thyroid disease; Type 1 APS = Addison + hypoparathyroidism + mucocutaneous candidiasis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis suppression by exogenous steroids and RAAS regulation of aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Discuss the following adrenal disorders in terms of etiology, pathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and management: Chronic adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), Acute adrenal apoplexy (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome), Cushing syndrome, Primary aldosteronism, Secondary aldosteronism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has hypertension refractory to three medications, hypokalemia of 3.0 mEq/L, and metabolic alkalosis. The aldosterone-to-renin ratio is 42. Adrenal CT shows a 1.5 cm right adrenal nodule and normal-appearing left adrenal. Hormonal workup confirms autonomous aldosterone secretion. Which is the most appropriate next diagnostic step before recommending right adrenalectomy?","choices":{"B":"Spironolactone alone is the most appropriate long-term management for primary aldosteronism regardless of whether a unilateral adenoma is identified on adrenal CT imaging.","A":"Bilateral adrenalectomy is required because primary aldosteronism from bilateral hyperplasia cannot be distinguished from adenoma without removing both glands for definitive histopathologic diagnosis.","C":"Immediate right adrenalectomy without adrenal vein sampling is indicated because the CT adenoma is unequivocal evidence of a unilateral aldosterone source and vein sampling adds risk without benefit.","D":"Fludrocortisone suppression testing should be performed next to confirm autonomous aldosterone secretion before any anatomic localization or procedural intervention.","E":"Adrenal vein sampling should be performed to confirm that the right nodule is the source of aldosterone, because CT alone cannot exclude bilateral disease or a nonfunctioning incidental nodule."},"explanation":"Adrenal vein sampling (AVS) is required before surgical management of primary hyperaldosteronism. CT has significant limitations: it misses small adenomas (may appear as bilateral hyperplasia), can falsely identify non-functioning nodules as the aldosterone source, and cannot detect microadenomas. AVS measures aldosterone and cortisol from both adrenal veins simultaneously; lateralization confirms unilateral disease. Without AVS, some patients would undergo unnecessary unilateral adrenalectomy (leaving the true bilateral disease untreated) or fail to benefit from surgery. Spironolactone is the medical management for bilateral disease or poor surgical candidates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The management algorithm for primary aldosteronism: confirm with ARR, then confirm with fludrocortisone/salt suppression, then perform adrenal CT for anatomy, then perform AVS if surgical candidate. Surgery only if AVS confirms unilateral. Medical therapy (spironolactone or eplerenone) for bilateral disease. COMLEX: After adrenalectomy for an aldosteronoma, stop potassium supplements and spironolactone; transient hyperkalemia can occur while the suppressed contralateral zona glomerulosa recovers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — HPA axis suppression by exogenous steroids and RAAS regulation of aldosterone secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Spironolactone is the medical ","C":"Without AVS, some patients wou"}},{"id":"sdl21_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of adrenal cortical tumors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a 2.8 cm right adrenal mass discovered incidentally. Cortisol after 1 mg overnight dexamethasone is 1.0 mcg/dL (normal suppression). Aldosterone-to-renin ratio and urine catecholamines are normal. Adrenalectomy is performed for increasing size on surveillance. Which histologic feature is most consistent with a benign adrenocortical adenoma rather than adrenocortical carcinoma?","choices":{"E":"Uniform clear vacuolated cells with abundant intracellular lipid, a low nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, no necrosis, and absence of vascular invasion are the histologic features most consistent with benign adrenocortical adenoma.","A":"High mitotic rate with atypical mitotic figures, geographic necrosis, and broad fibrous bands traversing the tumor are histologic features most consistent with benign adrenocortical adenoma.","B":"Vascular invasion with tumor thrombus extending into capsular vessels and lymphovascular spaces throughout the specimen is most consistent with benign adrenocortical adenoma.","C":"Diffuse growth pattern with sheets of compact dark eosinophilic cells, high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, and necrosis are histologic features of benign adrenocortical adenoma.","D":"Broad fibrous bands dividing the tumor into irregular lobules with central hemorrhage, necrosis, and more than 5 mitoses per 50 high power fields are consistent with benign adenoma."},"explanation":"Benign adrenocortical adenoma characteristically shows uniform, well-differentiated cells resembling zona fasciculata with abundant intracellular lipid (clear vacuolated cytoplasm from cholesterol ester stores), low nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, small nucleoli, rare or absent mitoses, and intact capsule without vascular or capsular invasion. These features contrast sharply with adrenocortical carcinoma, which exhibits the Weiss criteria: necrosis, vascular invasion, capsular invasion, broad fibrous bands, high mitotic rate, atypical mitoses, and diffuse growth architecture. A Weiss score of 3 or more out of 9 criteria classifies the tumor as carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Weiss score (9 criteria, 3+ = carcinoma): high nuclear grade, high mitotic rate, atypical mitoses, clear cell content under 25%, diffuse architecture, necrosis, venous invasion, sinusoidal invasion, capsular invasion. Remember that not all adrenocortical tumors are easy to classify — borderline cases (Weiss 2-3) are managed as potentially malignant. COMLEX: Functional adenomas require steroid replacement post-operatively because the contralateral ACTH-suppressed normal adrenal needs recovery time.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis governs ACTH-dependent versus autonomous cortisol in adrenal tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"These features contrast sharpl"}},{"id":"sdl21_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of adrenal cortical tumors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes resection of an 8.5 cm adrenal mass weighing 750 grams with areas of hemorrhage on cut section. Histology shows broad fibrous bands, confluent necrosis, vascular invasion, and 8 mitotic figures per 50 HPF. The Weiss score is 6. He has cushingoid features and markedly elevated urinary 17-ketosteroids. Which statement best describes the prognosis and management for this tumor type?","choices":{"D":"A Weiss score of 6 confirms adrenocortical carcinoma with high recurrence risk; complete surgical resection is first-line but adjuvant mitotane should be considered to reduce recurrence in patients with incomplete resection or high-risk features.","A":"A Weiss score of 6 indicates a borderline adrenal tumor requiring only observation after surgical resection with repeat imaging at 6 months without any adjuvant systemic therapy.","B":"A Weiss score of 6 indicates a benign adenoma that is incidentally large and functional; surgical resection alone is curative and no adjuvant therapy or surveillance is required.","C":"A Weiss score of 6 indicates adrenocortical carcinoma but systemic therapy is not indicated because mitotane is nephrotoxic and adrenocortical carcinoma is chemotherapy-resistant.","E":"A Weiss score of 6 confirms adrenocortical carcinoma; standard treatment is external beam radiation followed by platinum-based chemotherapy as first-line adjuvant therapy."},"explanation":"Adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a rare but aggressive malignancy with a median survival of about 2 years (Robbins Ch 24). Management requires complete surgical resection (R0) as the only potentially curative intervention. For high-risk features (large size, positive margins, high Weiss score), adjuvant mitotane is recommended. Mitotane is an adrenolytic agent that suppresses adrenal steroidogenesis and has direct cytotoxic activity against adrenal cortical cells; it requires glucocorticoid replacement due to adrenal suppression. The FIRM-ACT trial demonstrated that etoposide-doxorubicin-cisplatin plus mitotane is the standard first-line regimen for metastatic ACC. Elevated 17-ketosteroids (adrenal androgen metabolites) and Cushing syndrome together suggest ACC over adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mixed hormone excess (cortisol + androgens) is a red flag for ACC rather than adenoma. ACC most often presents at advanced stage with large tumor size. Mitotane is adrenolytic; patients require stress-dose steroids. COMLEX: Vertebral compression fractures and myopathy from cortisol excess require osteopathic evaluation and rehabilitation; OMT-directed treatment is important for quality of life in patients with ACC.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis governs ACTH-dependent versus autonomous cortisol in adrenal tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl21_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of adrenal cortical tumors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a 3.8 cm right adrenal incidentaloma on CT. Unenhanced CT attenuation is 12 HU consistent with a lipid-rich lesion. Cortisol after 1 mg dexamethasone is 3.8 mcg/dL, suggesting possible autonomous cortisol secretion. She has mild hypertension and prediabetes but no classic Cushingoid features. ACTH is low-normal. Urine catecholamines and aldosterone-to-renin ratio are normal. What is the most appropriate management approach?","choices":{"C":"An individualized decision between surgical resection and continued surveillance is most appropriate, weighing the degree of cortisol autonomy, reversibility of metabolic comorbidities, patient age, surgical risk, and preferences.","A":"Immediate surgical adrenalectomy is required because any dexamethasone suppression test result above 1.8 mcg/dL mandates adrenalectomy regardless of symptoms or metabolic severity.","B":"Repeat CT imaging in 3 months is the only required management because the mass is under 4 cm, has benign imaging characteristics, and borderline dexamethasone suppression is not clinically meaningful.","D":"MRI with chemical shift imaging must be performed next to confirm lipid content before any hormonal workup findings are interpreted or acted upon clinically.","E":"Fine needle aspiration biopsy of the adrenal mass should be performed immediately to confirm adenoma versus carcinoma before deciding between surveillance and adrenalectomy."},"explanation":"Mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS, formerly subclinical Cushing syndrome) is defined by incomplete suppression after 1 mg overnight dexamethasone (normal is usually <2 mcg/dL and overt Cushing syndrome usually >5 mcg/dL, Katzung Ch 39). Current guidelines (Endocrine Society 2016) recommend individualized decision-making: surgery is preferred in patients with hypertension, obesity, osteoporosis, type 2 DM, or cardiovascular disease that may be attributable to excess cortisol. Surveillance is acceptable for patients without metabolic complications or high surgical risk. Biopsy is contraindicated without first excluding pheochromocytoma biochemically. MRI can be used for characterization but is not required if CT shows typical benign, lipid-rich features. Absolute surgical indications: mass more than 4 cm or any features suspicious for malignancy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After 1 mg dexamethasone, normal morning cortisol is usually less than 2 mcg/dL and in Cushing syndrome usually more than 5 mcg/dL (Katzung Ch 39); values in between suggest mild autonomous cortisol secretion. MACS management is individualized. COMLEX: Shared decision-making and patient-centered care principles align with osteopathic philosophy; adrenalectomy for MACS has shown improvement in blood pressure and metabolic parameters in multiple studies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — Pituitary-adrenal axis governs ACTH-dependent versus autonomous cortisol in adrenal tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"MRI can be used for characteri","E":"Biopsy is contraindicated with"}},{"id":"sdl21_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man presents with episodic hypertension, diaphoresis, and palpitations occurring several times weekly. Plasma free metanephrines are markedly elevated. CT shows a 4.2 cm left adrenal mass. He is started on phenoxybenzamine before surgery. Adrenalectomy is performed. Which architectural pattern on histology is the hallmark of pheochromocytoma?","choices":{"E":"Polygonal chromaffin cells with granular cytoplasm arranged in rounded cell nests called zellballen, surrounded by sustentacular cells and fibrovascular stroma, are the hallmark architectural pattern of pheochromocytoma.","A":"Spindle cells arranged in interlacing fascicles with interspersed collagen fibers and a myxoid stromal background with rare mitotic figures are the hallmark pattern of pheochromocytoma.","B":"Pleomorphic cells with prominent nucleoli arranged in solid sheets with central comedonecrosis and fibrovascular septae separating tumor lobules are the hallmark of pheochromocytoma.","C":"Large clear vacuolated cells with lipid-rich cytoplasm arranged in anastomosing cords with small inconspicuous nuclei and no fibrovascular stroma are the hallmark of pheochromocytoma.","D":"Small blue round cells with high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio arranged in Homer-Wright rosettes with a central neuropil core are the hallmark pattern of pheochromocytoma."},"explanation":"Pheochromocytoma characteristically shows the zellballen (German for \"cell balls\") architecture: nests of polygonal chromaffin cells with abundant granular eosinophilic cytoplasm (from catecholamine-containing secretory granules) surrounded by fibrovascular stroma with sustentacular cells at the periphery. Sustentacular cells are S100-positive supporting cells visible at the outer aspect of each nest. Tumor cells often show nuclear pleomorphism and may have stippled \"salt-and-pepper\" chromatin typical of neuroendocrine tumors. There are NO reliable histologic criteria for malignancy in pheochromocytoma — malignancy is defined only by metastasis to non-chromaffin sites. IHC shows positivity for chromogranin A and synaptophysin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The zellballen pattern is the classic histologic description of pheochromocytoma. Important: you cannot diagnose malignancy histologically — it requires metastases. Sustentacular cells (S100+) are reduced or absent in malignant paragangliomas. Chromogranin A and synaptophysin positivity distinguish pheo from adrenocortical tumors. COMLEX: Pre-operative alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine for 1–3 weeks before surgery, Katzung Ch 10) is mandatory; add beta-blockade only after alpha-blockade is established to prevent unopposed alpha-mediated hypertensive crisis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma: clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and surgical treatment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl21_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy is brought for evaluation of progressive abdominal distension and irritability over 6 weeks. Examination reveals a firm, irregular abdominal mass extending across the midline. CT shows a heterogeneous retroperitoneal mass arising from the left adrenal with calcifications, lymphadenopathy, and liver metastases. Urine HVA and VMA are markedly elevated. Genetic testing reveals N-MYC gene amplification. Which statement about this finding is most accurate for predicting his prognosis?","choices":{"A":"N-MYC amplification is a strong adverse prognostic marker in neuroblastoma, associated with rapid progression, treatment resistance, and poor survival regardless of stage.","B":"N-MYC amplification is associated with spontaneous regression in infants under 18 months with stage 4S neuroblastoma, and these patients do not require systemic chemotherapy.","C":"N-MYC amplification defines a favorable neuroblastoma subtype that has high sensitivity to platinum-based first-line chemotherapy regimens with greater than 80% cure rate.","D":"Elevated urinary HVA and VMA confirm the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma rather than neuroblastoma in a child of this age, and the correct test is plasma metanephrines.","E":"Liver metastases at presentation indicate a favorable 4S stage neuroblastoma in this age group because hepatic neuroblastoma is radiosensitive and responds well to observation alone."},"explanation":"N-MYC (MYCN) amplification occurs in about 20% of primary neuroblastomas (Robbins Ch 10) and is the single most important adverse prognostic marker, independently predicting rapid progression and poor prognosis regardless of age or disease stage. Spontaneous regression occurs in stage 4S (skin, liver, bone marrow without cortical bone) in infants under 18 months WITHOUT N-MYC amplification — this is a distinct favorable biology that does not apply here. This boy is over 18 months with N-MYC amplification, liver metastases, and advanced disease (stage 4), placing him in the high-risk category with poor prognosis (5-year survival in the range of 40%, and fewer than 50% long-term survivors, in high-risk disease per Robbins Ch 10). HVA and VMA elevated in urine are consistent with neuroblastoma (not pheochromocytoma, which elevates metanephrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: N-MYC amplification = high-risk neuroblastoma, poor prognosis. Stage 4S = favorable (under 18 months, no N-MYC, skin/liver/marrow only). The mass crossing the midline distinguishes neuroblastoma from Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma), which does not cross the midline and presents with hematuria. Neuroblastoma elevates HVA and VMA; Wilms presents with hematuria and a flank mass that does not cross midline. COMLEX: Neuroblastoma can cause opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome (dancing eyes-dancing feet) via paraneoplastic antibodies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma: clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and surgical treatment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl21_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with a known RET proto-oncogene pathogenic variant undergoes surveillance. She is found to have bilateral adrenal masses with markedly elevated plasma free metanephrines, serum calcium of 11.4 mg/dL, PTH 102 pg/mL, and a calcitonin level of 580 pg/mL. She has no prior surgeries. Which management step must be completed before scheduling bilateral adrenalectomy?","choices":{"B":"Alpha-adrenergic blockade with phenoxybenzamine must be established for 1 to 3 weeks before adrenalectomy to prevent intraoperative hypertensive crisis from catecholamine release.","A":"Parathyroidectomy must be performed first because hypercalcemia from primary hyperparathyroidism triggers catecholamine release from chromaffin cells and must be corrected before adrenal surgery.","C":"Beta-blockade alone with metoprolol must be initiated first because phenoxybenzamine causes reflex tachycardia that is dangerous in the perioperative period and must be avoided.","D":"Calcium channel blockade must be initiated first because calcium influx into chromaffin cells is the primary mechanism driving catecholamine secretion during adrenalectomy under general anesthesia.","E":"Thyroid medullary carcinoma resection must be completed first because calcitonin excess from MTC potentiates catecholamine biosynthesis in pheochromocytoma cells and increases operative risk."},"explanation":"This patient has MEN 2A (RET mutation, pheochromocytoma, primary hyperparathyroidism, medullary thyroid carcinoma). Before ANY adrenal surgery for pheochromocytoma, alpha-adrenergic blockade (phenoxybenzamine or selective alpha-1 blockers) MUST be established, typically for 1-3 weeks (Katzung Ch 10), to prevent a life-threatening hypertensive crisis triggered by anesthetic induction, tumor manipulation, or catecholamine surge. Beta-blockade is added ONLY AFTER alpha-blockade is established (never first — unopposed alpha stimulation with tachycardia prevented can cause severe vasoconstriction and hypertensive emergency). In MEN 2A, the standard surgical sequence is: pheochromocytoma first (after alpha-blockade), then MTC (cervical exploration), then parathyroid. The MTC does not need to be resected first.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The perioperative management of pheochromocytoma is high-yield: alpha-blockade first (phenoxybenzamine for 1-3 weeks), then selective beta-blockade if tachycardic, high-salt diet and adequate hydration to counteract alpha-blockade-induced orthostasis. In MEN 2A, pheochromocytoma is treated before thyroid and parathyroid surgery. COMLEX: MEN 2A = medullary thyroid carcinoma (most penetrant), pheochromocytoma (bilateral in MEN), primary hyperparathyroidism. MEN 2B = MTC, pheo, marfanoid habitus, mucosal neuromas, but NO parathyroid disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma: clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and surgical treatment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"In MEN 2A, the standard surgic","C":"Beta-blockade is added ONLY AF","E":"The MTC does not need to be re"}}]};
